WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.886
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:12.510 --> 00:00:13.660
MODERATOR: Good evening.

00:00:13.660 --> 00:00:14.580
My name is Erica.

00:00:14.580 --> 00:00:17.790
And as president of the MIT
Western Hemisphere Project,

00:00:17.790 --> 00:00:20.100
I would like to welcome
each and every one of you

00:00:20.100 --> 00:00:24.529
for coming to tonight's event
entitled The Uses Of Haiti.

00:00:24.529 --> 00:00:26.820
What I would like to briefly
discuss with you right now

00:00:26.820 --> 00:00:31.200
is just to mention that
this event was co-sponsored

00:00:31.200 --> 00:00:35.460
by the MIT Western Hemisphere
Project, MIT Student

00:00:35.460 --> 00:00:40.140
Pugwash, and the MIT's
Technology and Culture Forum.

00:00:40.140 --> 00:00:41.640
As you can see up
here on the board,

00:00:41.640 --> 00:00:42.806
there's three coming events.

00:00:42.806 --> 00:00:43.860
Two are tomorrow.

00:00:43.860 --> 00:00:47.370
One is an evening of Latin
American chamber music

00:00:47.370 --> 00:00:50.400
and a Pugwash student
conference entitled Technology

00:00:50.400 --> 00:00:52.800
in the New Global
Context, Rethinking Social

00:00:52.800 --> 00:00:54.060
Responsibility.

00:00:54.060 --> 00:00:56.190
I encourage people to
attend those events.

00:00:56.190 --> 00:00:58.440
And the Western
Hemisphere Project

00:00:58.440 --> 00:01:03.100
will be having an open meeting
within the next two weeks.

00:01:03.100 --> 00:01:04.709
Okay, briefly to
discuss, I'd like

00:01:04.709 --> 00:01:08.650
to mention that our schedule
for this evening is as follows.

00:01:08.650 --> 00:01:11.580
First, I'll give introductions,
introduced Nancy Dorsinville,

00:01:11.580 --> 00:01:13.440
who will introduce
our two speakers

00:01:13.440 --> 00:01:19.170
this evening, Dr. Paul Farmer
and Professor Noam Chomsky.

00:01:19.170 --> 00:01:22.140
Professor Chomsky will speak
for roughly half an hour.

00:01:22.140 --> 00:01:24.840
And Professor Farmer will
speak for roughly half an hour

00:01:24.840 --> 00:01:27.660
and will be followed
by questions.

00:01:27.660 --> 00:01:32.160
And, now, I'd like to
begin with an introduction.

00:01:32.160 --> 00:01:36.300
As a research fellow at the
Harvard Center for Population

00:01:36.300 --> 00:01:38.970
and Development Studies,
Nancy Dorsinville's work

00:01:38.970 --> 00:01:41.160
focuses on health and
social justice issues

00:01:41.160 --> 00:01:43.740
within the Haitian diaspora.

00:01:43.740 --> 00:01:46.770
As a social anthropologist with
a specialization in migration

00:01:46.770 --> 00:01:49.470
studies, she lectures on
women's health and violence

00:01:49.470 --> 00:01:51.780
at the Harvard School
of Public Health.

00:01:51.780 --> 00:01:54.120
Her research also
includes domestic violence

00:01:54.120 --> 00:01:56.820
as a public health concern
among Haitian woman.

00:01:56.820 --> 00:01:59.040
She's presented her
work at a wide range

00:01:59.040 --> 00:02:01.920
of academic and international
conferences, most recently

00:02:01.920 --> 00:02:04.590
at the UN Conference
against Racism in Durban

00:02:04.590 --> 00:02:07.650
on the intersection of
migration, xenophobia,

00:02:07.650 --> 00:02:09.630
and violence against women.

00:02:09.630 --> 00:02:11.610
She is a member of
Partners in Health,

00:02:11.610 --> 00:02:14.420
an advisor to women's
program at the UN Academy

00:02:14.420 --> 00:02:17.010
and an affiliate at the Harvard
Program in International

00:02:17.010 --> 00:02:19.680
Analysis and
Conflict Resolution.

00:02:19.680 --> 00:02:22.530
Currently, she is a faculty
advisor for the Harvard Haitian

00:02:22.530 --> 00:02:24.050
Students Alliance.

00:02:24.050 --> 00:02:25.610
And now with great
pleasure, I would

00:02:25.610 --> 00:02:27.360
like to present to
you, Nancy Dorsinville.

00:02:27.360 --> 00:02:29.785
[APPLAUSE]

00:02:37.351 --> 00:02:38.850
DORSINVILLE: Good
evening, everyone.

00:02:38.850 --> 00:02:40.070
I'm very happy to be here.

00:02:40.070 --> 00:02:43.610
And I thank you for
being here to join us

00:02:43.610 --> 00:02:47.930
in hearing and learning
and expressing concerns

00:02:47.930 --> 00:02:52.430
about the present conditions
in our beloved Haiti.

00:02:52.430 --> 00:02:54.530
The two people here
with us this evening

00:02:54.530 --> 00:02:57.170
don't need much introduction.

00:02:57.170 --> 00:02:59.150
Dr. Farmer, as
you know, has been

00:02:59.150 --> 00:03:04.220
working in Haiti for the past
20 years in infectious disease,

00:03:04.220 --> 00:03:09.110
but fundamentally addressing
issues of the poor.

00:03:09.110 --> 00:03:12.830
From a systemic standpoint,
from of spiritual standpoint,

00:03:12.830 --> 00:03:15.840
and from an economic
development standpoint,

00:03:15.840 --> 00:03:21.140
his work is very much
rooted in the actualization

00:03:21.140 --> 00:03:29.150
and the empowerment of the more
marginalized people in Haiti

00:03:29.150 --> 00:03:34.350
of which our population
has the highest percentage.

00:03:34.350 --> 00:03:37.130
So I take this
opportunity to thank him

00:03:37.130 --> 00:03:41.120
for a lifetime of
dedication to my countrymen

00:03:41.120 --> 00:03:45.680
and, particularly, the most
disadvantaged of my countrymen.

00:03:45.680 --> 00:03:50.930
I then would like to
attempt to introduce

00:03:50.930 --> 00:03:55.670
Professor Chomsky, who
has written extensively

00:03:55.670 --> 00:03:57.630
on a number of issues.

00:03:57.630 --> 00:03:59.780
But, particularly,
our focus this evening

00:03:59.780 --> 00:04:04.670
is the impact of American
or US foreign relation

00:04:04.670 --> 00:04:07.160
on the current
situation in Haiti,

00:04:07.160 --> 00:04:10.250
namely the economic embargo.

00:04:10.250 --> 00:04:15.890
I just want to contextualize
our discussion this evening

00:04:15.890 --> 00:04:17.899
with some remarks.

00:04:17.899 --> 00:04:22.970
And I would like to start by
echoing something that Dr.

00:04:22.970 --> 00:04:26.960
Farmer has said consistently
in his writing and his

00:04:26.960 --> 00:04:32.210
well-attended lectures on the
other side of the river that

00:04:32.210 --> 00:04:37.070
what's happening today in
Haiti, the economic embargo,

00:04:37.070 --> 00:04:42.500
is the result of a series of
historical antecedents that

00:04:42.500 --> 00:04:47.330
cannot be taken out of the
equation if we are to really

00:04:47.330 --> 00:04:53.000
look at what the
circumstances are.

00:04:53.000 --> 00:04:57.410
In addition to that, I think
it's important to understand,

00:04:57.410 --> 00:05:01.820
as Professor Chomsky
has also said repeatedly

00:05:01.820 --> 00:05:06.260
in his writings, that
democracy is a process.

00:05:06.260 --> 00:05:12.020
And the outcome of
democracy requires a series

00:05:12.020 --> 00:05:15.980
of fundamental
conditions, namely

00:05:15.980 --> 00:05:20.960
that the sovereignty of the
people has to be respected.

00:05:20.960 --> 00:05:25.400
The agency of people in
making certain choices

00:05:25.400 --> 00:05:28.040
about the kind of
governance that they have,

00:05:28.040 --> 00:05:31.160
how that governance
will be applied,

00:05:31.160 --> 00:05:39.050
and who they will answer to
has to be part of their agency

00:05:39.050 --> 00:05:42.890
and has to be part
of their choice.

00:05:42.890 --> 00:05:48.110
I think on the eve of our
bicentennial independence,

00:05:48.110 --> 00:05:52.010
the question for me is what
does it mean that today we

00:05:52.010 --> 00:05:54.590
are under economic embargo.

00:05:54.590 --> 00:05:58.850
What does it mean that as the--

00:05:58.850 --> 00:06:00.800
I mean, everybody calls
us the poorest country

00:06:00.800 --> 00:06:01.730
in the hemisphere.

00:06:01.730 --> 00:06:07.220
And a lot of people have
contentions about that.

00:06:07.220 --> 00:06:11.930
I would rather say we are
the most impoverished country

00:06:11.930 --> 00:06:17.000
in the hemisphere, because it
has happened systematically

00:06:17.000 --> 00:06:22.670
with very deliberate,
intentional policies that

00:06:22.670 --> 00:06:25.400
have led to the
impoverishment of Haiti.

00:06:25.400 --> 00:06:28.160
One of the things that's
very topical today,

00:06:28.160 --> 00:06:30.290
people talk about reparation.

00:06:30.290 --> 00:06:32.450
Well, not only were
we the first country

00:06:32.450 --> 00:06:37.370
to be under embargo when
we became independent--

00:06:37.370 --> 00:06:40.460
which that, too, was a process.

00:06:40.460 --> 00:06:47.180
But the embargo allowed
the US and its allies

00:06:47.180 --> 00:06:51.560
not to recognize our
sovereignty for about 60 years.

00:06:51.560 --> 00:06:55.160
And yet during that
time, selectively, there

00:06:55.160 --> 00:06:58.910
were trade agreements
that benefited

00:06:58.910 --> 00:07:02.510
the countries of the
north, while beginning

00:07:02.510 --> 00:07:06.920
what we see today as the
most impoverished nation

00:07:06.920 --> 00:07:07.940
in the hemisphere.

00:07:10.490 --> 00:07:12.980
A lot of prerequisites
have been put

00:07:12.980 --> 00:07:16.310
on why we haven't
gotten the $500 million

00:07:16.310 --> 00:07:20.600
in loans and grants
that were approved.

00:07:20.600 --> 00:07:26.870
A lot of it has been relegated
to, allegedly, problems

00:07:26.870 --> 00:07:28.290
with elections.

00:07:28.290 --> 00:07:32.210
Well, I think the other oldest
republic in the Americas

00:07:32.210 --> 00:07:34.970
also understands
what it means to have

00:07:34.970 --> 00:07:38.060
problems with elections.

00:07:38.060 --> 00:07:41.420
The other issue that's
been put forward

00:07:41.420 --> 00:07:45.230
is that our cabinet was
not pluralistic enough.

00:07:45.230 --> 00:07:48.860
And in the name of
democracy and in the name

00:07:48.860 --> 00:07:53.120
of diversity in
our cabinet, it was

00:07:53.120 --> 00:07:56.210
important to change
certain senators.

00:07:56.210 --> 00:07:58.580
Every time they came up with
a different prerequisite

00:07:58.580 --> 00:08:01.160
which we fulfilled
as a sovereign state,

00:08:01.160 --> 00:08:03.690
something else came up.

00:08:03.690 --> 00:08:08.640
So I've thought about
this a great deal.

00:08:08.640 --> 00:08:12.660
And I thought, you know, this
is becoming like a kaleidoscope

00:08:12.660 --> 00:08:14.190
of democracy.

00:08:14.190 --> 00:08:16.080
And the colors are changing.

00:08:16.080 --> 00:08:17.850
The shapes are changing.

00:08:17.850 --> 00:08:20.130
The demands are changing.

00:08:20.130 --> 00:08:22.440
And we're not getting
a fixed picture

00:08:22.440 --> 00:08:24.780
from the powers of
the north as to what

00:08:24.780 --> 00:08:28.980
it is that they want in order
to release the funds, which

00:08:28.980 --> 00:08:33.780
are hampering any
humanitarian endeavors,

00:08:33.780 --> 00:08:40.140
any humanitarian initiatives,
and any real fundamental

00:08:40.140 --> 00:08:43.230
systems and building
blocks for the democracy

00:08:43.230 --> 00:08:46.990
that we've fought for
from the very beginning.

00:08:46.990 --> 00:08:51.750
2004 is just around the corner.

00:08:51.750 --> 00:09:00.060
And our struggle for democracy
began way back 200 years ago.

00:09:00.060 --> 00:09:04.350
And we have had a fixed picture
about what democracy means.

00:09:04.350 --> 00:09:06.270
We still have a fixed picture.

00:09:06.270 --> 00:09:08.580
The people of Haiti have spoken.

00:09:08.580 --> 00:09:12.330
We have heeded the
prerequisites of the changing

00:09:12.330 --> 00:09:14.610
shapes of the changing demands.

00:09:14.610 --> 00:09:19.620
And, now, in the name
of Democracy with big D

00:09:19.620 --> 00:09:23.400
in the name of humanitarian
concerns, which

00:09:23.400 --> 00:09:26.190
seems to be the epitaph
for everything that's

00:09:26.190 --> 00:09:28.770
being done today
in the world, we

00:09:28.770 --> 00:09:31.380
demand the release
of these funds.

00:09:31.380 --> 00:09:31.920
Thank you.

00:09:31.920 --> 00:09:34.355
[APPLAUSE]

00:09:45.080 --> 00:09:49.430
So, now, Professor Chomsky
is going to talk to us

00:09:49.430 --> 00:09:53.870
and share his thoughts
about the implications

00:09:53.870 --> 00:09:57.680
of American foreign policy on
the economic embargo imposed

00:09:57.680 --> 00:10:00.188
on our country today.

00:10:00.188 --> 00:10:02.668
[APPLAUSE]

00:10:10.130 --> 00:10:14.030
CHOMSKY: Glad to learn what
I'm supposed to talk about.

00:10:14.030 --> 00:10:15.890
That's fine.

00:10:15.890 --> 00:10:16.390
Well--

00:10:16.390 --> 00:10:16.930
DORSINVILLE: Anything will do.

00:10:16.930 --> 00:10:17.770
CHOMSKY: Anything will do.

00:10:17.770 --> 00:10:18.400
I understand.

00:10:18.400 --> 00:10:19.150
[LAUGHTER]

00:10:19.150 --> 00:10:22.610
Well, let me lead in from
the last talk I gave,

00:10:22.610 --> 00:10:26.230
which was last night in New
York where I had to be taken out

00:10:26.230 --> 00:10:28.720
under police protection.

00:10:28.720 --> 00:10:30.280
You can guess what
the topic was.

00:10:30.280 --> 00:10:32.130
It was about the Middle East.

00:10:32.130 --> 00:10:34.670
Tonight, if anything
controversial comes up,

00:10:34.670 --> 00:10:37.050
I'm going to hand it
over to Paul, so he can--

00:10:37.050 --> 00:10:42.010
[LAUGHTER]

00:10:42.010 --> 00:10:44.390
Well, a word about
the Middle East--

00:10:44.390 --> 00:10:45.940
[LAUGHTER]

00:10:45.940 --> 00:10:48.650
--maybe, just so I can
have some continuity--

00:10:48.650 --> 00:10:51.570
[APPLAUSE]

00:10:51.570 --> 00:10:53.590
--a very short word.

00:10:53.590 --> 00:10:56.500
You may have seen kind of an
interesting article in The New

00:10:56.500 --> 00:10:58.120
York Times Magazine
a couple of weeks

00:10:58.120 --> 00:11:02.160
ago by Deborah Sontag
on the Palestinians.

00:11:02.160 --> 00:11:04.410
In it, she made a good point.

00:11:04.410 --> 00:11:07.030
She was talking about the
difficulties of discourse

00:11:07.030 --> 00:11:09.720
between Palestinians
and the West

00:11:09.720 --> 00:11:12.250
and why it never seems to work.

00:11:12.250 --> 00:11:16.150
And the problem that she
pointed to is quite real.

00:11:16.150 --> 00:11:21.010
She said, the Palestinians
keep harping on history,

00:11:21.010 --> 00:11:23.740
where history means anything
that happened more than about

00:11:23.740 --> 00:11:25.840
5 minutes ago.

00:11:25.840 --> 00:11:28.360
And the West doesn't
want to waste time

00:11:28.360 --> 00:11:32.620
on all this sort of old
fashioned boring nonsense,

00:11:32.620 --> 00:11:35.080
but just get on, so
that the West can lead

00:11:35.080 --> 00:11:38.530
the way to a glorious future.

00:11:38.530 --> 00:11:41.590
And that makes
discourse really hard.

00:11:41.590 --> 00:11:45.650
And, actually, that point
generalizes all over the world.

00:11:45.650 --> 00:11:49.630
Those who have their
boot on somebody's neck

00:11:49.630 --> 00:11:53.110
never want to know
how it got that way.

00:11:53.110 --> 00:11:57.940
That's boring, old
fashioned, dusty, old stuff.

00:11:57.940 --> 00:12:00.250
Let's just go on with
our boot on the neck

00:12:00.250 --> 00:12:02.320
and make it even better.

00:12:02.320 --> 00:12:05.470
But the ones who have
their neck under the boot,

00:12:05.470 --> 00:12:07.930
they somehow have a
different view of things.

00:12:07.930 --> 00:12:11.190
Because they're
backward and uncivilized

00:12:11.190 --> 00:12:14.920
and, you know, that sort
of savage, you know.

00:12:14.920 --> 00:12:17.530
Well, I was asked to
say a couple of words

00:12:17.530 --> 00:12:20.230
about the background, including
the history, meaning what

00:12:20.230 --> 00:12:22.490
happened more than
5 minutes ago,

00:12:22.490 --> 00:12:24.160
which puts me on the
side of the victim.

00:12:24.160 --> 00:12:25.380
So that's good.

00:12:25.380 --> 00:12:27.440
I'm happy to take that position.

00:12:27.440 --> 00:12:33.490
That's a position we ought to
take, not necessary to say.

00:12:33.490 --> 00:12:35.390
As far as democracy
is concerned,

00:12:35.390 --> 00:12:38.740
I think it's very
clear why Haiti doesn't

00:12:38.740 --> 00:12:42.940
meet the standards of democracy,
the same reason it didn't meet

00:12:42.940 --> 00:12:47.110
the standards 10
years ago when it had

00:12:47.110 --> 00:12:50.080
its first democratic election.

00:12:50.080 --> 00:12:54.040
The election just came
out the wrong way.

00:12:54.040 --> 00:12:57.520
The US was certain, confident,
that the election would

00:12:57.520 --> 00:13:01.900
be won by its candidate,
a World Bank official, who

00:13:01.900 --> 00:13:05.530
would lead the country,
you know, on to this brand

00:13:05.530 --> 00:13:09.100
new era of neoliberalism
that had already

00:13:09.100 --> 00:13:11.290
had a certain history in Haiti.

00:13:11.290 --> 00:13:13.690
He got 14% of the vote.

00:13:13.690 --> 00:13:17.740
And out of the woodwork,
came a populist priest

00:13:17.740 --> 00:13:21.850
who got 2/3 of the vote without
any funds or any publicity

00:13:21.850 --> 00:13:23.020
or anything else.

00:13:23.020 --> 00:13:25.540
Because things had been
going on in the country

00:13:25.540 --> 00:13:27.790
that nobody paid
any attention to.

00:13:27.790 --> 00:13:29.370
They were in the
slums or, you know,

00:13:29.370 --> 00:13:33.230
in the hills and places
where no real people live.

00:13:33.230 --> 00:13:35.170
But they had
organized, you know,

00:13:35.170 --> 00:13:39.430
a vibrant democratic society,
maybe the most democratic

00:13:39.430 --> 00:13:42.920
society in the hemisphere
or maybe in the world,

00:13:42.920 --> 00:13:46.300
but on their own, you know,
without control from above.

00:13:46.300 --> 00:13:47.950
And that doesn't fly.

00:13:47.950 --> 00:13:50.480
You know, that's not democracy.

00:13:50.480 --> 00:13:52.330
It's not for us or anyone else.

00:13:52.330 --> 00:13:55.090
And, furthermore, this
is pretty official.

00:13:55.090 --> 00:14:00.040
So instead of quoting myself,
I'll quote a real expert.

00:14:00.040 --> 00:14:03.940
Thomas Carothers is
the best known scholar

00:14:03.940 --> 00:14:08.150
on issues of the emerging
democracies in Latin America.

00:14:08.150 --> 00:14:09.800
He's written a standard work.

00:14:09.800 --> 00:14:11.830
And he writes from an
insider's perspective.

00:14:11.830 --> 00:14:14.740
He was part of the
Reagan State Department

00:14:14.740 --> 00:14:18.560
involved in what they called
democracy enhancement programs.

00:14:18.560 --> 00:14:22.020
So when he writes
on the effect on,

00:14:22.020 --> 00:14:24.450
you know, bringing
democracy to Latin America,

00:14:24.450 --> 00:14:28.335
he writes from both as a
scholar and as an insider.

00:14:28.335 --> 00:14:30.520
And he's pretty honest about it.

00:14:30.520 --> 00:14:34.150
He says that the Reagan
policies were very sincere,

00:14:34.150 --> 00:14:35.530
but they failed.

00:14:35.530 --> 00:14:38.170
And they failed
in a curious way.

00:14:38.170 --> 00:14:42.610
He said, in the areas where
US influence was least--

00:14:42.610 --> 00:14:45.640
namely, in the southern
cone of Latin America--

00:14:45.640 --> 00:14:48.850
there were real steps towards
democracy, which the Reagan

00:14:48.850 --> 00:14:52.870
Administration tried to prevent,
but wasn't able to prevent.

00:14:52.870 --> 00:14:55.750
They sort of happened anyway.

00:14:55.750 --> 00:14:58.810
In the areas closer where
the United States has

00:14:58.810 --> 00:15:01.840
much more influence and control,
in effect it has basically

00:15:01.840 --> 00:15:05.290
run it for a century
or more, the policies

00:15:05.290 --> 00:15:06.220
still were sincere.

00:15:06.220 --> 00:15:07.900
But they just didn't work.

00:15:07.900 --> 00:15:10.300
And the reason was that
the United States, he says,

00:15:10.300 --> 00:15:18.130
wanted top down democracy, in
which the traditional ruling

00:15:18.130 --> 00:15:21.400
elites who have been associated
with the United States

00:15:21.400 --> 00:15:25.000
remain in power, but
under democratic forms.

00:15:25.000 --> 00:15:26.950
And, in a sense,
that was achieved.

00:15:26.950 --> 00:15:29.420
But, you know, as
an honest person,

00:15:29.420 --> 00:15:31.430
methinks that's
not real democracy.

00:15:31.430 --> 00:15:32.350
But he's correct.

00:15:32.350 --> 00:15:35.170
That's exactly what the
United States wants.

00:15:35.170 --> 00:15:36.640
And you'd expect
any powerful state

00:15:36.640 --> 00:15:39.730
to want some kind
of formal democracy,

00:15:39.730 --> 00:15:42.850
because it sort of looks good.

00:15:42.850 --> 00:15:47.290
But top down forms in which
traditional elites, those

00:15:47.290 --> 00:15:51.500
who have concentrated wealth
and power in their hands,

00:15:51.500 --> 00:15:55.250
because of their alliances
with the boss in the West--

00:15:55.250 --> 00:15:56.900
us, in this case--

00:15:56.900 --> 00:15:58.700
they have to remain in power.

00:15:58.700 --> 00:16:00.650
Incidentally, the
same picture is

00:16:00.650 --> 00:16:02.670
held for the United
States itself

00:16:02.670 --> 00:16:07.760
or always has been back to
the Constitutional Convention.

00:16:07.760 --> 00:16:09.290
So that's the picture for us.

00:16:09.290 --> 00:16:11.331
And it's got to be the
picture for everyone else.

00:16:11.331 --> 00:16:12.890
There's even a
technical name for it

00:16:12.890 --> 00:16:15.250
in the academic literature.

00:16:15.250 --> 00:16:16.880
It's called polyarchy.

00:16:16.880 --> 00:16:21.020
So theorists of
democracy argue that we

00:16:21.020 --> 00:16:23.690
ought to have polyarchy,
not democracy.

00:16:23.690 --> 00:16:28.220
Polyarchy means small
groups of elites.

00:16:28.220 --> 00:16:30.830
What James Madison
called the wealth

00:16:30.830 --> 00:16:37.400
of the nation, the
wise section, those

00:16:37.400 --> 00:16:39.920
who have sympathy with
property and its rights,

00:16:39.920 --> 00:16:41.450
they ought to rule.

00:16:41.450 --> 00:16:43.940
The rest ought to
be factionalized.

00:16:43.940 --> 00:16:46.610
They are allowed to participate,
because it's a democracy,

00:16:46.610 --> 00:16:48.350
but periodically.

00:16:48.350 --> 00:16:52.310
They participate by every
once in a while coming out

00:16:52.310 --> 00:16:55.370
and lending their
weight to one another

00:16:55.370 --> 00:17:00.050
of the responsible
men who represent

00:17:00.050 --> 00:17:01.940
the wealth of the
nation, those who

00:17:01.940 --> 00:17:04.901
have the interests
of property at heart.

00:17:04.901 --> 00:17:06.859
That's the way this
country is supposed to run.

00:17:06.859 --> 00:17:09.020
That's the way every
country is supposed to run.

00:17:09.020 --> 00:17:11.420
And that means, if a
populist priest comes out

00:17:11.420 --> 00:17:14.210
of the woodwork
with 2/3 of the vote

00:17:14.210 --> 00:17:16.430
without any money
or any publicity

00:17:16.430 --> 00:17:19.550
just basing himself on
grassroots organizations that

00:17:19.550 --> 00:17:21.920
aren't supposed to
exist, that's not

00:17:21.920 --> 00:17:23.990
the right kind of democracy.

00:17:23.990 --> 00:17:26.480
So, of course, it
has to be stopped.

00:17:26.480 --> 00:17:31.550
And that standard--
Haiti, still, they're

00:17:31.550 --> 00:17:34.070
kind of backward and
uncivilized and haven't

00:17:34.070 --> 00:17:37.220
figured this out yet,
though the United

00:17:37.220 --> 00:17:40.060
States has been trying to teach
it to them for a long time.

00:17:40.060 --> 00:17:43.536
Well, I'm-- say a few
words about history.

00:17:43.536 --> 00:17:45.410
Those of you who've
taken a history of course

00:17:45.410 --> 00:17:49.880
in junior high school will
remember that half a millennium

00:17:49.880 --> 00:17:56.000
ago a group of savage
barbarians in the west of Europe

00:17:56.000 --> 00:17:58.280
launched an assault on
the rest of the world

00:17:58.280 --> 00:18:01.640
and have practically
devastated it ever since.

00:18:01.640 --> 00:18:04.130
One of the first
places they came to

00:18:04.130 --> 00:18:09.080
is, in fact, Haiti,
which they were

00:18:09.080 --> 00:18:12.110
overawed by what they found.

00:18:12.110 --> 00:18:14.520
They regarded it as--

00:18:14.520 --> 00:18:17.320
the Spanish, this is Columbus.

00:18:17.320 --> 00:18:20.120
Las Cases, the great
contemporary historian,

00:18:20.120 --> 00:18:23.750
described it as the most densely
populated part of the world,

00:18:23.750 --> 00:18:25.130
which could happen true.

00:18:25.130 --> 00:18:27.200
And they were
overwhelmed by the wealth

00:18:27.200 --> 00:18:33.710
and the peaceful population,
maybe 8 million people or so.

00:18:33.710 --> 00:18:35.750
And they just
couldn't believe it.

00:18:35.750 --> 00:18:38.690
Well, the current
Haitians can't remember

00:18:38.690 --> 00:18:44.810
what happened to the original,
to those who Columbus found.

00:18:44.810 --> 00:18:48.920
Because they were wiped out
within about 25 years or so.

00:18:48.920 --> 00:18:52.640
They just were no good
as slaves and so on.

00:18:52.640 --> 00:18:55.640
And they brought
in African slaves

00:18:55.640 --> 00:19:01.070
to be able extract the wealth
of the country and send it back

00:19:01.070 --> 00:19:05.300
where it belongs, namely to
the savages in Western Europe.

00:19:05.300 --> 00:19:06.290
And it did.

00:19:06.290 --> 00:19:09.180
Haiti was the richest
colony of the world.

00:19:09.180 --> 00:19:13.250
Perhaps, it was the
only comparable one also

00:19:13.250 --> 00:19:16.360
discovered so-called
pretty early.

00:19:16.360 --> 00:19:21.140
It was Bengal, which,
when the English came,

00:19:21.140 --> 00:19:24.870
they were also astonished at
its wealth and advanced industry

00:19:24.870 --> 00:19:26.310
and so on.

00:19:26.310 --> 00:19:33.950
And it's maybe an oddity that
the two symbols, very symbols

00:19:33.950 --> 00:19:37.490
of disaster and
impoverishment today,

00:19:37.490 --> 00:19:39.530
are Haiti and Bangladesh.

00:19:39.530 --> 00:19:42.980
That is the two
richest areas, maybe

00:19:42.980 --> 00:19:45.260
the two richest
areas of the world,

00:19:45.260 --> 00:19:48.340
and the longest
under Western rule.

00:19:48.340 --> 00:19:49.940
Actually, that
generalization kind of

00:19:49.940 --> 00:19:53.090
extends to the Western
hemisphere itself.

00:19:53.090 --> 00:19:57.500
In the 20th century, Haiti has
been the leading beneficiary

00:19:57.500 --> 00:20:00.290
of US intervention, all kinds--

00:20:00.290 --> 00:20:02.930
military, military
occupation, you know,

00:20:02.930 --> 00:20:07.220
aid, experimental programs,
scientific approaches

00:20:07.220 --> 00:20:08.570
to how to do things.

00:20:08.570 --> 00:20:10.970
And, oddly, it's the
most impoverished country

00:20:10.970 --> 00:20:12.290
in the hemisphere.

00:20:12.290 --> 00:20:15.290
There are two countries
that vie for second place.

00:20:15.290 --> 00:20:18.020
Every year, they shift a little.

00:20:18.020 --> 00:20:21.710
They're Guatemala and Nicaragua,
which, by accident, happen

00:20:21.710 --> 00:20:25.580
to be the second major
targets of US intervention

00:20:25.580 --> 00:20:27.470
in the hemisphere.

00:20:27.470 --> 00:20:30.740
That's another one of these
odd accidents, like, you know,

00:20:30.740 --> 00:20:35.060
the fate of Bengal and
Haiti over centuries

00:20:35.060 --> 00:20:38.060
that you would only
know about if you

00:20:38.060 --> 00:20:39.680
took a real course in history.

00:20:39.680 --> 00:20:41.630
Then it would be taught
in junior high school

00:20:41.630 --> 00:20:43.070
and the implications of it.

00:20:43.070 --> 00:20:44.230
And it goes on from there.

00:20:44.230 --> 00:20:46.055
This is not the only example.

00:20:49.880 --> 00:20:52.120
Haiti was incredibly rich.

00:20:52.120 --> 00:20:54.882
It was producing about 3/4
of the sugar in the world

00:20:54.882 --> 00:20:56.590
and all sorts of
things, but producing it

00:20:56.590 --> 00:21:00.340
for the conquerors.

00:21:00.340 --> 00:21:03.610
A large part of France's
wealth comes from Haiti

00:21:03.610 --> 00:21:06.010
just as a substantial
part of England's wealth

00:21:06.010 --> 00:21:11.320
comes from Bengal and other
areas it conquered and left

00:21:11.320 --> 00:21:14.860
completely
devastated, of course.

00:21:14.860 --> 00:21:19.690
200 years ago, the Haitians
did make a serious mistake.

00:21:19.690 --> 00:21:23.950
They carried out the
first and only revolution

00:21:23.950 --> 00:21:25.760
of the 18th century.

00:21:25.760 --> 00:21:28.780
It's the only one that
met the conditions

00:21:28.780 --> 00:21:29.860
of the Enlightenment.

00:21:29.860 --> 00:21:32.960
That is it called for
universal freedom.

00:21:32.960 --> 00:21:34.180
None of the others did that.

00:21:34.180 --> 00:21:36.520
The French and the
American Revolution,

00:21:36.520 --> 00:21:38.850
obviously, didn't qualify.

00:21:38.850 --> 00:21:41.230
And that appalled
civilized people.

00:21:41.230 --> 00:21:45.880
It appalled them so much
that there apparently

00:21:45.880 --> 00:21:50.350
was no discussion in
France, or in England,

00:21:50.350 --> 00:21:52.570
or the United States
of the very idea

00:21:52.570 --> 00:21:55.360
that black slaves could
liberate themselves.

00:21:55.360 --> 00:21:57.280
It was just too appalling.

00:21:57.280 --> 00:22:04.270
When they finally succeeded
at a horrifying cost, well,

00:22:04.270 --> 00:22:06.280
Tallyrand, the great
French statesman

00:22:06.280 --> 00:22:08.980
wrote to James Madison
that all civilized people

00:22:08.980 --> 00:22:13.270
must be horrified at the
sight of armed Negroes

00:22:13.270 --> 00:22:16.810
carrying out massive atrocities
against the French, you know,

00:22:16.810 --> 00:22:20.350
who just really belonged
there and driving

00:22:20.350 --> 00:22:21.670
Europeans out of their country.

00:22:21.670 --> 00:22:23.110
And that was, in fact, the view.

00:22:23.110 --> 00:22:25.960
I mean, Haiti was
punished, as you said.

00:22:25.960 --> 00:22:29.650
It was punished by
being forced to pay

00:22:29.650 --> 00:22:33.940
severe indemnity to France for
the crime of having liberated

00:22:33.940 --> 00:22:35.350
itself.

00:22:35.350 --> 00:22:37.930
That's not the only
case, incidentally.

00:22:37.930 --> 00:22:41.260
Again, as I'm sure you
learned in junior high school,

00:22:41.260 --> 00:22:43.960
when George Washington in the
middle of the Revolutionary War

00:22:43.960 --> 00:22:47.140
decided to wipe out the
Iroquois civilization, which

00:22:47.140 --> 00:22:51.490
was in many ways more
advanced than the colonists

00:22:51.490 --> 00:22:55.780
except in modes of
warfare, he succeeded.

00:22:55.780 --> 00:22:58.400
But they had to pay
an indemnity, too.

00:22:58.400 --> 00:23:00.370
DeWitt Clinton, the
governor of New York,

00:23:00.370 --> 00:23:04.060
made a treaty with them
after a sufficient bashing.

00:23:04.060 --> 00:23:06.470
And they demanded
and had to do it

00:23:06.470 --> 00:23:10.390
that they compensate the
colonists for the crimes they

00:23:10.390 --> 00:23:14.710
had committed by
resisting the assault

00:23:14.710 --> 00:23:17.500
that Washington's
forces led against them.

00:23:17.500 --> 00:23:20.080
This was right during
the Revolutionary War,

00:23:20.080 --> 00:23:21.130
incidentally.

00:23:21.130 --> 00:23:23.440
And it's not the only time.

00:23:23.440 --> 00:23:28.630
In the 1980s, the US was
ordered by the world court

00:23:28.630 --> 00:23:31.780
to pay substantial
reparations to Nicaragua

00:23:31.780 --> 00:23:34.660
for the crime of
international terrorism

00:23:34.660 --> 00:23:36.490
for which it was condemned.

00:23:36.490 --> 00:23:39.940
But power relations turned
it the other way around.

00:23:39.940 --> 00:23:43.090
Nicaragua pays
indemnities to us.

00:23:43.090 --> 00:23:51.450
The Vietnamese have to
pay the United States

00:23:51.450 --> 00:23:56.440
the costs of running the
client regime in South Vietnam.

00:23:56.440 --> 00:23:58.490
Literally, that's
what's happening.

00:23:58.490 --> 00:24:00.640
And that's the way
power relations work.

00:24:00.640 --> 00:24:05.150
Haiti's a particularly grotesque
example of it, but it's true.

00:24:05.150 --> 00:24:07.750
Finally, the US did
recognize Haiti,

00:24:07.750 --> 00:24:10.670
was the last major
country to do so.

00:24:10.670 --> 00:24:12.040
And it was 60 years.

00:24:12.040 --> 00:24:13.510
It was 1862.

00:24:13.510 --> 00:24:15.180
And there was a reason.

00:24:15.180 --> 00:24:17.620
It was right in the
middle of the Civil War.

00:24:17.620 --> 00:24:19.870
They were going to
liberate the slaves.

00:24:19.870 --> 00:24:22.580
And there was a problem
about what to do with them.

00:24:22.580 --> 00:24:26.650
We had to satisfy Thomas
Jefferson's dictum

00:24:26.650 --> 00:24:30.040
that the country had to be,
as he put it, free of blot

00:24:30.040 --> 00:24:32.920
or a mixture of red or black.

00:24:32.920 --> 00:24:36.640
Well, the red part was pretty
easy, just exterminate them.

00:24:36.640 --> 00:24:40.960
But the black part, they
were hoping that they

00:24:40.960 --> 00:24:42.010
could be sent somewhere.

00:24:42.010 --> 00:24:44.500
In fact, the United
States recognized Liberia

00:24:44.500 --> 00:24:46.864
in the same year, 1862.

00:24:46.864 --> 00:24:48.280
The idea is, well,
you sort of get

00:24:48.280 --> 00:24:51.940
rid of these savages
who don't belong here.

00:24:51.940 --> 00:24:55.270
And that was the context
in which Haiti and Liberia

00:24:55.270 --> 00:24:57.160
were, indeed, recognized.

00:24:57.160 --> 00:25:01.540
The latter part of the 19th
century, the European powers

00:25:01.540 --> 00:25:03.280
and the United
States were jockeying

00:25:03.280 --> 00:25:06.010
for control of the
Caribbean, which

00:25:06.010 --> 00:25:10.240
was a very central
area in world affairs.

00:25:10.240 --> 00:25:17.600
For one reason, because it was
the main source of soft drugs--

00:25:17.600 --> 00:25:23.140
tobacco, rum, sugar, which
were very badly needed in order

00:25:23.140 --> 00:25:28.360
to pacify the working
classes in England

00:25:28.360 --> 00:25:32.110
and others who were benefiting
from early industrialization

00:25:32.110 --> 00:25:34.580
by being devastated themselves.

00:25:34.580 --> 00:25:38.200
And a lot of the
wars of the period

00:25:38.200 --> 00:25:42.712
were, in fact, fought over the
Caribbean for those reasons.

00:25:42.712 --> 00:25:44.600
Actually, the same
story goes on in India,

00:25:44.600 --> 00:25:46.380
but I don't want
to get too far off.

00:25:46.380 --> 00:25:50.760
The British actually were
running the biggest narco

00:25:50.760 --> 00:25:52.620
trafficking
enterprise in history

00:25:52.620 --> 00:25:56.460
it turned out in the late 19th
century, interesting story

00:25:56.460 --> 00:25:57.180
for that reason.

00:25:57.180 --> 00:25:59.346
[LAUGHTER]

00:25:59.346 --> 00:26:02.010
The end result of
it was that the US

00:26:02.010 --> 00:26:06.180
became the dominant power by the
early part of the last century.

00:26:06.180 --> 00:26:11.460
And Haiti was still considered
a very important colony,

00:26:11.460 --> 00:26:14.460
plenty of wealth worth having.

00:26:14.460 --> 00:26:19.230
Woodrow Wilson
invaded it in 1915.

00:26:19.230 --> 00:26:24.180
It's an example of what's called
until today Wilsonian idealism.

00:26:24.180 --> 00:26:27.300
He invaded Haiti and
the Dominican Republic.

00:26:27.300 --> 00:26:29.370
And, here, the memories split.

00:26:29.370 --> 00:26:31.740
You know, the Haitians
remember it one way.

00:26:31.740 --> 00:26:34.590
The United States
remember it another way.

00:26:34.590 --> 00:26:40.690
When Clinton sent the Marines
in 1994 to restore democracy--

00:26:40.690 --> 00:26:42.270
I'll get to that--

00:26:42.270 --> 00:26:44.460
there was a lot of
discussion in the press

00:26:44.460 --> 00:26:46.660
and scholarly
literature and so on.

00:26:46.660 --> 00:26:49.440
And there were a lot of
warnings that Clinton

00:26:49.440 --> 00:26:53.190
ought to be careful
before following

00:26:53.190 --> 00:26:57.600
the course of Wilsonian
idealism, which

00:26:57.600 --> 00:27:03.150
tried to impose order in
this savage place in 1915.

00:27:03.150 --> 00:27:04.770
And, in fact, The
New York Times even

00:27:04.770 --> 00:27:08.880
compared the problems he was
facing to those of Napoleon

00:27:08.880 --> 00:27:11.580
in the early 18th century.

00:27:11.580 --> 00:27:14.640
Napoleon and Wilson,
both idealists,

00:27:14.640 --> 00:27:19.680
were trying to impose
order on savage places

00:27:19.680 --> 00:27:22.560
where the people have
homicidal tendencies

00:27:22.560 --> 00:27:25.750
and have no experience with
democracy, like Napoleon had--

00:27:25.750 --> 00:27:26.250
[LAUGHTER]

00:27:26.250 --> 00:27:28.920
--and have no savagery,
like the United States has.

00:27:28.920 --> 00:27:31.350
You read this right
in the main columnists

00:27:31.350 --> 00:27:33.990
in The New York Times
and Washington Post.

00:27:33.990 --> 00:27:35.940
And we have to be really
careful about that,

00:27:35.940 --> 00:27:40.800
because these savage gangs with
their homicidal tendencies--

00:27:40.800 --> 00:27:44.760
on the one hand, the Tonton
Macoute who kill, I don't know,

00:27:44.760 --> 00:27:46.770
tens of thousands of
people under the Duvalier

00:27:46.770 --> 00:27:47.940
dictatorship.

00:27:47.940 --> 00:27:51.640
And another one was
the military junta,

00:27:51.640 --> 00:27:53.650
which the US was
actually supporting,

00:27:53.650 --> 00:27:55.460
but was pretending to
be opposed to, which

00:27:55.460 --> 00:27:58.500
it killed I don't know how
many, maybe 5,000 people.

00:27:58.500 --> 00:28:01.620
And then the third
homicidal gang

00:28:01.620 --> 00:28:03.810
was the peasants in the
hills and the people

00:28:03.810 --> 00:28:08.760
in the slums, who had
conducted, you know,

00:28:08.760 --> 00:28:12.240
who had really made this
hideous error of having voted

00:28:12.240 --> 00:28:16.950
in as president somebody
was going to carry out

00:28:16.950 --> 00:28:18.690
reformist policies.

00:28:18.690 --> 00:28:23.820
So between these three gangs
with their homicidal tendencies

00:28:23.820 --> 00:28:27.700
seeking revenge in the
traditional Haitian fashion--

00:28:27.700 --> 00:28:31.110
you know, barbarians who
don't understanding anything

00:28:31.110 --> 00:28:33.300
about civilization and
stuff, which is exactly

00:28:33.300 --> 00:28:35.070
the way it's discussed--

00:28:35.070 --> 00:28:37.065
the US should be cautious.

00:28:43.320 --> 00:28:49.560
From the US point of view,
the Wilsonian occupation

00:28:49.560 --> 00:28:52.140
was benevolent.

00:28:52.140 --> 00:28:57.060
So in 1994, leading historians
like David Landes at Harvard

00:28:57.060 --> 00:28:59.790
explained that this
was a period that

00:28:59.790 --> 00:29:03.420
really brought many benefits
to Haiti for the first time.

00:29:03.420 --> 00:29:07.560
But as he pointed out, even
the most benevolent occupation

00:29:07.560 --> 00:29:10.450
does sometimes
arouse resentment.

00:29:10.450 --> 00:29:14.280
So the Haitians kind of
didn't all totally applaud.

00:29:14.280 --> 00:29:16.740
And others chimed in
with the same story,

00:29:16.740 --> 00:29:18.210
other distinguished figures.

00:29:20.760 --> 00:29:23.670
And the State Department
had explained at the time--

00:29:23.670 --> 00:29:26.580
and this goes into
history-- that many

00:29:26.580 --> 00:29:30.180
of the measures that
Wilson introduced

00:29:30.180 --> 00:29:32.130
were, indeed, very benevolent.

00:29:32.130 --> 00:29:37.650
So, for example, they
induced the Haitians,

00:29:37.650 --> 00:29:41.120
as the State Department put it,
by rather high handed methods.

00:29:41.120 --> 00:29:42.210
I'll come back to those.

00:29:42.210 --> 00:29:46.260
But they induced the Haitians
to accept progressive changes

00:29:46.260 --> 00:29:47.700
in the constitution.

00:29:47.700 --> 00:29:50.280
See, these backward
people had laws

00:29:50.280 --> 00:29:55.200
which prevented foreigners from
buying up the entire country.

00:29:55.200 --> 00:29:57.690
And that is very unprogressive.

00:29:57.690 --> 00:29:58.800
And it's obvious why.

00:29:58.800 --> 00:30:00.670
Anybody who's taken
an economics course

00:30:00.670 --> 00:30:03.480
would understand the State
Department reasoning.

00:30:03.480 --> 00:30:07.170
Obviously, to develop, Haiti
needs foreign investment.

00:30:07.170 --> 00:30:10.710
And you can't expect US
investors to put money

00:30:10.710 --> 00:30:12.540
in there unless they own it.

00:30:12.540 --> 00:30:16.950
So the best way to raise
Haiti out of its backwardness

00:30:16.950 --> 00:30:21.990
is to change the constitution
to allow Western,

00:30:21.990 --> 00:30:28.140
means US, corporations
to buy up Haiti's lands.

00:30:28.140 --> 00:30:31.030
And what were the
high handed methods?

00:30:31.030 --> 00:30:32.580
Well, the high
handed methods were

00:30:32.580 --> 00:30:36.930
to send the Marines in to
kick out the parliament

00:30:36.930 --> 00:30:40.350
and then to write
a constitution.

00:30:40.350 --> 00:30:42.870
The author or the
person who took credit

00:30:42.870 --> 00:30:45.320
for the constitution,
though probably falsely,

00:30:45.320 --> 00:30:48.210
was Franklin Delano
Roosevelt who, incidentally,

00:30:48.210 --> 00:30:49.980
hoped to make a
killing on it himself

00:30:49.980 --> 00:30:52.390
by buying up some Haitian lands.

00:30:52.390 --> 00:30:55.870
The new constitution was
presented to the Haitians

00:30:55.870 --> 00:30:58.700
for a vote, because we're
a democratic society.

00:30:58.700 --> 00:31:02.590
And it was voted in
by, I think, 99.9%

00:31:02.590 --> 00:31:05.290
of the 5% of the
population who voted.

00:31:05.290 --> 00:31:06.070
[LAUGHTER]

00:31:06.070 --> 00:31:10.900
So the methods were kind of
high handed, but benevolent.

00:31:10.900 --> 00:31:12.970
And it was also necessary
to impose order.

00:31:12.970 --> 00:31:16.900
There are these savage gangs
with homicidal tendencies.

00:31:16.900 --> 00:31:18.760
So the Marines had
to restore order.

00:31:18.760 --> 00:31:21.970
And according to the internal
Marine investigation,

00:31:21.970 --> 00:31:23.920
they killed 3,500 of them.

00:31:23.920 --> 00:31:28.580
According to Haitian historians,
they killed and 15,000 of them.

00:31:28.580 --> 00:31:30.400
But in any event,
they killed enough so

00:31:30.400 --> 00:31:34.300
that they put down the
resistance that even the most

00:31:34.300 --> 00:31:38.950
benevolent occupation elicits.

00:31:38.950 --> 00:31:41.920
They essentially
reinstituted slavery.

00:31:41.920 --> 00:31:45.172
They forced labor.

00:31:45.172 --> 00:31:46.630
There was some road
building mainly

00:31:46.630 --> 00:31:50.260
for the corporations that were
coming in to buy the place up.

00:31:50.260 --> 00:31:52.810
And, indeed, a lot of it
was bought up by the West,

00:31:52.810 --> 00:31:53.950
very progressive.

00:31:53.950 --> 00:31:55.720
West means mostly US.

00:31:55.720 --> 00:31:58.060
And they left the
country after 20 years

00:31:58.060 --> 00:32:02.650
in the hands of a
murderous National

00:32:02.650 --> 00:32:07.000
Guard trained armed by the
United States run by dictators.

00:32:07.000 --> 00:32:10.150
Exactly the same thing
happened in the next door

00:32:10.150 --> 00:32:12.490
Dominican Republic,
except, in Haiti, it

00:32:12.490 --> 00:32:13.780
was a little more vicious.

00:32:13.780 --> 00:32:16.540
Because there was plenty
of racism with regard

00:32:16.540 --> 00:32:21.770
to the Dominican Republic, but
they were only spics and dagos

00:32:21.770 --> 00:32:25.360
in the terminology that was
used, whereas, in Haiti,

00:32:25.360 --> 00:32:26.410
they were niggers.

00:32:26.410 --> 00:32:29.410
And, worse, they
were uppity niggers.

00:32:29.410 --> 00:32:33.400
So Wilson's secretary
of state thought

00:32:33.400 --> 00:32:35.560
it was really comical
to see, as he put it,

00:32:35.560 --> 00:32:37.270
niggers talking
French, you know?

00:32:37.270 --> 00:32:38.420
Like, this is really funny.

00:32:41.080 --> 00:32:43.570
And they really
had to be put down.

00:32:43.570 --> 00:32:47.470
So it was sort of similar in
the two countries, the two

00:32:47.470 --> 00:32:52.540
halves of the island, but
much more vicious in Haiti.

00:32:52.540 --> 00:32:56.830
After that comes a period of
US domination from a little bit

00:32:56.830 --> 00:32:57.520
of a distance.

00:32:57.520 --> 00:33:00.610
So the Marines finally
left after 20 years,

00:33:00.610 --> 00:33:02.800
but the US kept control.

00:33:02.800 --> 00:33:04.900
This is in the '30s now.

00:33:04.900 --> 00:33:09.670
At that time, Haiti, according
to US agricultural producers,

00:33:09.670 --> 00:33:12.160
Haiti was producing
cotton, for example,

00:33:12.160 --> 00:33:16.390
more efficiently than
the United States was.

00:33:16.390 --> 00:33:17.870
But that's no good.

00:33:17.870 --> 00:33:21.250
They had to be taught scientific
methods of agriculture.

00:33:21.250 --> 00:33:24.820
So during the Second World
War, when the United States

00:33:24.820 --> 00:33:28.010
needed rubber and other
commodities for the war effort,

00:33:28.010 --> 00:33:33.580
it just took over large parts
of Haitian agricultural lands

00:33:33.580 --> 00:33:36.220
and turned them over
to scientific methods

00:33:36.220 --> 00:33:41.530
of production of rubber and
other crops, total failure.

00:33:41.530 --> 00:33:43.920
A couple of tons
of rubber came out,

00:33:43.920 --> 00:33:46.540
but the country was devastated.

00:33:46.540 --> 00:33:48.820
When peasants were
finally able to go back

00:33:48.820 --> 00:33:52.910
to what used to be their lands,
there was a scraggly desert.

00:33:52.910 --> 00:33:54.400
You know, trees
had been destroyed.

00:33:54.400 --> 00:33:56.540
The irrigation head
been destroyed.

00:33:56.540 --> 00:33:58.180
And there was nothing there--

00:33:58.180 --> 00:34:02.050
only the first of a number
of scientific experiments.

00:34:02.050 --> 00:34:05.170
There were others.

00:34:05.170 --> 00:34:08.080
Meanwhile, it was under
US-backed dictatorships.

00:34:08.080 --> 00:34:11.980
In the 1970s, the
World Bank and USAID

00:34:11.980 --> 00:34:16.719
decided to lend their assistance
to this backward country.

00:34:16.719 --> 00:34:20.770
And they initiated a course
of economic development

00:34:20.770 --> 00:34:23.080
that was going to turn
Haiti into, as they put it,

00:34:23.080 --> 00:34:24.763
the Taiwan of the Caribbean.

00:34:24.763 --> 00:34:26.679
They really had a bright
future ahead of them.

00:34:26.679 --> 00:34:27.530
[LAUGHTER]

00:34:27.530 --> 00:34:32.320
The method was to destroy
wasteful activities,

00:34:32.320 --> 00:34:36.010
like agricultural production
for your own use--

00:34:36.010 --> 00:34:38.380
because, again, if you've
taken an economics course

00:34:38.380 --> 00:34:41.860
you know it's much
more efficient to buy

00:34:41.860 --> 00:34:44.889
highly subsidized
US agribusiness

00:34:44.889 --> 00:34:50.020
exports instead of wasting your
time producing your own food

00:34:50.020 --> 00:34:51.880
or producing anything else.

00:34:51.880 --> 00:34:55.210
And the idea was to turn
Haiti into an assembly plant,

00:34:55.210 --> 00:34:59.620
essentially, for US
corporations, who were very

00:34:59.620 --> 00:35:01.094
happy to use Haitian workers.

00:35:01.094 --> 00:35:03.010
They said they're very
docile and disciplined,

00:35:03.010 --> 00:35:05.350
especially when they've
got a military dictatorship

00:35:05.350 --> 00:35:07.180
and an army sitting
on their necks.

00:35:07.180 --> 00:35:09.760
And they are much cheaper
than the ones in, say,

00:35:09.760 --> 00:35:12.130
Panama and other
places where, you know,

00:35:12.130 --> 00:35:15.580
we haven't had
that much progress.

00:35:15.580 --> 00:35:19.540
And, in fact, this went
along with other experiments.

00:35:19.540 --> 00:35:21.386
Like, for example, USAID--

00:35:21.386 --> 00:35:22.510
Paul's written about this--

00:35:25.780 --> 00:35:27.340
a lot of the Haitian
peasant economy

00:35:27.340 --> 00:35:31.330
was based on a very
hardy breed of pigs.

00:35:31.330 --> 00:35:33.460
They were used
not only for food,

00:35:33.460 --> 00:35:36.010
but it's whole big
part of the culture.

00:35:36.010 --> 00:35:39.190
But the scientific
agricultural experts

00:35:39.190 --> 00:35:43.360
decided that these pigs might
be susceptible to swine flu.

00:35:43.360 --> 00:35:45.190
Although, there wasn't any.

00:35:45.190 --> 00:35:46.990
So they decided
the best idea would

00:35:46.990 --> 00:35:49.270
be to wipe them
out, which they did,

00:35:49.270 --> 00:35:53.590
and replace them with better
pings, ones from Iowa.

00:35:53.590 --> 00:35:57.280
So they brought in
fancy pigs from Iowa,

00:35:57.280 --> 00:36:00.290
which is that what they're
called, white pigs?

00:36:00.290 --> 00:36:01.200
Yeah, white pigs.

00:36:01.200 --> 00:36:05.480
[LAUGHTER]

00:36:05.480 --> 00:36:08.590
And they're great as
long as you keep them

00:36:08.590 --> 00:36:10.390
in air conditioned places--

00:36:10.390 --> 00:36:11.150
[LAUGHTER]

00:36:11.150 --> 00:36:14.800
--and, you know, feed
them the very fancy food

00:36:14.800 --> 00:36:18.760
that you buy from US producers,
you know, at a high cost.

00:36:18.760 --> 00:36:20.770
So you can actually
keep one of these pigs,

00:36:20.770 --> 00:36:23.020
apparently, if you have an
income about, I don't know,

00:36:23.020 --> 00:36:26.740
three times that of the annual
income of a peasant in Haiti.

00:36:26.740 --> 00:36:28.180
That was another contribution.

00:36:28.180 --> 00:36:31.150
Meanwhile, the economic
miracle proceeded.

00:36:31.150 --> 00:36:36.280
Haiti was on its way to becoming
the Taiwan of the Caribbean.

00:36:36.280 --> 00:36:40.720
During the 1980s, wages
dropped by about 50%.

00:36:44.860 --> 00:36:47.589
Haiti was self-sufficient
in rice, virtually

00:36:47.589 --> 00:36:49.380
in effect, a pretty
efficient rice producer

00:36:49.380 --> 00:36:51.370
and could have been better even.

00:36:51.370 --> 00:36:52.790
But that's inefficient.

00:36:52.790 --> 00:36:58.044
So they were forced to adopt
the standard neoliberal package,

00:36:58.044 --> 00:36:59.710
you know, structural
adjustment package.

00:36:59.710 --> 00:37:03.430
Cut your tariffs, privatize,
that sort of thing.

00:37:03.430 --> 00:37:06.320
The result was that
Haiti now produces,

00:37:06.320 --> 00:37:09.010
I don't know what it is,
maybe 50% of its rice,

00:37:09.010 --> 00:37:11.260
maybe less, something like that.

00:37:11.260 --> 00:37:14.380
Because Haitian producers, no
matter how efficient they are,

00:37:14.380 --> 00:37:17.620
cannot compete with
US agribusiness,

00:37:17.620 --> 00:37:21.760
which is getting 40% of
its income from subsidies,

00:37:21.760 --> 00:37:24.580
government subsidies under
the Reagan Administration.

00:37:24.580 --> 00:37:28.690
Remember, this is
free market theory,

00:37:28.690 --> 00:37:30.790
which has a traditional form.

00:37:30.790 --> 00:37:34.120
It's markets for you, but
state protection for me.

00:37:34.120 --> 00:37:37.160
You know, that's the way it's
run for hundreds of years.

00:37:37.160 --> 00:37:40.300
And that's why the world is
divided into a first world

00:37:40.300 --> 00:37:41.530
and a third world.

00:37:41.530 --> 00:37:44.920
Actually, that's literally
very close to true.

00:37:44.920 --> 00:37:48.160
So Haitian peasants,
of course, can't

00:37:48.160 --> 00:37:52.510
compete with highly
subsidized US agribusiness.

00:37:52.510 --> 00:37:53.620
And they were wiped out.

00:37:53.620 --> 00:37:58.180
Now, in the neoliberal order,
this free world that we

00:37:58.180 --> 00:38:01.120
live in, Haiti has a remedy.

00:38:01.120 --> 00:38:04.540
They can impose
anti-dumping restrictions

00:38:04.540 --> 00:38:05.777
on the United States.

00:38:05.777 --> 00:38:08.110
That is they can threaten the
United States that they'll

00:38:08.110 --> 00:38:11.410
close the Haitian
market to US exports

00:38:11.410 --> 00:38:14.620
the same way that Clinton
does, for example, when

00:38:14.620 --> 00:38:17.710
US consumers prefer
Mexican tomatoes

00:38:17.710 --> 00:38:19.980
or Japanese supercomputers.

00:38:19.980 --> 00:38:23.740
He can just close the
market to Mexico and Japan

00:38:23.740 --> 00:38:27.430
unless they threatened to do
so, unless they stop exporting.

00:38:27.430 --> 00:38:29.942
And then they stop
for odd reasons.

00:38:29.942 --> 00:38:31.900
And Haiti can do the same
to the United States.

00:38:31.900 --> 00:38:33.970
Because after all,
it is a free system.

00:38:33.970 --> 00:38:36.250
You know, it's the World
Trade Organization rules.

00:38:36.250 --> 00:38:37.970
But for some reason,
they didn't do it.

00:38:37.970 --> 00:38:39.850
I didn't figure out why.

00:38:39.850 --> 00:38:46.450
But so Haiti was, in fact,
devastated by this experiment

00:38:46.450 --> 00:38:47.560
again.

00:38:47.560 --> 00:38:50.980
Finally, the US was
supporting the dictators

00:38:50.980 --> 00:38:53.800
all the way through until the
last minute when even the army

00:38:53.800 --> 00:38:55.849
turned against
them, at which point

00:38:55.849 --> 00:38:57.640
the United States
decided that there should

00:38:57.640 --> 00:39:00.010
be a transition to democracy.

00:39:00.010 --> 00:39:02.200
Then came out-- I won't
go through the details--

00:39:02.200 --> 00:39:05.110
but the build up to the
election, which was certain.

00:39:05.110 --> 00:39:07.790
You know, they were certain
that the US candidate would win.

00:39:07.790 --> 00:39:09.390
There's no way he
could fail to win.

00:39:09.390 --> 00:39:11.200
And it came out the wrong way.

00:39:11.200 --> 00:39:15.340
And the US instantly
changed policies.

00:39:15.340 --> 00:39:19.840
USAID was diverted away
from the government

00:39:19.840 --> 00:39:22.600
towards anti-government
agencies.

00:39:22.600 --> 00:39:25.780
Every effort was made to
undermine the government.

00:39:25.780 --> 00:39:30.220
Aristide came into
office in February 1991.

00:39:30.220 --> 00:39:36.160
The refugee policy was
changed in radical violation

00:39:36.160 --> 00:39:38.230
of international law
and the Universal

00:39:38.230 --> 00:39:40.150
Declaration of Human Rights.

00:39:40.150 --> 00:39:44.350
The Carter
Administration had begun

00:39:44.350 --> 00:39:47.200
barring people who were
fleeing from the paradise

00:39:47.200 --> 00:39:51.310
that the US was creating,
essentially not unusual.

00:39:51.310 --> 00:39:53.110
Take the Mexican border.

00:39:53.110 --> 00:39:56.050
The Mexican border is
artificial, like every border.

00:39:56.050 --> 00:39:57.550
It's the result of conquest.

00:39:57.550 --> 00:40:00.130
And it's been very porous
throughout its history

00:40:00.130 --> 00:40:02.140
until 1994.

00:40:02.140 --> 00:40:07.180
After NAFTA, the border was
militarized under Clinton's

00:40:07.180 --> 00:40:08.830
Operation Gatekeeper.

00:40:08.830 --> 00:40:12.010
And the reason was because the
effects of the economic miracle

00:40:12.010 --> 00:40:16.840
in Mexico were going to drive
hundreds of thousands or maybe

00:40:16.840 --> 00:40:20.080
more impoverished
devastated people north,

00:40:20.080 --> 00:40:21.550
and that had to be stopped.

00:40:21.550 --> 00:40:24.490
Because it's our
conception of free trade.

00:40:24.490 --> 00:40:26.430
It's for capital, not people.

00:40:26.430 --> 00:40:27.850
And the same was true in Haiti.

00:40:27.850 --> 00:40:30.610
As the economic miracle
was put into effect,

00:40:30.610 --> 00:40:33.730
people started fleeing
in desperation.

00:40:33.730 --> 00:40:38.620
So the US Coast Guard and
Navy blockaded the place

00:40:38.620 --> 00:40:40.570
under Carter and
just sent them back.

00:40:43.930 --> 00:40:47.290
They were fleet fleeing
real persecution as well as

00:40:47.290 --> 00:40:48.160
impoverishment.

00:40:48.160 --> 00:40:49.660
But US sent them back.

00:40:49.660 --> 00:40:52.680
Under Reagan, that was
turned into a formal treaty

00:40:52.680 --> 00:40:54.760
with the Duvalier dictatorship.

00:40:54.760 --> 00:40:59.040
But in 1991, the US reversed it.

00:40:59.040 --> 00:41:01.800
An independent democratic
government came in.

00:41:01.800 --> 00:41:04.200
The flow of refugees
virtually halted.

00:41:04.200 --> 00:41:06.150
In fact, it turned
the other direction

00:41:06.150 --> 00:41:08.940
as people were going
back in a moment of hope.

00:41:08.940 --> 00:41:12.060
But at that point, any
refugee who did come

00:41:12.060 --> 00:41:15.690
was granted asylum, no more
sending them back to Haiti.

00:41:15.690 --> 00:41:19.150
So for seven months,
until the military coup,

00:41:19.150 --> 00:41:21.420
the policy on refugees reversed.

00:41:21.420 --> 00:41:23.310
After the military coup--

00:41:23.310 --> 00:41:25.530
went back to the old policy.

00:41:25.530 --> 00:41:28.530
Bush, again, started
returning the refugees.

00:41:28.530 --> 00:41:33.420
Clinton harshly condemned
this in the 1992 election.

00:41:33.420 --> 00:41:34.840
And as soon as he
got into office,

00:41:34.840 --> 00:41:35.840
he made it even harsher.

00:41:38.980 --> 00:41:44.010
The government, actually,
in the seven months it had,

00:41:44.010 --> 00:41:46.590
it was getting
considerable praise

00:41:46.590 --> 00:41:48.450
from international
institutions, even

00:41:48.450 --> 00:41:52.380
from secret State Department
reports that were leaked.

00:41:52.380 --> 00:41:56.160
It was for progressive
measures, for eliminating

00:41:56.160 --> 00:41:57.930
needless bureaucracy,
for getting rid

00:41:57.930 --> 00:41:59.130
of oppression, and so on.

00:41:59.130 --> 00:42:00.720
They were getting
substantial loans,

00:42:00.720 --> 00:42:02.860
in fact, which
made it even worse.

00:42:02.860 --> 00:42:05.670
Because the country might
fall out of US control.

00:42:05.670 --> 00:42:08.340
A military coup came along.

00:42:08.340 --> 00:42:12.180
The Organization of American
States declared an embargo.

00:42:12.180 --> 00:42:14.700
The Bush Administration
almost immediately

00:42:14.700 --> 00:42:21.720
undermined the embargo by
exempting US producers, which

00:42:21.720 --> 00:42:26.565
happened to include plants
owned by the Haitian elite,

00:42:26.565 --> 00:42:28.860
the rich Mevs family and others.

00:42:28.860 --> 00:42:30.960
They were excluded
from the embargo

00:42:30.960 --> 00:42:32.850
and imports were allowed in.

00:42:32.850 --> 00:42:36.420
The explanation for this
was, again, our benevolence.

00:42:36.420 --> 00:42:39.360
The New York Times
explained that this

00:42:39.360 --> 00:42:42.270
was what they called
fine-tuning the embargo

00:42:42.270 --> 00:42:47.580
to help the people of Haiti
by exempting US exports.

00:42:47.580 --> 00:42:50.850
The people who they were helping
were bitterly protesting.

00:42:50.850 --> 00:42:54.240
And the military junta
and its wealthy supporters

00:42:54.240 --> 00:42:55.240
were overjoyed.

00:42:55.240 --> 00:42:57.120
But it was all done
for the people of Haiti

00:42:57.120 --> 00:42:59.310
just as we've always done.

00:42:59.310 --> 00:43:03.210
It got worse under Clinton,
considerably worse.

00:43:03.210 --> 00:43:05.790
Furthermore, both the Bush
and the Clinton Administration

00:43:05.790 --> 00:43:08.340
authorized the
Texaco Oil Company

00:43:08.340 --> 00:43:11.250
to violate
presidential directives

00:43:11.250 --> 00:43:13.140
and provide oil to the junta.

00:43:13.140 --> 00:43:16.100
Anybody who was there could
see the oil coming in,

00:43:16.100 --> 00:43:18.960
but the US government and
CIA testified to Congress

00:43:18.960 --> 00:43:20.430
that no oil was coming in.

00:43:20.430 --> 00:43:22.110
You know, those oil
farms that you saw

00:43:22.110 --> 00:43:24.460
didn't exist in the picture.

00:43:24.460 --> 00:43:28.020
It turned out the day
before the invasion

00:43:28.020 --> 00:43:32.610
to restore democracy it
was revealed that both Bush

00:43:32.610 --> 00:43:35.790
and Clinton had authorized
that oil as the centerpiece

00:43:35.790 --> 00:43:37.290
of the embargo.

00:43:37.290 --> 00:43:42.900
Also, the US was arming and
training Haitian officers.

00:43:42.900 --> 00:43:46.980
The head of the paramilitary
organization, FRAPH,

00:43:46.980 --> 00:43:49.110
which is responsible
for thousands of deaths,

00:43:49.110 --> 00:43:50.610
was on CIA payroll.

00:43:50.610 --> 00:43:52.110
It's kind of conceded by now.

00:43:55.240 --> 00:44:00.330
So, officially, the US
was opposing the junta.

00:44:00.330 --> 00:44:02.670
In fact, it was supporting it.

00:44:02.670 --> 00:44:07.050
Then it restored
democracy after they

00:44:07.050 --> 00:44:09.060
thought the popular
organizations had taken

00:44:09.060 --> 00:44:11.910
a sufficient beating and
thousands had been killed

00:44:11.910 --> 00:44:14.020
and they had been
sufficiently terrorized.

00:44:14.020 --> 00:44:15.640
And the terror was extreme.

00:44:15.640 --> 00:44:17.490
I don't want to talk
about it, because you

00:44:17.490 --> 00:44:18.650
know much more than I do.

00:44:18.650 --> 00:44:21.860
But I was there for a few days
at the peak of the terror.

00:44:21.860 --> 00:44:23.700
And, you know, I've
been in places that

00:44:23.700 --> 00:44:26.490
are under severe repression.

00:44:26.490 --> 00:44:29.110
I just came back from
Turkey a couple days ago

00:44:29.110 --> 00:44:31.980
where, in the
Southeast where I was,

00:44:31.980 --> 00:44:36.000
there's 15 million people
living in a dungeon in an area

00:44:36.000 --> 00:44:38.190
where millions of people
have been driven out

00:44:38.190 --> 00:44:43.530
of the surrounding region
which has just been devastated

00:44:43.530 --> 00:44:46.470
in some of the worst ethnic
cleansing and atrocities

00:44:46.470 --> 00:44:50.070
of the 1990s for which you
and I paid, incidentally.

00:44:50.070 --> 00:44:55.000
This was done with US
support and military aid.

00:44:55.000 --> 00:44:57.450
And that's why you
don't hear about it.

00:44:57.450 --> 00:44:59.010
Yeah.

00:44:59.010 --> 00:45:03.900
But, you know, the people that
are under severe repression--

00:45:03.900 --> 00:45:05.650
police everywhere,
army everywhere.

00:45:05.650 --> 00:45:08.160
But there or other places,
I've never seen anything

00:45:08.160 --> 00:45:12.150
like what I saw in the
slums of Haiti back in 1993

00:45:12.150 --> 00:45:15.040
when people were
literally afraid to talk.

00:45:15.040 --> 00:45:16.740
The only thing they
would say is things

00:45:16.740 --> 00:45:18.430
like there are eyes everywhere.

00:45:18.430 --> 00:45:21.660
You know, I can't talk and
all that sort of thing.

00:45:21.660 --> 00:45:24.790
But after this
terror had succeeded,

00:45:24.790 --> 00:45:27.180
they thought, in
achieving its ends,

00:45:27.180 --> 00:45:31.050
Aristide was permitted to
return, but on a condition,

00:45:31.050 --> 00:45:32.610
namely the condition
that he accept

00:45:32.610 --> 00:45:38.940
the program of the defeated US
candidate, which is precisely

00:45:38.940 --> 00:45:40.740
the wording that was used.

00:45:40.740 --> 00:45:47.280
They said, the
renovated government

00:45:47.280 --> 00:45:55.290
has to focus its policies on
the needs of civil society.

00:45:55.290 --> 00:45:58.520
Primarily, that means
the private sector

00:45:58.520 --> 00:46:01.230
both national and international.

00:46:01.230 --> 00:46:04.850
So US investors are
Haitian civil society,

00:46:04.850 --> 00:46:07.880
but the people in the
slums and the hills aren't.

00:46:07.880 --> 00:46:12.410
And a very harsh structural
adjustment program was imposed.

00:46:12.410 --> 00:46:14.660
I won't go through the
details, because it's too late.

00:46:14.660 --> 00:46:18.020
But it succeeded in
further devastating

00:46:18.020 --> 00:46:20.180
what was left of the economy.

00:46:20.180 --> 00:46:23.450
And the Haitians still resisted.

00:46:23.450 --> 00:46:26.739
That's why they haven't
understood what democracy is.

00:46:26.739 --> 00:46:28.780
And they'll have to come
to learn this, you know.

00:46:28.780 --> 00:46:31.130
200 years we've been
trying to teach it to them.

00:46:31.130 --> 00:46:32.780
They still haven't
gotten the idea.

00:46:32.780 --> 00:46:35.178
So you could understand
why there's an embargo.

00:46:35.178 --> 00:46:36.174
DORSINVILLE: Thank you.

00:46:36.174 --> 00:46:38.664
[APPLAUSE]

00:46:57.130 --> 00:46:59.500
Thank you, Professor
Chomsky, for laying

00:46:59.500 --> 00:47:05.710
the groundwork for Dr.
Farmer's current analysis

00:47:05.710 --> 00:47:11.010
of the Haitian situation,
economic, political

00:47:11.010 --> 00:47:12.850
in the backyard of democracy.

00:47:12.850 --> 00:47:13.410
Thank you.

00:47:18.430 --> 00:47:19.180
FARMER: Thank you.

00:47:19.180 --> 00:47:22.960
It's a real privilege to
be here with all of you

00:47:22.960 --> 00:47:24.410
and the two of you.

00:47:24.410 --> 00:47:26.890
I want to thank Dinesh
and the many people who

00:47:26.890 --> 00:47:29.230
organized this forum.

00:47:29.230 --> 00:47:33.570
And you used the term the
uses of Haiti for tonight.

00:47:33.570 --> 00:47:34.570
And I was very grateful.

00:47:34.570 --> 00:47:38.515
But I would add that when
Noam wrote the introduction

00:47:38.515 --> 00:47:42.220
to The Uses of Haiti,
he wrote, "this book is

00:47:42.220 --> 00:47:43.480
slated for oblivion."

00:47:43.480 --> 00:47:45.610
And I want to thank
you for that, Noam.

00:47:45.610 --> 00:47:48.730
That was very helpful
as a marketing ploy.

00:47:48.730 --> 00:47:51.430
[LAUGHTER]

00:47:51.430 --> 00:47:55.000
And, of course, as
ever, he is correct.

00:47:55.000 --> 00:47:57.910
I just came from the
hospital, and I was rounding.

00:47:57.910 --> 00:47:59.700
And I was seeing a patient.

00:47:59.700 --> 00:48:00.200
I was late.

00:48:00.200 --> 00:48:01.199
And I said, I got to go.

00:48:01.199 --> 00:48:03.210
I'm giving a talk
with Noam Chomsky.

00:48:03.210 --> 00:48:05.710
One of the people on my team,
by the way, said, why can't he

00:48:05.710 --> 00:48:08.860
come round with us, you know?

00:48:08.860 --> 00:48:13.480
But the other person said, why
are you guys talking together,

00:48:13.480 --> 00:48:17.420
I mean, you know, if you
guys agree on everything?

00:48:17.420 --> 00:48:21.160
And I said, well, he's about
the only American intellectual

00:48:21.160 --> 00:48:24.460
I know who's actually the
telling the truth about what's

00:48:24.460 --> 00:48:25.940
going on in Haiti.

00:48:25.940 --> 00:48:28.630
And I wish I could
make a long list.

00:48:28.630 --> 00:48:31.510
And maybe I'm
missing some things.

00:48:31.510 --> 00:48:32.920
I suspect that I am.

00:48:32.920 --> 00:48:36.820
But, you know, I think that
in the 20 years that I've

00:48:36.820 --> 00:48:40.660
been working there-- can we make
that obedient to my every whim

00:48:40.660 --> 00:48:41.560
here, that thing?

00:48:41.560 --> 00:48:42.490
[LAUGHTER]

00:48:42.490 --> 00:48:45.040
In the 20 years that
I've been working there,

00:48:45.040 --> 00:48:49.300
I think that a lack
of forthright analysis

00:48:49.300 --> 00:48:51.310
has never been so wanting.

00:48:51.310 --> 00:48:54.970
And I'm a physician and
mostly do medical work.

00:48:54.970 --> 00:49:02.080
But I feel compelled to make up
something as he fixes the slide

00:49:02.080 --> 00:49:03.000
machine, anyway.

00:49:03.000 --> 00:49:04.840
[LAUGHTER]

00:49:04.840 --> 00:49:06.310
Could you tell?

00:49:06.310 --> 00:49:07.930
This is going to
sound great on radio

00:49:07.930 --> 00:49:10.126
as slide presentations
always do.

00:49:10.126 --> 00:49:12.410
[LAUGHTER]

00:49:13.710 --> 00:49:17.390
You probably, you know,
made this not work.

00:49:17.390 --> 00:49:19.784
On, off maybe?

00:49:19.784 --> 00:49:21.696
[INTERPOSING VOICES]

00:49:23.340 --> 00:49:23.840
Yeah.

00:49:23.840 --> 00:49:25.580
It's not real remote.

00:49:25.580 --> 00:49:27.181
There we go.

00:49:27.181 --> 00:49:27.680
Is it on?

00:49:27.680 --> 00:49:28.520
AUDIENCE: Yeah.

00:49:28.520 --> 00:49:29.865
AUDIENCE: It's on now.

00:49:29.865 --> 00:49:33.870
FARMER: Nancy, don't
take away from my time.

00:49:33.870 --> 00:49:35.488
That was their fault.

00:49:35.488 --> 00:49:37.778
[LAUGHTER]

00:49:40.970 --> 00:49:42.780
No matter how you
look at Haiti--

00:49:42.780 --> 00:49:45.260
and I look at it as
a physician does--

00:49:45.260 --> 00:49:46.730
the situation is grave.

00:49:46.730 --> 00:49:49.220
And I'll spend a fair
amount of time talking

00:49:49.220 --> 00:49:52.580
about what's going on now.

00:49:52.580 --> 00:49:54.380
And I will not spend
a fair amount of time

00:49:54.380 --> 00:49:56.340
going over the statistics.

00:49:56.340 --> 00:49:59.300
I'll be glad to share them with
any of you from the sources

00:49:59.300 --> 00:50:01.010
that we think are
fairly reliable.

00:50:01.010 --> 00:50:03.920
But life expectancy
has dropped in Haiti

00:50:03.920 --> 00:50:06.110
over the past few years.

00:50:06.110 --> 00:50:08.780
People don't have
access to clean water.

00:50:08.780 --> 00:50:10.700
Births are not attended.

00:50:10.700 --> 00:50:13.400
It's very dangerous to
have a child in Haiti.

00:50:13.400 --> 00:50:17.270
In one study, for every 100,000
children who were born alive,

00:50:17.270 --> 00:50:21.500
1,400 women died in
childbirth, the highest

00:50:21.500 --> 00:50:24.870
by far in the hemisphere, one
of the highest in the world.

00:50:24.870 --> 00:50:28.520
And so as someone who works with
many others who are providing

00:50:28.520 --> 00:50:31.220
health care services to
people living in rural Haiti,

00:50:31.220 --> 00:50:35.510
in one of the poorer parts of
Haiti, which is saying a lot,

00:50:35.510 --> 00:50:37.730
we need to be
concerned and engaged

00:50:37.730 --> 00:50:41.520
in understanding what
is driving forward

00:50:41.520 --> 00:50:43.817
this really wall of pathology.

00:50:43.817 --> 00:50:45.650
And I'm, of course,
thinking about pathology

00:50:45.650 --> 00:50:47.750
in terms of individual
patient's illness.

00:50:47.750 --> 00:50:52.040
But the pathology, as Noam
has said, is social in origin

00:50:52.040 --> 00:50:55.850
and is transnational in origin
and is historically rooted

00:50:55.850 --> 00:50:58.790
in a series of processes
that, unfortunately,

00:50:58.790 --> 00:51:03.330
for many of our commentators did
happen more than 5 minutes ago.

00:51:03.330 --> 00:51:05.900
And so understanding
what's happening now

00:51:05.900 --> 00:51:09.230
means that we have to understand
what has happened in the past.

00:51:09.230 --> 00:51:11.810
The decline of the Haitian
economy in recent years

00:51:11.810 --> 00:51:12.800
has been severe.

00:51:12.800 --> 00:51:16.250
It's been, you know,
one of the most striking

00:51:16.250 --> 00:51:18.120
trends in Latin America.

00:51:18.120 --> 00:51:21.200
This is from the United
Nations Development Program,

00:51:21.200 --> 00:51:24.140
a what I've called here
economic devolution,

00:51:24.140 --> 00:51:29.540
shows the negative growth
rates of the economy.

00:51:29.540 --> 00:51:31.790
And there are many ways
of looking at misery.

00:51:31.790 --> 00:51:35.240
I do have to wonder who
puts together things

00:51:35.240 --> 00:51:37.130
like the human suffering index.

00:51:37.130 --> 00:51:39.710
It's not really what I would
like to do as a pastime.

00:51:39.710 --> 00:51:42.770
But when something called
the human suffering index was

00:51:42.770 --> 00:51:45.770
put together and first
published not too long ago,

00:51:45.770 --> 00:51:49.970
Haiti was the only
country in the hemisphere

00:51:49.970 --> 00:51:52.340
that figured in the top five.

00:51:52.340 --> 00:51:53.390
It was number three.

00:51:53.390 --> 00:51:56.150
And the only two other
countries ahead of it

00:51:56.150 --> 00:51:58.280
were both in the
middle of a civil war.

00:51:58.280 --> 00:52:02.180
And in reflecting on this
datum, of Haiti ranking

00:52:02.180 --> 00:52:04.560
number three in terms
of human suffering,

00:52:04.560 --> 00:52:07.460
you know, you came to
think about the conditions

00:52:07.460 --> 00:52:08.840
there as a sort of war.

00:52:08.840 --> 00:52:12.350
And it's a war of the rich
against the poor, basically.

00:52:12.350 --> 00:52:13.965
And the rich, of
course, are sitting

00:52:13.965 --> 00:52:16.340
in many different places, not
so much in Haiti right now.

00:52:16.340 --> 00:52:19.880
But they're there, too,
and waging a sort of war.

00:52:19.880 --> 00:52:22.130
And a patient who
I saw in the clinic

00:52:22.130 --> 00:52:26.000
said, it's like a war here
every day the fight for food,

00:52:26.000 --> 00:52:27.800
and wood, and water.

00:52:27.800 --> 00:52:30.744
And that's what the
situation is reduced

00:52:30.744 --> 00:52:33.410
to in Haiti, every day the fight
for food, and wood, and water--

00:52:33.410 --> 00:52:34.910
wood, of course,
to cook the food.

00:52:34.910 --> 00:52:37.720
So Haiti has become-- and
this is from The Economist,

00:52:37.720 --> 00:52:40.500
"the nightmare next door."

00:52:40.500 --> 00:52:43.910
And it's very important
to find out why.

00:52:43.910 --> 00:52:46.940
And you should look
back at the characters

00:52:46.940 --> 00:52:49.910
of the American occupation
during that time,

00:52:49.910 --> 00:52:52.010
the characters not of
the American occupation,

00:52:52.010 --> 00:52:55.400
but really in the American press
at the time of the occupation

00:52:55.400 --> 00:52:59.990
of Haiti, which were
very similar to the one

00:52:59.990 --> 00:53:00.980
I just showed you.

00:53:00.980 --> 00:53:04.500
Now, back to this question
of more than 5 minutes ago,

00:53:04.500 --> 00:53:08.840
this was a comment made by John
le Carré the English novelist,

00:53:08.840 --> 00:53:11.840
who wrote in The Nation
about "A War We Cannot Win."

00:53:11.840 --> 00:53:13.970
And Noam already said it.

00:53:13.970 --> 00:53:16.160
"Suggesting that there
is a historical context

00:53:16.160 --> 00:53:18.680
for the recent atrocities
is by implication

00:53:18.680 --> 00:53:20.640
to make excuses for them.

00:53:20.640 --> 00:53:23.690
Anyone who is with
us doesn't do that--"

00:53:23.690 --> 00:53:25.070
doesn't do historical analysis.

00:53:25.070 --> 00:53:27.290
"Anyone who does is against us."

00:53:27.290 --> 00:53:29.240
And he's referring
very specifically to,

00:53:29.240 --> 00:53:31.670
he was referring this
essay to, discussions

00:53:31.670 --> 00:53:37.250
about what happened
here on September 11th.

00:53:37.250 --> 00:53:39.410
Noam used another
expression, that those

00:53:39.410 --> 00:53:40.880
with their boot
on the neck don't

00:53:40.880 --> 00:53:44.870
want people who have their
neck constrained in that manner

00:53:44.870 --> 00:53:48.230
to be asking questions about
history or making comments.

00:53:48.230 --> 00:53:51.752
And I'm going to do just that
in talking about echoing many

00:53:51.752 --> 00:53:53.210
of the things
they've already said,

00:53:53.210 --> 00:53:56.960
but I want to add
a couple to that.

00:53:56.960 --> 00:53:58.460
And I can speed
through this part,

00:53:58.460 --> 00:54:00.080
because Noam went
through it already.

00:54:00.080 --> 00:54:03.740
The first European settlement
in the New World was in Haiti.

00:54:03.740 --> 00:54:06.515
And that's where, in fact,
one of those three ships--

00:54:06.515 --> 00:54:07.681
I can't remember which one.

00:54:07.681 --> 00:54:08.180
I'm sorry.

00:54:08.180 --> 00:54:10.970
Noam will, of course, know--

00:54:10.970 --> 00:54:14.780
foundered off the
coast of Haiti in 1492.

00:54:14.780 --> 00:54:16.760
And part of the
wreckage was used

00:54:16.760 --> 00:54:19.070
to make one of the first
European settlements

00:54:19.070 --> 00:54:20.840
in the new world.

00:54:20.840 --> 00:54:24.380
And the fact that as
many as 8 million people

00:54:24.380 --> 00:54:26.930
could have lived on
that island then in 1492

00:54:26.930 --> 00:54:29.960
is just an astounding
and horrific thought.

00:54:29.960 --> 00:54:32.480
And that figure
of 8 million comes

00:54:32.480 --> 00:54:35.000
from demographers at the
University of California

00:54:35.000 --> 00:54:38.000
who are using mathematical
models to try and figure out

00:54:38.000 --> 00:54:40.460
how densely populated
the country was

00:54:40.460 --> 00:54:45.640
in 1492, an astounding and
terrible series of events.

00:54:45.640 --> 00:54:48.290
But has already been mentioned,
if the Indians wouldn't

00:54:48.290 --> 00:54:52.260
do as slaves, then we
needed more hardy slaves.

00:54:52.260 --> 00:54:55.850
And this is actually the
subject of great interest

00:54:55.850 --> 00:54:57.920
in Haitian scholarship
and history,

00:54:57.920 --> 00:55:03.260
was the change from using
indigenous slaves to kidnapping

00:55:03.260 --> 00:55:04.940
people in Western Africa.

00:55:04.940 --> 00:55:08.300
Just as a couple of asides to
give an idea about the scale

00:55:08.300 --> 00:55:11.330
of what it took to make Haiti
the most profitable colony

00:55:11.330 --> 00:55:14.630
in the world, in European
history probably,

00:55:14.630 --> 00:55:20.090
it took up to 29,000
slaves a year as imports.

00:55:20.090 --> 00:55:23.750
So Haiti was, in fact,
the number one destination

00:55:23.750 --> 00:55:25.880
in the New World for slaves.

00:55:25.880 --> 00:55:30.159
It was ahead of
Cuba and far ahead

00:55:30.159 --> 00:55:32.450
of the other islands around
there ahead of the American

00:55:32.450 --> 00:55:33.140
South.

00:55:33.140 --> 00:55:36.470
And, in fact, that fact,
29,000, was at the peak.

00:55:36.470 --> 00:55:39.950
And that demographic fact
is one of the reasons

00:55:39.950 --> 00:55:42.080
that led to the
Haitian revolution

00:55:42.080 --> 00:55:44.210
is that there were just
so many slaves compared

00:55:44.210 --> 00:55:47.900
to slave masters that the
numbers were eventually

00:55:47.900 --> 00:55:51.800
on the side of the enslaved
who staged a series of revolts

00:55:51.800 --> 00:55:52.889
throughout the New World.

00:55:52.889 --> 00:55:54.680
But only one of them
was really successful,

00:55:54.680 --> 00:55:57.950
the Haitian Rebellion,
in terms of leading to,

00:55:57.950 --> 00:56:00.350
you know, victory to the slaves.

00:56:00.350 --> 00:56:04.100
And from 1697 on,
it was the French

00:56:04.100 --> 00:56:06.020
who were the slave masters.

00:56:06.020 --> 00:56:09.960
And they replaced, over
the ensuing century,

00:56:09.960 --> 00:56:14.390
the Spanish and made Haiti
into a really earnest producer

00:56:14.390 --> 00:56:16.880
of tropical produce, coffee.

00:56:16.880 --> 00:56:19.610
By the way, please don't
be knocking soft drugs--

00:56:19.610 --> 00:56:24.470
coffee, sugar, cotton,
indigo, other things

00:56:24.470 --> 00:56:26.390
that couldn't be
grown in Europe.

00:56:26.390 --> 00:56:29.600
And Haiti was, as has been
mentioned, providing about 2/3

00:56:29.600 --> 00:56:31.730
of all European tropical
produce by the time

00:56:31.730 --> 00:56:33.560
of the French
Revolution-- excuse me,

00:56:33.560 --> 00:56:38.630
the French "Revolution--"
got to get that right.

00:56:38.630 --> 00:56:43.129
And I think the Haitians would
be in agreement, the Haitians

00:56:43.129 --> 00:56:43.670
here tonight.

00:56:43.670 --> 00:56:45.680
I know the Haitians in
Haiti are in agreement

00:56:45.680 --> 00:56:49.610
that none of these revolutions
in Europe were really serious.

00:56:49.610 --> 00:56:51.710
As the Haitians would
say, they weren't serious.

00:56:51.710 --> 00:56:53.840
Because any serious
revolution would,

00:56:53.840 --> 00:56:56.000
of course, liberate the slaves.

00:56:56.000 --> 00:57:01.430
When the slave owning
mulattoes was went to France

00:57:01.430 --> 00:57:05.210
to speak in front of the
national parliament in France,

00:57:05.210 --> 00:57:07.730
the so-called
Revolutionary Parliament,

00:57:07.730 --> 00:57:13.580
they went to argue for the right
of mulattoes to own slaves.

00:57:13.580 --> 00:57:19.760
So, again, I have
to ask how resonant

00:57:19.760 --> 00:57:22.370
is that performance
of the first Haitian

00:57:22.370 --> 00:57:27.006
elite with their performance
in the ensuing 200 years.

00:57:27.006 --> 00:57:28.130
I'll just say it right now.

00:57:28.130 --> 00:57:30.470
I work in rural
Haiti with people

00:57:30.470 --> 00:57:34.370
who call themselves peasants
and who have very much slanted,

00:57:34.370 --> 00:57:37.580
if you will, towards truth in my
view, the way that I see this.

00:57:37.580 --> 00:57:41.810
But that was one of the first
gambits of the emerging Haitian

00:57:41.810 --> 00:57:44.750
elite who owned slaves was
to have the French recognize

00:57:44.750 --> 00:57:46.200
their right to own slaves.

00:57:46.200 --> 00:57:50.170
So the real Haitians,
the slaves who revolted,

00:57:50.170 --> 00:57:54.770
were also leading a revolt
against the mulatto elite

00:57:54.770 --> 00:57:56.940
who wanted to continue
owning slaves.

00:57:56.940 --> 00:57:58.980
And, of course, not
all the mulattoes--

00:57:58.980 --> 00:58:04.235
and this term by the way,
is from colonial times.

00:58:04.235 --> 00:58:06.290
And, of course, it's
a social construct.

00:58:06.290 --> 00:58:09.470
I'm just using the terms
that are used inside Haiti.

00:58:09.470 --> 00:58:13.640
But there was about 28,000
French living in Haiti,

00:58:13.640 --> 00:58:18.380
about maybe 40,000
so-called mulattoes

00:58:18.380 --> 00:58:23.300
mixed of French and African
descent, and about half

00:58:23.300 --> 00:58:25.280
a million adult slaves.

00:58:25.280 --> 00:58:27.080
And so there were also children.

00:58:27.080 --> 00:58:30.290
And the adults slaves
were men and women

00:58:30.290 --> 00:58:31.880
working and counted as slaves.

00:58:31.880 --> 00:58:36.680
And, in fact, the French kept
rather meticulous records

00:58:36.680 --> 00:58:37.460
of the colony.

00:58:37.460 --> 00:58:39.500
And so we have a
lot of, you know,

00:58:39.500 --> 00:58:43.230
information about what life
was like during the slavery

00:58:43.230 --> 00:58:44.180
and during the colony.

00:58:44.180 --> 00:58:46.910
And I'll just go
through some of that.

00:58:46.910 --> 00:58:51.950
I mentioned already
that the size and scope

00:58:51.950 --> 00:58:55.370
of this little factory,
which is the size

00:58:55.370 --> 00:58:59.000
of the state of Maryland,
was just churning out wealth.

00:58:59.000 --> 00:59:02.340
Noam mentioned that many French
cities owed their wealth.

00:59:02.340 --> 00:59:04.250
The cities of Bordeaux,
for example-- which,

00:59:04.250 --> 00:59:06.200
if you've seen, is
a beautiful city--

00:59:06.200 --> 00:59:08.270
was virtually built
out of this money.

00:59:08.270 --> 00:59:10.820
And all of the big
coastal cities of France

00:59:10.820 --> 00:59:12.150
came from this trade.

00:59:12.150 --> 00:59:14.439
They were built up
during the slave trade.

00:59:14.439 --> 00:59:15.980
And, in fact, the
same buildings that

00:59:15.980 --> 00:59:18.580
are there now that everyone
admires as so beautiful

00:59:18.580 --> 00:59:21.890
were really the fruits
of this Triangular Trade.

00:59:21.890 --> 00:59:25.430
The impact, of course, in Haiti
on the lives of these slaves

00:59:25.430 --> 00:59:29.360
was terrible-- one
in three slaves

00:59:29.360 --> 00:59:35.060
dying within the first three
years of life in Haiti,

00:59:35.060 --> 00:59:36.280
in the colony.

00:59:36.280 --> 00:59:38.500
Haiti, by the way, was
the name of the colony

00:59:38.500 --> 00:59:40.690
before the Europeans came.

00:59:40.690 --> 00:59:41.500
It's a local word.

00:59:41.500 --> 00:59:43.291
It means high country,
mountainous country.

00:59:43.291 --> 00:59:45.100
It was the indigenous term.

00:59:45.100 --> 00:59:47.950
And it was taken back by
the leaders of the Haitian

00:59:47.950 --> 00:59:50.260
revolution after the
Europeans were defeated.

00:59:50.260 --> 00:59:53.110
Now, this is from
a Haitian who was

00:59:53.110 --> 00:59:56.800
a slave during before
the revolution, then

00:59:56.800 --> 00:59:59.461
was one of the few
people writing, you know.

00:59:59.461 --> 01:00:01.210
Of course, in the
United States, we really

01:00:01.210 --> 01:00:03.460
don't have a lot of
testimonies from slaves,

01:00:03.460 --> 01:00:04.900
narratives from slaves.

01:00:04.900 --> 01:00:08.355
And it's a very interesting
area of historical scholarship.

01:00:08.355 --> 01:00:09.730
But in Haiti,
there actually are.

01:00:09.730 --> 01:00:12.670
Because after the
revolution, many people

01:00:12.670 --> 01:00:14.810
wrote about their experience.

01:00:14.810 --> 01:00:16.814
And I won't read
this to you here.

01:00:16.814 --> 01:00:17.480
You can read it.

01:00:17.480 --> 01:00:20.080
But I know that it
says, "have they not--"

01:00:20.080 --> 01:00:23.080
and then lists the
crimes against the slaves

01:00:23.080 --> 01:00:24.670
that the French perpetrated.

01:00:24.670 --> 01:00:29.050
Now, the revolution
began in 1791--

01:00:29.050 --> 01:00:32.080
as Noam said, the
only real revolution

01:00:32.080 --> 01:00:33.580
in the 18th century--

01:00:33.580 --> 01:00:36.790
and went on for a
decade and more.

01:00:36.790 --> 01:00:39.340
And one of the amazing
things about the revolution

01:00:39.340 --> 01:00:42.280
was that Haiti was
considered so important

01:00:42.280 --> 01:00:46.690
to the French economy-- again,
like Bangladesh, now considered

01:00:46.690 --> 01:00:48.970
the international basket
case of the hemisphere--

01:00:48.970 --> 01:00:54.490
that the largest European armada
ever to set forth from Europe

01:00:54.490 --> 01:00:58.930
since or before left France
under Napoleon's direction

01:00:58.930 --> 01:01:00.130
to retake Haiti.

01:01:00.130 --> 01:01:03.880
80,000 French troops and
also conscripts from Poland

01:01:03.880 --> 01:01:07.330
and other places that the
democratically-minded Napoleon

01:01:07.330 --> 01:01:11.110
was taking over in
Europe, 80,000 troops--

01:01:11.110 --> 01:01:13.720
40,000 Navy and
40,000 ground troops--

01:01:13.720 --> 01:01:16.090
went to Haiti, which,
again, even to this day

01:01:16.090 --> 01:01:19.180
is just astounding to think
of that sort of movement

01:01:19.180 --> 01:01:21.530
of troops across the Atlantic.

01:01:21.530 --> 01:01:24.160
And he sent his brother-in-law,
General Leclerc,

01:01:24.160 --> 01:01:26.260
who was married to
Pauline Bonaparte, who

01:01:26.260 --> 01:01:29.260
died in Haiti, in northern
Haiti, never returned.

01:01:29.260 --> 01:01:31.540
Pauline Bonaparte
also lived there.

01:01:31.540 --> 01:01:34.740
And this was, again,
at a horrible cost

01:01:34.740 --> 01:01:36.940
to the Haitian people.

01:01:36.940 --> 01:01:40.870
They started calling themselves
Haitians during the revolution,

01:01:40.870 --> 01:01:41.800
during the war.

01:01:41.800 --> 01:01:45.220
And when I say horrible
cost, the General Leclerc

01:01:45.220 --> 01:01:48.310
wrote a letter back
to his brother-in-law

01:01:48.310 --> 01:01:50.980
at one point
saying, we can never

01:01:50.980 --> 01:01:56.230
retake this colony unless
we kill every single man

01:01:56.230 --> 01:01:58.270
or woman who has
carried epaulettes,

01:01:58.270 --> 01:02:01.180
meaning the slaves who are now
in the army which is being led

01:02:01.180 --> 01:02:03.970
by the leaders of
the slave revolt,

01:02:03.970 --> 01:02:07.400
and every child above the age
of 13 who has done so as well.

01:02:07.400 --> 01:02:09.910
This is in the correspondence
between General Leclerc

01:02:09.910 --> 01:02:12.070
and Napoleon shortly
before he died.

01:02:12.070 --> 01:02:15.520
So he already was
sending messages back

01:02:15.520 --> 01:02:17.230
home that this was
not going to work,

01:02:17.230 --> 01:02:20.740
that they were going to fail
unless they had a campaign

01:02:20.740 --> 01:02:25.000
to wipe out all of the adults.

01:02:25.000 --> 01:02:28.860
And by adults-- as he
said, 13 years and over.

01:02:28.860 --> 01:02:32.340
Now, what happened after
the revolution has already

01:02:32.340 --> 01:02:35.040
been commented on here.

01:02:35.040 --> 01:02:37.770
The revolution
ended in the battle

01:02:37.770 --> 01:02:41.460
I just showed you, the Battle of
Vertieres in November of 1803.

01:02:41.460 --> 01:02:44.340
And on January 1st,
the Haitians declared

01:02:44.340 --> 01:02:46.200
independence, the first
independent nation

01:02:46.200 --> 01:02:47.460
in Latin America.

01:02:47.460 --> 01:02:51.850
They took the French tricolor
and ripped out the white part

01:02:51.850 --> 01:02:54.240
and, of course, saying we're
getting rid of the whites.

01:02:54.240 --> 01:02:57.570
And the Haitian
flag was initially

01:02:57.570 --> 01:03:00.930
the red and blue bicolor
that was recently restored.

01:03:00.930 --> 01:03:05.670
And in that year, they
declared Haiti a safe haven

01:03:05.670 --> 01:03:09.320
for any indigenous person and
any escaped slave in the New

01:03:09.320 --> 01:03:09.820
World.

01:03:09.820 --> 01:03:12.600
So that was another part of
these unprogressive features

01:03:12.600 --> 01:03:14.130
of their constitution.

01:03:14.130 --> 01:03:19.040
Noam mentioned, also, that it
said no European, no foreigner,

01:03:19.040 --> 01:03:21.720
will ever set foot in this
country as a landowner.

01:03:21.720 --> 01:03:26.940
And that was part of Dessalines'
constitution of 1804.

01:03:26.940 --> 01:03:29.100
And Dessalines, as
you might imagine,

01:03:29.100 --> 01:03:31.290
is regarded as the father
of Haitian independence

01:03:31.290 --> 01:03:35.700
by the Haitians and regarded
as a butcher by the Americans

01:03:35.700 --> 01:03:37.740
and their friends in Europe.

01:03:37.740 --> 01:03:39.960
Now, how does this
work itself out?

01:03:39.960 --> 01:03:42.300
Nancy used the
word trade embargo.

01:03:42.300 --> 01:03:44.910
But, actually,
what we usually do

01:03:44.910 --> 01:03:47.670
with Haiti is not
a trade embargo.

01:03:47.670 --> 01:03:50.970
And you clarified that by
saying unfavorable conditions

01:03:50.970 --> 01:03:52.920
for the Haitians and
very favorable conditions

01:03:52.920 --> 01:03:55.650
for the Europeans and Americans.

01:03:55.650 --> 01:03:57.010
But it's not a trade embargo.

01:03:57.010 --> 01:03:57.930
It was worse.

01:03:57.930 --> 01:04:01.740
It was a refusal to recognize
Haiti's independence

01:04:01.740 --> 01:04:02.260
as a state.

01:04:02.260 --> 01:04:06.750
And as has already been said,
this went on till in 1862

01:04:06.750 --> 01:04:09.210
and this is just stuff
from, again, the US

01:04:09.210 --> 01:04:11.020
official congressional record.

01:04:11.020 --> 01:04:13.800
You know, Noam gave
me advice years ago.

01:04:13.800 --> 01:04:16.380
He said, if you're going to
write books like that one,

01:04:16.380 --> 01:04:18.870
try to use formal
US official sources.

01:04:18.870 --> 01:04:22.000
And I followed that
over the years.

01:04:22.000 --> 01:04:25.830
It's a great source, I mean, of
information about our policy.

01:04:25.830 --> 01:04:30.060
And this is 1824, a senator
from South Carolina.

01:04:30.060 --> 01:04:32.130
Since this is being
taped, I'll read it.

01:04:32.130 --> 01:04:33.840
"Our policy with
regard to Haiti--

01:04:33.840 --> 01:04:35.730
Hay-ti-- is plain.

01:04:35.730 --> 01:04:37.500
We can never acknowledge
her independence.

01:04:37.500 --> 01:04:40.350
The peace and safety of a
large portion of our union

01:04:40.350 --> 01:04:42.360
forbids us even to discuss it."

01:04:42.360 --> 01:04:47.520
And this was the standard
fare inside the great halls

01:04:47.520 --> 01:04:51.660
of democratic United
States during those years,

01:04:51.660 --> 01:04:54.990
those decades, in which we were
exploiting relations to Haiti,

01:04:54.990 --> 01:04:56.550
but not recognizing.

01:04:56.550 --> 01:04:57.990
Here's the kicker to me.

01:04:57.990 --> 01:05:01.290
And this was mentioned, too.

01:05:01.290 --> 01:05:05.280
The Haitians, of course, still
were growing tropical produce

01:05:05.280 --> 01:05:08.380
after the abolition slavery.

01:05:08.380 --> 01:05:12.660
However, they were doing it with
free people, peasants mostly.

01:05:12.660 --> 01:05:14.314
And coffee is
something you don't

01:05:14.314 --> 01:05:15.480
need to grow on plantations.

01:05:15.480 --> 01:05:18.150
You can grow on small plots
of land, on hillsides.

01:05:18.150 --> 01:05:23.370
And it replaced sugar and cotton
as the main exports of Haiti,

01:05:23.370 --> 01:05:26.250
because peasants could grow it
without working on plantations.

01:05:26.250 --> 01:05:29.550
And I can promise you that the
Haitian peasantry has never

01:05:29.550 --> 01:05:31.970
wanted to work on plantations.

01:05:31.970 --> 01:05:33.450
I mean, I can't imagine why not.

01:05:33.450 --> 01:05:35.940
The conditions had been
so pleasant before.

01:05:35.940 --> 01:05:38.250
So it's been a long struggle.

01:05:38.250 --> 01:05:40.110
The land reform
struggle in Haiti

01:05:40.110 --> 01:05:42.630
is very interesting
and very poignant.

01:05:42.630 --> 01:05:47.430
But the bottom line is most
of the Haitian people refused

01:05:47.430 --> 01:05:49.680
to work under anything
that looked like plantation

01:05:49.680 --> 01:05:52.050
conditions, and so
were exporting coffee

01:05:52.050 --> 01:05:54.330
more than any other crop.

01:05:54.330 --> 01:05:57.960
But the amazing and,
you know, horrible thing

01:05:57.960 --> 01:06:01.620
is that when people talk
about reparations now--

01:06:01.620 --> 01:06:04.620
and Nancy mentioned the
Durban Declaration--

01:06:04.620 --> 01:06:06.570
the Haitians already
paid reparations

01:06:06.570 --> 01:06:08.610
to the slave owners.

01:06:08.610 --> 01:06:14.940
And the size of the payment was
astounding, 150 million francs.

01:06:14.940 --> 01:06:19.380
And according to a fairly
sober late 19th century Haitian

01:06:19.380 --> 01:06:25.470
historian, early 20th century,
who looked at this, you know,

01:06:25.470 --> 01:06:28.620
in retrospect just
aghast, he said

01:06:28.620 --> 01:06:32.100
that, from a relatively
balanced economy

01:06:32.100 --> 01:06:39.000
prior to the payment of this
massive debt to the French,

01:06:39.000 --> 01:06:42.450
this led to a major disruption
that really continued

01:06:42.450 --> 01:06:45.210
to have its impact on
the Haitian economy

01:06:45.210 --> 01:06:50.700
throughout the rest of the 19th
century, this payment of debt.

01:06:50.700 --> 01:06:53.650
And the French at this time were
also dominating Haitian trade.

01:06:53.650 --> 01:06:55.766
And that went on
along with struggles

01:06:55.766 --> 01:06:57.140
from the British
and the Germans.

01:06:57.140 --> 01:06:58.710
And some of you
might not know that

01:06:58.710 --> 01:07:02.850
at one point in late 18th,
19th century, the Germans--

01:07:02.850 --> 01:07:04.590
and I believe was under Kaiser.

01:07:04.590 --> 01:07:08.790
Because Kaiser sent some
very lovely envoys to Haiti,

01:07:08.790 --> 01:07:13.770
one of whom took the Haitian
flag and smeared excrement

01:07:13.770 --> 01:07:17.350
on it in the Bay
of Port-au-Prince,

01:07:17.350 --> 01:07:20.580
the military envoy of
the Kaiser of Germany.

01:07:20.580 --> 01:07:24.780
That was the way that the
civilized societies of Europe

01:07:24.780 --> 01:07:27.990
chose to mark their
respect for Haiti.

01:07:27.990 --> 01:07:32.340
And this went on
for some decades.

01:07:32.340 --> 01:07:34.049
Europe, France,
Britain-- I mean, sorry--

01:07:34.049 --> 01:07:36.423
France, Germany, and Britain
vying with the United States

01:07:36.423 --> 01:07:37.230
for influence.

01:07:37.230 --> 01:07:39.190
But by the end of
the century, there

01:07:39.190 --> 01:07:41.520
was really no competition.

01:07:41.520 --> 01:07:46.870
Although, when the Americans
sent in the Marines in 1915,

01:07:46.870 --> 01:07:49.470
they claimed one reason was
to limit German influence

01:07:49.470 --> 01:07:52.680
in Haiti, which had already
been virtually erased

01:07:52.680 --> 01:07:56.130
in the previous two decades.

01:07:56.130 --> 01:07:58.050
So when the Marines
went in, then

01:07:58.050 --> 01:08:04.500
began the intensified series
of events and processes

01:08:04.500 --> 01:08:06.630
of development and
scientific development that

01:08:06.630 --> 01:08:09.150
have made Haiti the
paradise that it is today.

01:08:09.150 --> 01:08:11.940
I would just add that
the modern Haitian

01:08:11.940 --> 01:08:18.420
army was created by an act
of Congress, US Congress,

01:08:18.420 --> 01:08:21.120
and signed into
existence in Washington.

01:08:21.120 --> 01:08:24.640
And I'm going to soon switch
to a sort of local analysis.

01:08:24.640 --> 01:08:27.270
But when I talk about
the valley in which we've

01:08:27.270 --> 01:08:29.370
been working for a
long time, you'll

01:08:29.370 --> 01:08:32.370
see very similar kinds of events
that are really transnational.

01:08:32.370 --> 01:08:34.350
I mean, the modern
Haitian army--

01:08:34.350 --> 01:08:38.264
how often do we
acknowledge that, you know,

01:08:38.264 --> 01:08:41.220
the army that we suddenly
declared a rogue army

01:08:41.220 --> 01:08:43.500
after, you know,
really uncompromising

01:08:43.500 --> 01:08:45.149
and ongoing support
for many decades,

01:08:45.149 --> 01:08:48.210
was really created by the
American occupiers-- not just

01:08:48.210 --> 01:08:49.750
trained, but created?

01:08:49.750 --> 01:08:54.120
And then in came the
agribusiness and missionaries

01:08:54.120 --> 01:08:57.689
of the early part of the
last century, 20th century.

01:08:57.689 --> 01:09:00.149
And the kind of
appropriations of land

01:09:00.149 --> 01:09:07.350
that Noam mentioned
in his talk, these

01:09:07.350 --> 01:09:10.170
were usually with these sort of
partnerships with the Haitian

01:09:10.170 --> 01:09:13.529
elites, so that there would
be Haitian elite ownership,

01:09:13.529 --> 01:09:18.106
meaning wealthy people in Haiti,
and US ownership of, you know,

01:09:18.106 --> 01:09:19.149
a plantation.

01:09:19.149 --> 01:09:20.189
Noam mentioned rubber.

01:09:20.189 --> 01:09:23.970
But during the World
War II, Haiti also

01:09:23.970 --> 01:09:27.000
began growing sisal
hemp-- or sy-sal hemp--

01:09:27.000 --> 01:09:32.880
in order to make big
ropes for the ships.

01:09:32.880 --> 01:09:34.700
This is before nylon
replaced those.

01:09:34.700 --> 01:09:38.790
Haiti was suddenly the world's
leading producer of hemp,

01:09:38.790 --> 01:09:41.760
because they could
flip the switches

01:09:41.760 --> 01:09:44.010
on these plantations--
rubber, hemp,

01:09:44.010 --> 01:09:47.801
whatever was necessary locally.

01:09:47.801 --> 01:09:48.300
I'm kidding.

01:09:48.300 --> 01:09:50.050
Because, of course, I
don't know that hemp

01:09:50.050 --> 01:09:51.510
has many utilities locally.

01:09:51.510 --> 01:09:54.390
I guess you can make
baskets or things out of it.

01:09:54.390 --> 01:09:58.637
Now, what can we read
in that great journal,

01:09:58.637 --> 01:10:00.720
the national journal of
record The New York Times?

01:10:00.720 --> 01:10:03.240
It's already been quoted today.

01:10:03.240 --> 01:10:06.210
Instability was the
pretext reducing

01:10:06.210 --> 01:10:09.750
European, German,
influence during the war.

01:10:09.750 --> 01:10:12.400
The Convention
haitiano-améicaine--

01:10:12.400 --> 01:10:15.120
spelled wrong, sorry--

01:10:15.120 --> 01:10:18.600
basically, it gave
the United States

01:10:18.600 --> 01:10:21.480
complete administrative
control of Haiti including

01:10:21.480 --> 01:10:24.300
the ability, as was mentioned,
to rewrite the Constitution

01:10:24.300 --> 01:10:28.230
to remove those very
unproductive features

01:10:28.230 --> 01:10:30.450
of the Haitian Constitution.

01:10:30.450 --> 01:10:34.920
And the troops stayed
in Haiti until 1934.

01:10:34.920 --> 01:10:37.080
And you already heard
about the resistance.

01:10:37.080 --> 01:10:40.290
The Haitian people gave spirited
resistance to the occupation.

01:10:40.290 --> 01:10:43.950
And that resistance was
based among the peasantry.

01:10:43.950 --> 01:10:46.917
In the very place where I work,
Central Haiti, Central Plateau,

01:10:46.917 --> 01:10:48.500
that's where the
resistance was based.

01:10:48.500 --> 01:10:50.730
It did not come from
the Haitian elite.

01:10:50.730 --> 01:10:54.360
Although, there were
some intellectuals who

01:10:54.360 --> 01:10:58.890
opposed the American operation
in their own somewhat

01:10:58.890 --> 01:11:00.690
timorous way compared
to the peasantry,

01:11:00.690 --> 01:11:03.180
who really opposed it.

01:11:03.180 --> 01:11:07.470
The occupation of Haiti led to
the restoration of forced labor

01:11:07.470 --> 01:11:09.360
crews, [FRENCH].

01:11:09.360 --> 01:11:11.790
The major roads built
during that time

01:11:11.790 --> 01:11:14.230
were built largely
with forced labor.

01:11:14.230 --> 01:11:16.020
There's grotesque
documentation of

01:11:16.020 --> 01:11:19.460
this available in the
historical record.

01:11:19.460 --> 01:11:20.880
And it was very
interesting to see

01:11:20.880 --> 01:11:25.270
how it gets sanitized and retold
in the non-Haitian accounting

01:11:25.270 --> 01:11:25.770
of this.

01:11:25.770 --> 01:11:28.500
But the Haitian accountings
had the added advantage

01:11:28.500 --> 01:11:29.370
of being correct.

01:11:29.370 --> 01:11:31.620
[LAUGHTER]

01:11:31.620 --> 01:11:35.760
Now, this led rather
seamlessly, in fact,

01:11:35.760 --> 01:11:37.710
to the Duvalier
family dictatorship.

01:11:37.710 --> 01:11:42.450
That is no one could
come to power in Haiti

01:11:42.450 --> 01:11:44.640
without the benediction
of the US government

01:11:44.640 --> 01:11:47.100
from the time of the
occupation, of course,

01:11:47.100 --> 01:11:49.460
when it was overt and formal.

01:11:49.460 --> 01:11:55.780
But from 1934 till
1990, that was really

01:11:55.780 --> 01:11:59.760
the prerequisite for coming to
power was to have US backing,

01:11:59.760 --> 01:12:04.200
you know, from the US ambassador
and from the latest whatever

01:12:04.200 --> 01:12:10.020
USAID might be called on that
particular day or the other US

01:12:10.020 --> 01:12:11.220
institutions.

01:12:11.220 --> 01:12:14.372
And so forth Francois
Duvalier, you

01:12:14.372 --> 01:12:16.830
know, again, I think there's
a lot of confusion about this.

01:12:16.830 --> 01:12:21.570
Francois Duvalier was supported
by the American embassy

01:12:21.570 --> 01:12:24.157
in Haiti in 1957.

01:12:24.157 --> 01:12:25.740
He received the
report of the embassy.

01:12:25.740 --> 01:12:28.020
Because you read a lot
about the United States

01:12:28.020 --> 01:12:31.775
spirited resistance to
the Duvalier dictatorship,

01:12:31.775 --> 01:12:33.150
you know of course,
not in Haiti,

01:12:33.150 --> 01:12:34.680
but in the United States.

01:12:34.680 --> 01:12:38.580
But it was pretty
unremitting, our support

01:12:38.580 --> 01:12:39.990
for the Duvalier dictatorship.

01:12:39.990 --> 01:12:41.610
There were a couple
of years where

01:12:41.610 --> 01:12:46.080
the activities of the
dictatorship were so terrible.

01:12:46.080 --> 01:12:48.859
I mean, just to give an
example, which, again, seems

01:12:48.859 --> 01:12:50.400
to have escaped
everybody's mind when

01:12:50.400 --> 01:12:54.450
they're talking about the reign
of terror that exists now--

01:12:54.450 --> 01:12:57.120
I mean, Francois Duvalier
would have someone killed

01:12:57.120 --> 01:13:00.720
and then display the body across
from the airport for, you know,

01:13:00.720 --> 01:13:01.500
days.

01:13:01.500 --> 01:13:04.350
So that's not very
subtle symbolism

01:13:04.350 --> 01:13:06.900
according to anthropologists
with whom I work.

01:13:06.900 --> 01:13:11.160
And this was, again,
obviously meant

01:13:11.160 --> 01:13:15.390
to draw attention to the fact
that the dictatorship would

01:13:15.390 --> 01:13:21.150
tolerate any sort of
challenge to its power.

01:13:21.150 --> 01:13:27.450
Every single independent
press, radio, newspaper,

01:13:27.450 --> 01:13:29.700
the few independent-minded
intellectuals

01:13:29.700 --> 01:13:32.130
that may have theoretically
existed at that time,

01:13:32.130 --> 01:13:35.370
they left or were killed.

01:13:35.370 --> 01:13:38.850
And it took about,
you know, 10 years

01:13:38.850 --> 01:13:42.540
to really shut down any sort
of threat from the military,

01:13:42.540 --> 01:13:46.080
from the army, which
had been previously

01:13:46.080 --> 01:13:50.220
the primary determinant of who
would come to power, the United

01:13:50.220 --> 01:13:52.530
States and the
Haitian military--

01:13:52.530 --> 01:13:55.890
not that those two institutions
had any relation, of course.

01:13:55.890 --> 01:13:58.440
And so Francois
Duvalier undermined

01:13:58.440 --> 01:14:01.890
the army and the opposition,
silenced the opposition very

01:14:01.890 --> 01:14:07.770
completely, and spent the
rest of his very long reign--

01:14:07.770 --> 01:14:09.900
he used to say
[SPEAKING FRENCH]..

01:14:09.900 --> 01:14:12.450
I'm going from the palace
to the cemetery, you know?

01:14:12.450 --> 01:14:13.410
And he did.

01:14:13.410 --> 01:14:15.120
He was there until he died.

01:14:15.120 --> 01:14:19.110
And then his 19-year-old son,
who, from what I understand,

01:14:19.110 --> 01:14:21.830
had never left Haiti, became
president of the oldest

01:14:21.830 --> 01:14:23.060
Republica in Latin America--

01:14:23.060 --> 01:14:25.440
19-year-old son who had,
you know, of course,

01:14:25.440 --> 01:14:27.400
an advanced degree from
The Fletcher School.

01:14:27.400 --> 01:14:29.484
Anyway-- I'm kidding.

01:14:29.484 --> 01:14:30.150
That was a joke.

01:14:34.530 --> 01:14:35.850
Well, anyway, I better be good.

01:14:35.850 --> 01:14:36.570
I'm on tape.

01:14:36.570 --> 01:14:37.525
Sorry.

01:14:37.525 --> 01:14:39.244
[LAUGHTER]

01:14:43.640 --> 01:14:47.360
Now, 1971 that happened.

01:14:47.360 --> 01:14:51.380
And the years from
'71 to '86 were

01:14:51.380 --> 01:14:55.250
years of decline for reasons
that are acknowledged.

01:14:55.250 --> 01:14:57.170
And there were really
thousands of refugees.

01:14:57.170 --> 01:15:00.830
You heard about the welcome they
got from us, a really amazingly

01:15:00.830 --> 01:15:03.050
repugnant policy, actually.

01:15:03.050 --> 01:15:07.880
The seizing and interception of
refugee ships on the high seas

01:15:07.880 --> 01:15:10.490
and then the return
of refugees is, again,

01:15:10.490 --> 01:15:13.070
in violation of many
treaties to which the United

01:15:13.070 --> 01:15:15.395
States is a formal signatory.

01:15:15.395 --> 01:15:19.010
Is that [INAUDIBLE], signatory?

01:15:19.010 --> 01:15:21.120
But it didn't
bother us too much.

01:15:21.120 --> 01:15:24.140
And then there was political
unrest that began in '85

01:15:24.140 --> 01:15:26.720
and was very much associated
with a popular movement.

01:15:26.720 --> 01:15:29.570
That is a movement based,
as has been mentioned,

01:15:29.570 --> 01:15:33.560
among the rural poor and
the urban poor in slums.

01:15:33.560 --> 01:15:36.975
And it was an amazing thing to
be living in Haiti at that time

01:15:36.975 --> 01:15:37.850
and be working there.

01:15:37.850 --> 01:15:39.800
Because I had
never seen anything

01:15:39.800 --> 01:15:44.270
like that in my
experience as an American.

01:15:44.270 --> 01:15:47.090
There was so much
popular support

01:15:47.090 --> 01:15:49.940
for the popular movement
that virtually every member,

01:15:49.940 --> 01:15:52.340
every person who
lived in the village

01:15:52.340 --> 01:15:55.550
where I was living and
working, am living and working,

01:15:55.550 --> 01:15:58.950
everyone was part of the popular
movement without any exception.

01:15:58.950 --> 01:16:04.100
And in 1990 when,
at the last minute,

01:16:04.100 --> 01:16:07.870
Aristide announced his
candidature, you know,

01:16:07.870 --> 01:16:11.120
you could practically hear
the stampede of people

01:16:11.120 --> 01:16:12.260
going to register to vote.

01:16:12.260 --> 01:16:17.570
Because after what
happened in 1987,

01:16:17.570 --> 01:16:21.200
which is unstinting support from
the United States government

01:16:21.200 --> 01:16:23.240
for the Haitian military--

01:16:23.240 --> 01:16:24.800
and here's just
a number for you.

01:16:24.800 --> 01:16:28.070
Over $200 million in aid
passed through the hands

01:16:28.070 --> 01:16:29.480
of the military junta.

01:16:29.480 --> 01:16:32.090
I actually am going to
risk saying this on tape.

01:16:32.090 --> 01:16:36.440
I spoke with someone
today in a prominent US

01:16:36.440 --> 01:16:41.150
office with the fate
of Haiti in its hands.

01:16:41.150 --> 01:16:42.560
I will just say that.

01:16:42.560 --> 01:16:45.290
And he was saying,
well, you know,

01:16:45.290 --> 01:16:51.020
I would really like to see
the frozen aid released.

01:16:51.020 --> 01:16:56.080
What we need to do is get
the government to, you know,

01:16:56.080 --> 01:17:02.000
promise to use the aid in
an open and clear manner

01:17:02.000 --> 01:17:05.000
and be subject to various
forms of scrutiny, et cetera.

01:17:05.000 --> 01:17:05.940
And I couldn't resist.

01:17:05.940 --> 01:17:06.898
I couldn't stop myself.

01:17:06.898 --> 01:17:08.840
I knew I just sort
of said nothing.

01:17:08.840 --> 01:17:11.750
I was standing in the ICU unit
in the surgical ICU, you know,

01:17:11.750 --> 01:17:13.700
with a phone and trying to
write a note about a patient,

01:17:13.700 --> 01:17:15.616
listening to this guy,
knowing that, you know,

01:17:15.616 --> 01:17:17.640
I didn't have to
listen the whole time.

01:17:17.640 --> 01:17:21.740
But I said to him,
well, you know,

01:17:21.740 --> 01:17:23.240
I've been working
in Haiti 20 years.

01:17:23.240 --> 01:17:26.420
And, you know, during
the dictatorship,

01:17:26.420 --> 01:17:28.820
there was never any sort
of real scrutiny of USAID

01:17:28.820 --> 01:17:30.320
and during the military juntas.

01:17:30.320 --> 01:17:31.820
Why now?

01:17:31.820 --> 01:17:33.320
And then he just
said, you're right.

01:17:33.320 --> 01:17:36.376
I really don't understand
US foreign policy.

01:17:36.376 --> 01:17:38.750
I did not say I'm going to
give a talk with Noam Chomsky.

01:17:38.750 --> 01:17:41.060
I just said, I've got to
get back to seeing patients.

01:17:41.060 --> 01:17:43.340
And I'll call you tomorrow.

01:17:43.340 --> 01:17:46.400
Now, you already heard
that we trained a lot

01:17:46.400 --> 01:17:49.539
of those very delightful
military officers who held sway

01:17:49.539 --> 01:17:50.330
during these times.

01:17:50.330 --> 01:17:54.260
I'm going to skip ahead
to what happened in 1987

01:17:54.260 --> 01:17:56.690
and skip through some of
the stuff where we were.

01:17:56.690 --> 01:17:59.990
But in 1987, there
was a massacre

01:17:59.990 --> 01:18:01.880
of voters at the polling place.

01:18:01.880 --> 01:18:03.770
Now, again, that's more
than 5 minutes ago,

01:18:03.770 --> 01:18:05.180
so everyone's forgotten.

01:18:05.180 --> 01:18:06.350
But can you imagine?

01:18:06.350 --> 01:18:08.330
I was there in Haiti that day.

01:18:08.330 --> 01:18:11.360
And can you imagine
seeing groups of people

01:18:11.360 --> 01:18:16.370
lined up to vote and then, you
know, seeing them taken out

01:18:16.370 --> 01:18:17.300
at the polls?

01:18:17.300 --> 01:18:19.900
I mean, this was
really incredible.

01:18:19.900 --> 01:18:28.100
And, of course, in the middle of
the reign of a military junta--

01:18:28.100 --> 01:18:30.440
I mean, granted it
is rather far-fetched

01:18:30.440 --> 01:18:32.570
to suggest that the military
may have had something

01:18:32.570 --> 01:18:33.236
to do with this.

01:18:33.236 --> 01:18:36.440
But it was pretty
clear to the Haitians

01:18:36.440 --> 01:18:42.260
that this was basically the
action of the Haitian army,

01:18:42.260 --> 01:18:45.760
which has had no non-Haitian
enemies, you know,

01:18:45.760 --> 01:18:47.210
in hundreds of years.

01:18:47.210 --> 01:18:50.090
Only Haitians have been the
enemies of the Haitian army.

01:18:50.090 --> 01:18:51.920
And so a lot of
Haitians were not

01:18:51.920 --> 01:18:54.900
going to vote in 1990
until Aristide registered.

01:18:54.900 --> 01:18:58.560
And then it was something like
a million people registered

01:18:58.560 --> 01:19:00.470
in the last two days.

01:19:00.470 --> 01:19:04.940
And so, you know, experience
that, to see that happen

01:19:04.940 --> 01:19:08.800
and to experience that, was
really very eye opening for me.

01:19:08.800 --> 01:19:12.350
In the village, you
know, where we work,

01:19:12.350 --> 01:19:16.017
there was this one woman who's
a good friend of mine who's

01:19:16.017 --> 01:19:18.350
what we used to call when I
was growing up a lunch lady.

01:19:18.350 --> 01:19:20.390
She makes lunch in
the school cafeteria.

01:19:20.390 --> 01:19:23.090
And she was and
remains, as do most

01:19:23.090 --> 01:19:26.180
of them, a very devout
Aristide supporter.

01:19:26.180 --> 01:19:28.490
And I told her that I had
been listening to the radio.

01:19:28.490 --> 01:19:30.684
And I heard a poll
from Port-au-Prince.

01:19:30.684 --> 01:19:31.850
And she said, what's a poll?

01:19:31.850 --> 01:19:35.450
And I said, well, that's when
people go out on the street

01:19:35.450 --> 01:19:37.770
and ask people how
they're going to vote.

01:19:37.770 --> 01:19:39.140
And she said, what did it show?

01:19:39.140 --> 01:19:41.390
And I said, that
Aristide would win over

01:19:41.390 --> 01:19:44.840
50% of the vote in the first
elections without a runoff.

01:19:44.840 --> 01:19:46.690
And she said, well,
that can't be true.

01:19:46.690 --> 01:19:50.910
And I said, I thought you
were a big Aristide supporter.

01:19:50.910 --> 01:19:53.900
And she said, oh yeah,
he'll win 99% of the vote

01:19:53.900 --> 01:19:55.580
if it's a re-election.

01:19:55.580 --> 01:19:57.641
And so she thought
that was unlikely,

01:19:57.641 --> 01:19:59.390
that even though there
were 11 candidates,

01:19:59.390 --> 01:20:02.570
that he would win with only a
plurality in the first round

01:20:02.570 --> 01:20:03.320
with 50-something.

01:20:03.320 --> 01:20:07.050
And, in fact, he did win
with a much higher plurality

01:20:07.050 --> 01:20:08.540
in the first election.

01:20:08.540 --> 01:20:11.560
There were never any runoffs.

01:20:11.560 --> 01:20:14.420
I mean, now, granted, we
always have very clear outcomes

01:20:14.420 --> 01:20:17.870
in our elections as well and
that runoffs are not necessary.

01:20:17.870 --> 01:20:20.690
But, you know, it was still
a learning experience for me

01:20:20.690 --> 01:20:22.940
even though, you know,
I'm American, so therefore

01:20:22.940 --> 01:20:25.010
democratic by training.

01:20:25.010 --> 01:20:28.010
Now, in the interest
of skipping ahead,

01:20:28.010 --> 01:20:31.370
I'm going to just close
by talking about health

01:20:31.370 --> 01:20:32.392
conditions in Haiti.

01:20:32.392 --> 01:20:33.350
Because it's important.

01:20:33.350 --> 01:20:37.890
And I think everybody here
should know about this.

01:20:37.890 --> 01:20:42.620
The health situation in Haiti
is dire by any kind of criteria.

01:20:42.620 --> 01:20:45.660
And this is an
image-- not an image--

01:20:45.660 --> 01:20:49.040
a picture of a girl
with typhoid fever,

01:20:49.040 --> 01:20:53.590
which along with
tuberculosis and other

01:20:53.590 --> 01:20:56.600
what the Haitians call
stupid diseases, actually,

01:20:56.600 --> 01:21:00.920
are the primary determinants
of life and death in Haiti.

01:21:00.920 --> 01:21:03.290
So, of course, one of
the first things to do

01:21:03.290 --> 01:21:05.030
is to have a public
health system that

01:21:05.030 --> 01:21:06.710
takes on these problems.

01:21:06.710 --> 01:21:09.890
By the way, I couldn't resist
putting this image here.

01:21:09.890 --> 01:21:12.170
This is a child with anthrax.

01:21:12.170 --> 01:21:14.180
And, you know, it
must be because Haiti

01:21:14.180 --> 01:21:17.960
is a terrorist state that they,
you know, have anthrax there.

01:21:17.960 --> 01:21:21.560
That was actually brought
up in the American press.

01:21:21.560 --> 01:21:24.290
And, you know, Haiti
has real anthrax,

01:21:24.290 --> 01:21:28.520
meaning the kind that's
zoonosis, contact with animals.

01:21:28.520 --> 01:21:30.950
In the case of this girl,
she has anthrax of the face.

01:21:30.950 --> 01:21:32.900
It was a goat.

01:21:32.900 --> 01:21:37.070
And someone died of
anthrax in this outbreak.

01:21:37.070 --> 01:21:39.380
She came in with
another, an adult.

01:21:39.380 --> 01:21:42.440
And they got treated and
did fine, as one does.

01:21:42.440 --> 01:21:45.162
But someone died
in their village.

01:21:45.162 --> 01:21:47.120
Anyway, someone mentioned
in the American press

01:21:47.120 --> 01:21:50.150
that Haiti could be a terrorist
state manufacturing anthrax.

01:21:50.150 --> 01:21:54.130
And a Haitian veterinarian
said, that's just ridiculous.

01:21:54.130 --> 01:21:56.090
And, well, you can't
respond to every one

01:21:56.090 --> 01:21:59.496
of these nut-so suggestions.

01:21:59.496 --> 01:22:01.370
And I had this image in
my mind of, you know,

01:22:01.370 --> 01:22:03.260
a group of Haitian
goats getting together

01:22:03.260 --> 01:22:06.350
and say, let's get a
lab going and get some

01:22:06.350 --> 01:22:09.200
of this stuff off of our fur.

01:22:09.200 --> 01:22:11.330
[LAUGHTER]

01:22:11.330 --> 01:22:15.280
Anyway, now, I told you
about election day 1987.

01:22:15.280 --> 01:22:18.980
And I mean, this is
inside a school, you know.

01:22:18.980 --> 01:22:22.440
And it's just awful
what happened.

01:22:22.440 --> 01:22:25.490
And so the willingness
to participate

01:22:25.490 --> 01:22:29.480
in this election of 1990 was
really a strong statement

01:22:29.480 --> 01:22:32.670
about commitment to democracy.

01:22:32.670 --> 01:22:34.640
And so I mentioned
what happened already.

01:22:34.640 --> 01:22:38.630
But after seven months, there
was a violent military coup.

01:22:38.630 --> 01:22:39.410
And you know this.

01:22:39.410 --> 01:22:41.240
And it's already been
discussed by Noam,

01:22:41.240 --> 01:22:44.030
which led to the deaths of
really thousands of people

01:22:44.030 --> 01:22:46.610
and massive internal
migration in addition

01:22:46.610 --> 01:22:48.080
to external migration.

01:22:48.080 --> 01:22:51.140
Internal migration, because we
were working in rural Haiti.

01:22:51.140 --> 01:22:54.200
And people fled from the
slums out to where we work.

01:22:54.200 --> 01:22:57.390
And so we saw, you
know, enormous changes.

01:22:57.390 --> 01:23:00.320
And it was just a huge
and ongoing disruption

01:23:00.320 --> 01:23:00.990
for a long time.

01:23:00.990 --> 01:23:03.640
And as Noam said, it
was very frightening

01:23:03.640 --> 01:23:05.370
and not theoretically.

01:23:05.370 --> 01:23:07.130
I mean, I personally
know a number

01:23:07.130 --> 01:23:11.060
of people who were
killed during that time,

01:23:11.060 --> 01:23:15.931
including a Haitian businessman,
one of the only ones to say,

01:23:15.931 --> 01:23:17.930
well, you know, the popular
movement is probably

01:23:17.930 --> 01:23:20.600
a good thing, since
it includes about 90%

01:23:20.600 --> 01:23:22.100
of the Haitian population.

01:23:22.100 --> 01:23:26.840
And his brother was murdered
in downtown Port-au-Prince.

01:23:26.840 --> 01:23:29.090
And during a memorial
mass for his brother,

01:23:29.090 --> 01:23:31.370
he was shot in the head
and killed during mass

01:23:31.370 --> 01:23:33.710
by paramilitary forces.

01:23:33.710 --> 01:23:36.180
And this is the level.

01:23:36.180 --> 01:23:38.750
And this is a prominent
businessman who, you know,

01:23:38.750 --> 01:23:42.790
was very wealthy actually, who
made the mistake of not doing

01:23:42.790 --> 01:23:45.290
what business people in Haiti
are supposed to do and saying,

01:23:45.290 --> 01:23:48.410
you know, these savages from the
hills and slums really cannot

01:23:48.410 --> 01:23:51.680
play a role in the
future of Haiti.

01:23:51.680 --> 01:23:54.110
And then, again, the
embargo has already

01:23:54.110 --> 01:23:56.247
been discussed,
the fake embargo.

01:23:56.247 --> 01:23:57.830
Now, we have a real
embargo, mind you.

01:23:57.830 --> 01:23:58.610
But the fake one--

01:23:58.610 --> 01:24:01.760
I mean, I, again, am sitting
in Haiti reading The New York

01:24:01.760 --> 01:24:06.070
Times and thinking, what on
Earth are they talking about?

01:24:06.070 --> 01:24:07.160
My mother's a librarian.

01:24:07.160 --> 01:24:09.919
And so she was, you
know, sending me things.

01:24:09.919 --> 01:24:11.960
This was when we had an
office in Port-au-Prince,

01:24:11.960 --> 01:24:14.043
before we had to close it
down during those years,

01:24:14.043 --> 01:24:16.070
because of death threats
and intimidation.

01:24:16.070 --> 01:24:18.140
We finally just closed
up our operation

01:24:18.140 --> 01:24:20.240
in Port-au-Prince completely.

01:24:20.240 --> 01:24:22.380
But she was sending
me these things.

01:24:22.380 --> 01:24:24.630
And I'd say, this is just
not at all what's happening.

01:24:24.630 --> 01:24:27.600
But the image that comes
to mind is, you know,

01:24:27.600 --> 01:24:31.360
we were able to detect refugees
in boats from, you know,

01:24:31.360 --> 01:24:33.710
miles away with radar.

01:24:33.710 --> 01:24:37.820
But an oil tanker steaming
from Texas to Port-au-Prince

01:24:37.820 --> 01:24:39.350
didn't show up on the radar.

01:24:39.350 --> 01:24:40.850
So, I mean, granted
they're small

01:24:40.850 --> 01:24:44.000
boats and all, those tankers.

01:24:44.000 --> 01:24:45.500
So fine-tuning-- I
remember that was

01:24:45.500 --> 01:24:47.124
Barbara Crossette,
wasn't it, who wrote

01:24:47.124 --> 01:24:48.360
that fine-tuning the embargo?

01:24:48.360 --> 01:24:50.271
That was The New York Times.

01:24:50.271 --> 01:24:52.520
She probably won a million
awards, Pulitzer and things

01:24:52.520 --> 01:24:54.230
like that.

01:24:54.230 --> 01:24:57.800
And Noam has already
covered the fake embargo.

01:24:57.800 --> 01:25:01.100
So I'm going to go ahead and
turn to the real embargo.

01:25:01.100 --> 01:25:05.000
Now, here's where we end
up after, as Nancy said,

01:25:05.000 --> 01:25:06.690
use the term impoverishment.

01:25:06.690 --> 01:25:08.675
Because it implies a
historical process.

01:25:08.675 --> 01:25:11.420
It takes a long time
to impoverish a place.

01:25:11.420 --> 01:25:14.610
But here's where we end
up, you know, with, again,

01:25:14.610 --> 01:25:17.180
the most impoverished
country in the hemisphere,

01:25:17.180 --> 01:25:18.950
which, of course,
only coincidentally

01:25:18.950 --> 01:25:20.750
is the country most
linked to the United

01:25:20.750 --> 01:25:22.400
States for the longest time.

01:25:22.400 --> 01:25:26.570
I mean, that's surely just
a erroneous association.

01:25:26.570 --> 01:25:31.130
And the part that I see
and concerns me just

01:25:31.130 --> 01:25:33.260
in terms of everyday
practice as a physician

01:25:33.260 --> 01:25:36.350
is that the impact of this on
health is, of course, profound.

01:25:36.350 --> 01:25:40.850
And so most of the patients
we see have the diseases

01:25:40.850 --> 01:25:46.100
that poverty engenders
and sustains.

01:25:46.100 --> 01:25:48.920
And they can be prevented and
treated at the end, you know,

01:25:48.920 --> 01:25:50.986
distally as we say in medicine.

01:25:50.986 --> 01:25:51.860
And that's what I do.

01:25:51.860 --> 01:25:52.360
I'm sorry.

01:25:52.360 --> 01:25:54.650
I mean, it's kind of
what I was trained

01:25:54.650 --> 01:25:58.430
to do is to sort of get
people when it's too late.

01:25:58.430 --> 01:26:02.670
But the real responses to
this will be, of course,

01:26:02.670 --> 01:26:06.230
determined, in my view,
outside of rural Haiti.

01:26:06.230 --> 01:26:07.530
This is the receiving end.

01:26:07.530 --> 01:26:10.826
This is not the determining end.

01:26:10.826 --> 01:26:12.200
I mean, the numbers
are terrible.

01:26:12.200 --> 01:26:14.241
Well, here, let me just
give you this one example

01:26:14.241 --> 01:26:15.810
to close my comments.

01:26:15.810 --> 01:26:20.450
So what would a public health
infrastructure do in Haiti?

01:26:20.450 --> 01:26:23.960
What would a government do if,
instead of being interested in,

01:26:23.960 --> 01:26:25.490
you know, imprisoning
and arresting,

01:26:25.490 --> 01:26:28.070
what if it really wanted
to promote health?

01:26:28.070 --> 01:26:29.957
Well, it would have
to have capital.

01:26:29.957 --> 01:26:31.040
They'd have to have funds.

01:26:31.040 --> 01:26:34.940
You can't do public
health with no money.

01:26:34.940 --> 01:26:36.440
You know, if you
knew the money that

01:26:36.440 --> 01:26:39.290
gets spent in Massachusetts
on public health,

01:26:39.290 --> 01:26:41.450
I was astounded when
I saw the numbers.

01:26:41.450 --> 01:26:44.640
It's something like more
of all China and India

01:26:44.640 --> 01:26:47.812
in just our little tiny
state of 6 million people.

01:26:47.812 --> 01:26:48.650
And it's good.

01:26:48.650 --> 01:26:49.670
Hey, we like this.

01:26:49.670 --> 01:26:52.262
You want to, you know, have
huge investment in things,

01:26:52.262 --> 01:26:53.720
why not choose
public health rather

01:26:53.720 --> 01:26:55.594
than, you know, bombers
and things like that?

01:26:55.594 --> 01:26:56.610
I'm all for it.

01:26:56.610 --> 01:26:57.740
Sign me up.

01:26:57.740 --> 01:26:59.810
However, you can't
do it with no money.

01:26:59.810 --> 01:27:00.860
You have to have money.

01:27:00.860 --> 01:27:03.530
So, not coincidentally,
Haiti, of course,

01:27:03.530 --> 01:27:05.390
was completely broke
just like it was

01:27:05.390 --> 01:27:07.460
at the end of the revolution.

01:27:07.460 --> 01:27:09.890
Everything had been
burned down and destroyed.

01:27:09.890 --> 01:27:11.630
In fact, it may be
worse now than then,

01:27:11.630 --> 01:27:14.090
because some parts of
Haiti were not burned down.

01:27:14.090 --> 01:27:16.860
And the soil was still fertile.

01:27:16.860 --> 01:27:19.850
And deforestation had not
done what has done now.

01:27:19.850 --> 01:27:22.465
But a government, should
it want to do this,

01:27:22.465 --> 01:27:23.840
would have the
Ministry of Health

01:27:23.840 --> 01:27:27.200
put together with
consultation, which they did--

01:27:29.900 --> 01:27:32.750
I don't like this word
health reform, okay?

01:27:32.750 --> 01:27:37.460
But reorganization was
an inoffensive one.

01:27:37.460 --> 01:27:39.260
I had nothing to do with this.

01:27:39.260 --> 01:27:40.730
I went and found this.

01:27:40.730 --> 01:27:42.452
It's a matter of public record.

01:27:42.452 --> 01:27:44.660
It was very easy to get from
the Haitian authorities,

01:27:44.660 --> 01:27:45.830
by the way, and
a little bit more

01:27:45.830 --> 01:27:48.288
difficult to get out of the
Inter-American Development Bank

01:27:48.288 --> 01:27:49.220
authorities.

01:27:49.220 --> 01:27:52.700
But on July 21, 1998,
the Haitian government

01:27:52.700 --> 01:27:55.160
and the Inter-American
Development Bank,

01:27:55.160 --> 01:27:59.270
which is a very important
institution in Latin America.

01:28:01.910 --> 01:28:04.220
With a couple of
notable exceptions,

01:28:04.220 --> 01:28:07.580
all of these
officialdom countries

01:28:07.580 --> 01:28:10.190
go through these mechanisms.

01:28:10.190 --> 01:28:14.060
And they signed a loan
for $22.5 million,

01:28:14.060 --> 01:28:17.150
which is a huge amount for the
Haitian Ministry of Health.

01:28:17.150 --> 01:28:20.450
By the way, in that
anthrax outbreak--

01:28:20.450 --> 01:28:21.950
I think it was the
anthrax outbreak.

01:28:21.950 --> 01:28:24.380
It may have been an outbreak
of meningococcal meningitis.

01:28:24.380 --> 01:28:26.570
I sent an email
from central Haiti

01:28:26.570 --> 01:28:29.341
to a friend of mine who was with
the Ministry of Health saying,

01:28:29.341 --> 01:28:29.840
you know--

01:28:29.840 --> 01:28:31.550
it was meningococcal meningitis.

01:28:31.550 --> 01:28:35.990
I said, we're having an
outbreak of meningococcemia,

01:28:35.990 --> 01:28:36.920
meningococcal disease.

01:28:36.920 --> 01:28:38.150
And a baby had just died.

01:28:38.150 --> 01:28:42.320
And we saw another case
with purpura fulminans

01:28:42.320 --> 01:28:44.650
and then saw another
case the next day.

01:28:44.650 --> 01:28:45.734
So we alerted them.

01:28:45.734 --> 01:28:47.900
And I got no answer back,
because they were supposed

01:28:47.900 --> 01:28:49.160
to come out and investigate.

01:28:49.160 --> 01:28:50.670
And that's what they
had asked us to do.

01:28:50.670 --> 01:28:52.340
And a couple of months
later, I saw the guy

01:28:52.340 --> 01:28:54.173
who was, like I said,
a friend of mine who's

01:28:54.173 --> 01:28:56.750
a very earnest, good person.

01:28:56.750 --> 01:28:59.810
And I said, why didn't you
come out to investigate this?

01:28:59.810 --> 01:29:03.364
And he said, we didn't
have any gas for the Jeep.

01:29:03.364 --> 01:29:05.780
And I said, you know, that was
the end of that discussion.

01:29:05.780 --> 01:29:07.279
Because I thought,
you know, I'm not

01:29:07.279 --> 01:29:09.240
going to be rebuking
people for not having gas.

01:29:09.240 --> 01:29:11.115
It's like, that's sort
of our foreign policy,

01:29:11.115 --> 01:29:12.590
yell at people for being poor.

01:29:12.590 --> 01:29:15.770
So, now, let's yell at
the Haitian government

01:29:15.770 --> 01:29:17.215
for being poor.

01:29:17.215 --> 01:29:18.590
We do that very
well, by the way,

01:29:18.590 --> 01:29:23.667
with very little in the way
of critical response from, you

01:29:23.667 --> 01:29:26.814
know, the mandarins of
American foreign policy,

01:29:26.814 --> 01:29:27.980
including the intellectuals.

01:29:27.980 --> 01:29:28.813
Again, I don't know.

01:29:28.813 --> 01:29:30.470
You follow this more
closely than I do.

01:29:30.470 --> 01:29:32.870
I haven't seen a
lot of commentary

01:29:32.870 --> 01:29:35.630
on what it is exactly we're
doing, but it looks a lot to me

01:29:35.630 --> 01:29:38.120
like yelling at
the poor as really

01:29:38.120 --> 01:29:40.690
powerful, wealthy people--

01:29:40.690 --> 01:29:41.750
nation, rather.

01:29:41.750 --> 01:29:43.850
So this was the
goal of the loan.

01:29:43.850 --> 01:29:46.340
And, you know, it's pretty
standard fare, really quite

01:29:46.340 --> 01:29:47.390
dull to read, actually.

01:29:47.390 --> 01:29:51.440
I can't believe I had to
have help from my co-workers

01:29:51.440 --> 01:29:52.310
getting through it.

01:29:52.310 --> 01:29:54.504
It was boring.

01:29:54.504 --> 01:29:55.670
These are the project goals.

01:29:55.670 --> 01:29:57.310
I'm just summarizing
them for you.

01:29:57.310 --> 01:29:59.810
But, basically, they were very
standard public health goals,

01:29:59.810 --> 01:30:01.717
reduce infant mortality.

01:30:01.717 --> 01:30:03.050
And, you know, there was a plan.

01:30:03.050 --> 01:30:06.110
And it was approved by
the Haitian government.

01:30:06.110 --> 01:30:08.780
And it got stalled
in parliament,

01:30:08.780 --> 01:30:11.600
because there was a famously
obstructionist parliament.

01:30:11.600 --> 01:30:14.810
By the way, that's why the
Haitians voted in March.

01:30:14.810 --> 01:30:17.660
These were the famous
disputed elections.

01:30:17.660 --> 01:30:20.780
In March of 2000-- nothing to do
with the presidential elections

01:30:20.780 --> 01:30:22.400
that would happen
later on that year--

01:30:22.400 --> 01:30:26.150
the Haitians went out and
tried to do clean slate for one

01:30:26.150 --> 01:30:28.940
party, so they could get rid of
the obstructionist parliament.

01:30:28.940 --> 01:30:29.680
And they did.

01:30:29.680 --> 01:30:31.221
You know, everyone
went out and voted

01:30:31.221 --> 01:30:34.850
for the same party in order
to have the obstructionist

01:30:34.850 --> 01:30:37.385
parliament not block--

01:30:37.385 --> 01:30:41.190
it's sort of Haitian
gridlock of blocking every--

01:30:41.190 --> 01:30:44.330
and I would say this is,
by any kind of standard,

01:30:44.330 --> 01:30:46.370
a reasonable proposal
and, you know,

01:30:46.370 --> 01:30:50.400
what you'd want a public
health project to look like.

01:30:50.400 --> 01:30:53.010
So in any case,
then what happened?

01:30:53.010 --> 01:30:55.940
Well, in October 1998, the
Haitian Minister of Health

01:30:55.940 --> 01:30:57.920
presented the project
to the legislature.

01:30:57.920 --> 01:30:59.540
And I just wrote
obstructionists.

01:30:59.540 --> 01:31:00.260
Because that's what
they were doing.

01:31:00.260 --> 01:31:02.990
They were basically trying
to block everything that came

01:31:02.990 --> 01:31:04.924
from the central government.

01:31:04.924 --> 01:31:06.590
This was not under
Aristide, by the way.

01:31:06.590 --> 01:31:09.050
This is under Préval.

01:31:09.050 --> 01:31:13.490
And then after the
March elections

01:31:13.490 --> 01:31:16.490
had led to a non-obstructionist
parliament, that

01:31:16.490 --> 01:31:21.290
is a parliament that would
vote to approve proposals

01:31:21.290 --> 01:31:25.170
from the central government,
it was ratified there.

01:31:25.170 --> 01:31:28.040
The Inter-American
Development Bank said, okay,

01:31:28.040 --> 01:31:29.360
money's on the way.

01:31:29.360 --> 01:31:31.970
This is announced in
the papers as official.

01:31:31.970 --> 01:31:35.210
And then there were
new conditions put on.

01:31:35.210 --> 01:31:37.970
And this is when I started
hearing about this.

01:31:37.970 --> 01:31:40.130
You know, if you're sitting
there in rural Haiti

01:31:40.130 --> 01:31:43.910
and your number of patients
is increased by two-fold in 18

01:31:43.910 --> 01:31:45.080
months--

01:31:45.080 --> 01:31:47.630
and you have seen various
clinics and hospitals

01:31:47.630 --> 01:31:49.670
close down in the region.

01:31:49.670 --> 01:31:51.560
And, you know, your
doctors and nurses

01:31:51.560 --> 01:31:54.200
are saying, we can't
see all these patients

01:31:54.200 --> 01:31:58.084
and it's just not
possible, if there's

01:31:58.084 --> 01:31:59.750
a medical staff of
eight or nine people,

01:31:59.750 --> 01:32:02.730
to see 400, 500
patients in a day.

01:32:02.730 --> 01:32:05.720
And so I'm the medical
director, so they're

01:32:05.720 --> 01:32:07.040
coming to me with complaints.

01:32:07.040 --> 01:32:08.790
They say, we can't see
all these patients.

01:32:08.790 --> 01:32:09.940
And I say, what can I do?

01:32:09.940 --> 01:32:11.315
These are all
Haitian colleagues.

01:32:11.315 --> 01:32:13.670
I say, you know,
the country is sick.

01:32:13.670 --> 01:32:14.980
We have to see the patients.

01:32:14.980 --> 01:32:17.950
But I'm just waiting
for this IDB thing

01:32:17.950 --> 01:32:21.730
to go through, so that the other
institutions can be reopened,

01:32:21.730 --> 01:32:24.310
as is the stated intention of
the Haitian Ministry of Health.

01:32:24.310 --> 01:32:25.120
So I'm waiting.

01:32:25.120 --> 01:32:25.990
So I started asking.

01:32:25.990 --> 01:32:29.830
Where's that loan that would
allow the other public health--

01:32:29.830 --> 01:32:33.040
because this is a
private charity hospital.

01:32:33.040 --> 01:32:37.810
That is a private hospital doing
the work of the public health

01:32:37.810 --> 01:32:38.694
infrastructure.

01:32:38.694 --> 01:32:39.610
So we're just waiting.

01:32:39.610 --> 01:32:40.910
And we started looking into it.

01:32:40.910 --> 01:32:44.090
And we found out, we
started following it.

01:32:44.090 --> 01:32:46.780
And the most amazing
thing I heard

01:32:46.780 --> 01:32:49.810
was last summer when I found
that the Haitian government was

01:32:49.810 --> 01:32:51.700
actually paying
credit commissions

01:32:51.700 --> 01:32:54.400
and interest on a loan that
had not been dispersed.

01:32:54.400 --> 01:32:57.800
And I said, you know, I'm
not an economist or a banker.

01:32:57.800 --> 01:32:58.960
You know, I'm sorry.

01:32:58.960 --> 01:33:00.910
I'm just a physician
anthropologist.

01:33:00.910 --> 01:33:02.840
But isn't that illegal?

01:33:02.840 --> 01:33:05.410
You know, can you do that?

01:33:05.410 --> 01:33:07.300
And this is last year.

01:33:07.300 --> 01:33:08.631
This is still going on today.

01:33:08.631 --> 01:33:09.130
Yeah.

01:33:09.130 --> 01:33:10.671
I don't care if my
30 minutes are up.

01:33:10.671 --> 01:33:12.280
I'm just getting
warmed up, all right?

01:33:12.280 --> 01:33:14.470
And they were having
their arms twisted.

01:33:14.470 --> 01:33:17.775
And I'm telling you,
from what I understand--

01:33:17.775 --> 01:33:19.900
and I know this to be true--
the Ministry of Health

01:33:19.900 --> 01:33:21.970
has no money.

01:33:21.970 --> 01:33:25.420
Let me just give you
another little ironic thing.

01:33:25.420 --> 01:33:28.690
The size of the Brigham and
Women's Hospital budget, okay,

01:33:28.690 --> 01:33:31.510
where I work and some
of you have been there,

01:33:31.510 --> 01:33:33.737
is larger than the
Republic of Haiti.

01:33:33.737 --> 01:33:35.320
And you read in the
newspaper a couple

01:33:35.320 --> 01:33:38.440
of days that Brigham and Women's
Hospital has $163 million

01:33:38.440 --> 01:33:40.810
a year in support
just from the INH.

01:33:40.810 --> 01:33:43.600
The INH is isoniazid, sorry.

01:33:43.600 --> 01:33:44.362
NIH.

01:33:44.362 --> 01:33:45.070
DORSINVILLE: NIH.

01:33:45.070 --> 01:33:47.403
FARMER: INH is a drug to treat
tuberculosis, forgive me.

01:33:47.403 --> 01:33:48.780
[LAUGHTER]

01:33:48.780 --> 01:33:49.702
Did I really do that?

01:33:49.702 --> 01:33:50.410
DORSINVILLE: Yes.

01:33:50.410 --> 01:33:51.035
FARMER: Anyway.

01:33:51.035 --> 01:33:52.450
AUDIENCE: On tape.

01:33:52.450 --> 01:33:54.670
FARMER: [SPEAKING FRENCH].

01:33:54.670 --> 01:33:56.950
Anyway, so this is
what we're left with.

01:33:56.950 --> 01:33:58.840
And this is what I'm
going to close with.

01:33:58.840 --> 01:34:01.229
There they are.

01:34:01.229 --> 01:34:02.770
You know, people
can say, okay, well,

01:34:02.770 --> 01:34:04.660
what are you doing getting
involved in this stuff?

01:34:04.660 --> 01:34:06.118
Or, as I heard
today during rounds,

01:34:06.118 --> 01:34:09.130
why are you giving a
talk with Noam Chomsky?

01:34:09.130 --> 01:34:10.580
That's true, isn't it?

01:34:10.580 --> 01:34:12.770
And he didn't say it.

01:34:12.770 --> 01:34:14.530
But, well, here's why.

01:34:14.530 --> 01:34:18.520
Because we're sick of
seeing sick patients who

01:34:18.520 --> 01:34:21.010
don't want to be sick
who've already, you know,

01:34:21.010 --> 01:34:22.690
worked so hard.

01:34:22.690 --> 01:34:26.350
And, again, I take the
view that Nancy does.

01:34:26.350 --> 01:34:28.720
They worked hard
for about 210 years

01:34:28.720 --> 01:34:33.740
by my count to have a
society in which everyone,

01:34:33.740 --> 01:34:35.810
including poor people
and, back 200 years ago,

01:34:35.810 --> 01:34:39.730
including slaves, had a say in
what happens to their destiny.

01:34:39.730 --> 01:34:42.400
That seems to me
the clear message

01:34:42.400 --> 01:34:45.250
of all the Haitian popular
movements from the 18th century

01:34:45.250 --> 01:34:47.930
to now is that the
Haitian people are saying,

01:34:47.930 --> 01:34:52.600
we don't want to be left out
of, you know, true democracy.

01:34:52.600 --> 01:34:54.730
We want to have a say in this.

01:34:54.730 --> 01:34:56.410
And to see these
same people, who

01:34:56.410 --> 01:35:00.970
I regard as extremely valiant,
be reduced to sitting outside

01:35:00.970 --> 01:35:03.730
of our clinic and going there
the night before and sleeping

01:35:03.730 --> 01:35:07.930
on the ground to see the
physicians, you know,

01:35:07.930 --> 01:35:09.910
it's more than disheartening
for those of us

01:35:09.910 --> 01:35:11.140
who are working there.

01:35:11.140 --> 01:35:13.060
And, of course,
we know very well

01:35:13.060 --> 01:35:16.480
that the source
of these illnesses

01:35:16.480 --> 01:35:17.660
are social conditions.

01:35:17.660 --> 01:35:20.920
And we know, because we've
heard now twice I hope,

01:35:20.920 --> 01:35:24.010
what causes the
social conditions

01:35:24.010 --> 01:35:25.660
that we see now in Haiti.

01:35:25.660 --> 01:35:29.680
And when I speak to friends
and family and co-workers and I

01:35:29.680 --> 01:35:31.570
tell them this story about--

01:35:31.570 --> 01:35:34.330
you know, the only two
countries, by the way--

01:35:34.330 --> 01:35:36.732
this is after Durban, the
meeting-- not the Durban AIDS,

01:35:36.732 --> 01:35:38.440
meeting which I did
go to, but the Durban

01:35:38.440 --> 01:35:40.750
meeting that Nancy
went to on racism.

01:35:40.750 --> 01:35:43.930
The declaration of Durban,
which many of you may have read,

01:35:43.930 --> 01:35:47.020
has very pointed
language on reparations

01:35:47.020 --> 01:35:49.480
owed to post-colonial societies.

01:35:49.480 --> 01:35:53.620
And the two major
destinations for slaves

01:35:53.620 --> 01:35:55.990
kidnapped from West
Africa in the New World

01:35:55.990 --> 01:35:57.010
were Cuba and Haiti.

01:35:57.010 --> 01:35:59.410
And those the only two
countries under formal aid

01:35:59.410 --> 01:36:01.960
embargoes by the United States.

01:36:01.960 --> 01:36:06.700
So that's, right now, how we're
paying reparations to Haiti.

01:36:06.700 --> 01:36:08.191
Thank you very much.

01:36:08.191 --> 01:36:10.676
[APPLAUSE]

01:36:43.540 --> 01:36:49.096
DORSINVILLE: You
heard it, reparations.

01:36:54.080 --> 01:37:01.090
I think from both Professor
Chomsky and Dr. Farmer,

01:37:01.090 --> 01:37:06.990
we have a sense of
the sustainability

01:37:06.990 --> 01:37:10.510
and the resilience
of Haitian people

01:37:10.510 --> 01:37:14.980
in their determination
to have a democracy,

01:37:14.980 --> 01:37:18.760
to be part of the
governance of the island,

01:37:18.760 --> 01:37:24.040
and to be part of the process
of self-determination.

01:37:24.040 --> 01:37:27.700
Over and over, we've
challenged, have

01:37:27.700 --> 01:37:34.210
been challenged, interference
coming from a number of places.

01:37:34.210 --> 01:37:41.320
But we've steadfastly kept
on our desire for democracy

01:37:41.320 --> 01:37:43.960
and for autonomy.

01:37:43.960 --> 01:37:49.030
I think after hearing these
two prominent scholars,

01:37:49.030 --> 01:37:54.400
I'd like to give a chance to
you guys, the emerging scholars,

01:37:54.400 --> 01:38:00.790
to air your thoughts and ask
questions and, perhaps, offer

01:38:00.790 --> 01:38:08.800
some guidance to the scholarly
and powerful people who

01:38:08.800 --> 01:38:12.730
have interfered with the
sovereignty of the Haitian

01:38:12.730 --> 01:38:14.960
people.

01:38:14.960 --> 01:38:15.813
Go ahead.

01:38:15.813 --> 01:38:17.854
AUDIENCE: You never
mentioned the United Nations.

01:38:17.854 --> 01:38:20.240
I was wondering what their role
is, what their position is.

01:38:20.240 --> 01:38:22.070
DORSINVILLE: Do you want
to take a microphone?

01:38:22.070 --> 01:38:23.403
Did everybody hear the question?

01:38:23.403 --> 01:38:26.409
AUDIENCE: You could repeat it.

01:38:26.409 --> 01:38:28.950
FARMER: The first question was
what about the United Nations.

01:38:28.950 --> 01:38:29.910
I never mentioned them.

01:38:29.910 --> 01:38:32.661
What is the United
Nations again?

01:38:32.661 --> 01:38:33.160
Oh.

01:38:33.160 --> 01:38:35.530
Oh, the thing in New York.

01:38:35.530 --> 01:38:37.190
[LAUGHTER]

01:38:37.190 --> 01:38:38.190
I'll let Noam take this.

01:38:38.190 --> 01:38:42.090
But I just want to say one
thing before I pass it on to him

01:38:42.090 --> 01:38:45.490
is, having lived in
Haiti for 20 years,

01:38:45.490 --> 01:38:48.990
I have developed
a deep suspicion

01:38:48.990 --> 01:38:53.949
of the international
bilateral groups like that.

01:38:53.949 --> 01:38:55.740
I mean, the Organization
of American States

01:38:55.740 --> 01:38:57.870
has been quite craven.

01:38:57.870 --> 01:39:01.320
And it certainly will not
countenance any United States

01:39:01.320 --> 01:39:02.400
dictum.

01:39:02.400 --> 01:39:06.930
And so that the degree to
which anybody can really

01:39:06.930 --> 01:39:11.050
challenge what are
profoundly unjust policies

01:39:11.050 --> 01:39:13.740
any body like that, you know,
I don't know much about it.

01:39:13.740 --> 01:39:17.610
But I can tell you that they
have been pretty cowardly

01:39:17.610 --> 01:39:18.570
on many occasions.

01:39:18.570 --> 01:39:21.300
Now, the governments
are not in a position

01:39:21.300 --> 01:39:22.710
to say things like that.

01:39:22.710 --> 01:39:24.480
That's not the
language that they use,

01:39:24.480 --> 01:39:26.611
you know, regardless of
what stripe of government.

01:39:26.611 --> 01:39:27.610
But that's the language.

01:39:27.610 --> 01:39:29.276
I mean, I have the
latitude to use that.

01:39:29.276 --> 01:39:34.050
And I've found them cowardly
and unwilling to speak clearly

01:39:34.050 --> 01:39:35.745
about what they see going on.

01:39:35.745 --> 01:39:38.130
You know, Pan American
Health Organization

01:39:38.130 --> 01:39:41.550
is an apolitical
organization that's

01:39:41.550 --> 01:39:45.390
100 years old, twice as old as
the World Health Organization.

01:39:45.390 --> 01:39:47.940
And when the head of the Pan
American Health Organization,

01:39:47.940 --> 01:39:51.360
Sir George Alleyne, went
to Haiti recently, he said,

01:39:51.360 --> 01:39:52.390
this is nuts.

01:39:52.390 --> 01:39:54.590
I mean, since we're
on radio, I suppose

01:39:54.590 --> 01:39:56.510
I should say that's
a paraphrasing.

01:39:56.510 --> 01:39:58.950
You know, Sir George probably
didn't say this is nuts.

01:39:58.950 --> 01:40:01.710
But my friends in
international public health

01:40:01.710 --> 01:40:04.950
say this is really
awful, an embargo

01:40:04.950 --> 01:40:09.060
against the country that is
least able to really resist

01:40:09.060 --> 01:40:12.210
that makes no sense.

01:40:12.210 --> 01:40:16.560
And yet I think international
politicians and diplomats are

01:40:16.560 --> 01:40:19.950
really now getting very divorced
from clear and straightforward

01:40:19.950 --> 01:40:22.920
language that says, this
is right, this is wrong.

01:40:22.920 --> 01:40:24.300
So what about the UN?

01:40:24.300 --> 01:40:29.122
I guess my big question is when
the United Nations contradicts

01:40:29.122 --> 01:40:30.455
US foreign policy, what happens?

01:40:30.455 --> 01:40:34.760
And I think we have, you know,
a lot of information about that.

01:40:34.760 --> 01:40:36.990
[LAUGHTER]

01:40:36.990 --> 01:40:39.330
I'm sure this will
be very cheerful.

01:40:39.330 --> 01:40:41.010
[LAUGHTER]

01:40:41.010 --> 01:40:41.740
CHOMSKY: Well.

01:40:41.740 --> 01:40:43.265
[LAUGHTER]

01:40:43.265 --> 01:40:45.452
There's a history,
and it's very clear.

01:40:45.452 --> 01:40:46.410
In fact, it's official.

01:40:46.410 --> 01:40:48.570
I'll just quote an
official source, okay?

01:40:48.570 --> 01:40:50.680
So I don't have to make it up.

01:40:50.680 --> 01:40:52.186
[LAUGHTER]

01:40:52.186 --> 01:40:57.600
I mentioned that in 1986
the World Court, highest

01:40:57.600 --> 01:41:00.120
judicial body, condemned
the United States

01:41:00.120 --> 01:41:03.930
for international terrorism
for the unlawful use of force,

01:41:03.930 --> 01:41:06.960
ordered it to terminate
its atrocities,

01:41:06.960 --> 01:41:11.290
and to pay substantial
reparations to the victim,

01:41:11.290 --> 01:41:13.260
namely Nicaragua.

01:41:13.260 --> 01:41:18.780
The US responded by
immediately escalating the war,

01:41:18.780 --> 01:41:21.500
bipartisan incidentally.

01:41:21.500 --> 01:41:23.980
The judgment was
dismissed with contempt.

01:41:23.980 --> 01:41:26.100
It's disappeared from history.

01:41:26.100 --> 01:41:28.680
Nicaragua went to
the Security Council,

01:41:28.680 --> 01:41:32.040
which debated a resolution not
mentioning anyone, but calling

01:41:32.040 --> 01:41:34.830
on all states to observe
international law.

01:41:34.830 --> 01:41:36.960
It was vetoed by
the United States.

01:41:36.960 --> 01:41:42.030
Britain, which is our attack
dog, not a country anymore,

01:41:42.030 --> 01:41:42.660
agreed.

01:41:42.660 --> 01:41:47.090
They helped out by abstaining.

01:41:47.090 --> 01:41:49.170
They went to the General
Assembly, same result,

01:41:49.170 --> 01:41:49.950
you know.

01:41:49.950 --> 01:41:51.690
It's out of history,
doesn't exist.

01:41:51.690 --> 01:41:54.990
I mean, just how
many reams of paper

01:41:54.990 --> 01:41:57.750
have been devoted to
the war on terrorism

01:41:57.750 --> 01:41:59.620
in the last several months?

01:41:59.620 --> 01:42:02.370
Try to find one
reference to the fact

01:42:02.370 --> 01:42:04.770
that the leader of
the war on terrorism

01:42:04.770 --> 01:42:06.840
happens to be the only
state in the world, which

01:42:06.840 --> 01:42:08.460
has been condemned
by the highest

01:42:08.460 --> 01:42:11.250
international authorities
for international terrorism

01:42:11.250 --> 01:42:14.220
and responded by
escalating the terror.

01:42:14.220 --> 01:42:17.100
Well, you know,
the legal advisor

01:42:17.100 --> 01:42:20.717
of the State Department,
Abraham Sofaer, explained--

01:42:20.717 --> 01:42:22.800
you can read it in the
State Department Bulletin--

01:42:22.800 --> 01:42:26.485
why the US was rejecting the
World Court jurisdiction.

01:42:26.485 --> 01:42:28.110
They didn't even wait
for the judgment,

01:42:28.110 --> 01:42:29.859
because they knew what
it was going to be.

01:42:29.859 --> 01:42:32.930
They rejected the jurisdiction
when the case was brought.

01:42:32.930 --> 01:42:35.160
And he explained,
and he's right.

01:42:35.160 --> 01:42:38.820
He said, in the early days
of the United Nations,

01:42:38.820 --> 01:42:41.250
everybody voted our way.

01:42:41.250 --> 01:42:43.350
He didn't explain
why, but it's obvious.

01:42:43.350 --> 01:42:46.560
You take a look at what the
world was like in the late '40s

01:42:46.560 --> 01:42:47.520
and the early '50s.

01:42:47.520 --> 01:42:50.870
And if you didn't vote the
way the United States said,

01:42:50.870 --> 01:42:53.410
you were in very deep trouble.

01:42:53.410 --> 01:42:56.220
So everybody voted our
way, and it was nice.

01:42:56.220 --> 01:42:59.250
Then came
decolonization, you know.

01:42:59.250 --> 01:43:02.220
And the membership
of the United Nations

01:43:02.220 --> 01:43:04.620
became much more diversified.

01:43:04.620 --> 01:43:08.170
It fell under what's called
the tyranny of the majority,

01:43:08.170 --> 01:43:09.870
otherwise known as democracy.

01:43:12.840 --> 01:43:15.120
You know, even the
industrial world

01:43:15.120 --> 01:43:16.710
began to recover from the war.

01:43:16.710 --> 01:43:19.230
And the world has
gotten more diverse.

01:43:19.230 --> 01:43:22.700
And what he said is, by now, we
can no longer count on other--

01:43:22.700 --> 01:43:25.490
he said, other nations of the
world just don't agree with us

01:43:25.490 --> 01:43:28.910
and often vote against
us in the United Nations

01:43:28.910 --> 01:43:30.860
or anywhere else,
the World Court.

01:43:30.860 --> 01:43:33.830
Therefore, we must
reserve to ourselves

01:43:33.830 --> 01:43:35.660
the right to
determine what falls

01:43:35.660 --> 01:43:38.840
within the domestic jurisdiction
of the United States.

01:43:38.840 --> 01:43:41.840
In this case, what fell within
the domestic jurisdiction

01:43:41.840 --> 01:43:46.580
of the United States was a
terrorist attack on Nicaragua.

01:43:46.580 --> 01:43:47.930
And that's correct.

01:43:47.930 --> 01:43:52.140
Now, nobody would be so impolite
as to quote this in public.

01:43:52.140 --> 01:43:55.470
It's been on the public record
for, you know, 17 years.

01:43:55.470 --> 01:43:59.130
You can judge how often it's
been quoted, but he's correct.

01:43:59.130 --> 01:44:02.960
In other words, if you
do what we say, fine.

01:44:02.960 --> 01:44:06.080
Then we'd like the
international institutions.

01:44:06.080 --> 01:44:10.580
If you happen to cross
us, you know, get lost.

01:44:10.580 --> 01:44:13.710
We have the guns, and
we'll do what we feel like.

01:44:13.710 --> 01:44:17.360
In fact, when you read
about the United Nations,

01:44:17.360 --> 01:44:20.900
what you hear is, you know, the
tyranny of the Russian veto.

01:44:20.900 --> 01:44:22.355
Nothing can ever happen.

01:44:22.355 --> 01:44:24.230
You know, that was true
for a couple of years

01:44:24.230 --> 01:44:26.240
in the late '40s
and the early '50s

01:44:26.240 --> 01:44:29.540
when it was totally under the
lash of the United States,

01:44:29.540 --> 01:44:32.176
as The Wall Street Journal
put it at the time.

01:44:32.176 --> 01:44:33.800
Yeah, then it was
used as an instrument

01:44:33.800 --> 01:44:34.675
against the Russians.

01:44:34.675 --> 01:44:38.390
And they voted,
you know, against.

01:44:38.390 --> 01:44:40.130
They vetoed resolutions.

01:44:40.130 --> 01:44:43.960
In fact, I was a student in
those days, graduate student,

01:44:43.960 --> 01:44:45.867
in your profession.

01:44:45.867 --> 01:44:47.450
You mind if I dump
on your profession?

01:44:47.450 --> 01:44:48.366
FARMER: No, please do.

01:44:48.366 --> 01:44:50.240
CHOMSKY: Leading
anthropologists, you know,

01:44:50.240 --> 01:44:52.020
famous anthropologists--

01:44:52.020 --> 01:44:53.525
I mean, Margaret
Mead, those guys.

01:44:53.525 --> 01:44:54.650
FARMER: Knock yourself out.

01:44:54.650 --> 01:44:57.350
[LAUGHTER]

01:44:57.350 --> 01:44:58.997
CHOMSKY: They worked
on this question

01:44:58.997 --> 01:45:00.830
of why the Russians
were always so negative.

01:45:00.830 --> 01:45:03.650
You know, they kept saying
no at the United Nations.

01:45:03.650 --> 01:45:05.750
And they developed
a theory, which

01:45:05.750 --> 01:45:07.130
was taken quite seriously.

01:45:07.130 --> 01:45:09.470
I mean, there may be
three people in Cambridge

01:45:09.470 --> 01:45:11.120
who thought it was a joke.

01:45:11.120 --> 01:45:13.250
We used to call it diaperology.

01:45:13.250 --> 01:45:15.320
The theory was that
the Russians were

01:45:15.320 --> 01:45:17.652
raised with swaddling clothes.

01:45:17.652 --> 01:45:18.860
And that makes them negative.

01:45:18.860 --> 01:45:20.070
[LAUGHTER]

01:45:20.070 --> 01:45:22.700
So then when Gromyko is
up at the United Nations,

01:45:22.700 --> 01:45:25.270
he says, no.

01:45:25.270 --> 01:45:28.280
And that was taken very
seriously if you read it.

01:45:28.280 --> 01:45:30.710
Okay, well, and that's what
it was like in the late '40s

01:45:30.710 --> 01:45:31.590
and the early '50s.

01:45:31.590 --> 01:45:33.990
Then, as I say, came
decolonization, you know,

01:45:33.990 --> 01:45:35.150
all these changes.

01:45:35.150 --> 01:45:38.030
Since the 1960s, the
US is far in the lead

01:45:38.030 --> 01:45:40.490
in vetoing Security Council
resolution on just about

01:45:40.490 --> 01:45:42.590
everything, every topic.

01:45:42.590 --> 01:45:44.240
Second is Britain.

01:45:44.240 --> 01:45:47.030
A very distant third is France.

01:45:47.030 --> 01:45:50.390
I haven't heard any
anthropological investigations

01:45:50.390 --> 01:45:53.420
as to why Americans
are so negative,

01:45:53.420 --> 01:45:56.330
you know, and the British
only slightly less negative.

01:45:56.330 --> 01:45:59.570
Well, you know, that's how
the United Nations works.

01:45:59.570 --> 01:46:02.210
Now, sometimes--
very often, in fact--

01:46:02.210 --> 01:46:04.530
it does vote against
the United States.

01:46:04.530 --> 01:46:05.690
It hasn't on Haiti.

01:46:05.690 --> 01:46:07.355
But take the Cuban embargo.

01:46:07.355 --> 01:46:10.170
I mean, that's been
condemned by everybody.

01:46:10.170 --> 01:46:12.290
You know, it comes up
over and over again.

01:46:12.290 --> 01:46:13.610
It doesn't come up in
the Security Council,

01:46:13.610 --> 01:46:14.776
because the US will veto it.

01:46:14.776 --> 01:46:18.170
But it comes up in the
General Assembly all the time.

01:46:18.170 --> 01:46:21.240
The votes are unanimous.

01:46:21.240 --> 01:46:22.970
There are two votes against.

01:46:22.970 --> 01:46:25.640
Israel is compelled to vote
with the United States.

01:46:25.640 --> 01:46:27.180
It has no choice.

01:46:27.180 --> 01:46:28.850
So there's two votes
against, but Israel

01:46:28.850 --> 01:46:30.080
violates the embargo.

01:46:30.080 --> 01:46:31.730
I mean, you know,
they know they've

01:46:31.730 --> 01:46:34.970
got to vote with the boss,
but they violate the embargo.

01:46:34.970 --> 01:46:37.820
The OAS, which, as you
say, is totally supine,

01:46:37.820 --> 01:46:38.810
they've condemned it.

01:46:38.810 --> 01:46:40.820
Their legal commission
has condemned it.

01:46:40.820 --> 01:46:43.892
It's been condemned as in
violation of international law.

01:46:43.892 --> 01:46:46.100
The European Union brought
it up with the World Trade

01:46:46.100 --> 01:46:48.680
Organization, because
the embargo, especially

01:46:48.680 --> 01:46:52.430
the secondary boycott parts, are
in gross violation of the World

01:46:52.430 --> 01:46:54.410
Trade Organization rules.

01:46:54.410 --> 01:46:55.250
They brought it up.

01:46:55.250 --> 01:46:59.360
The Clinton Administration
responded first

01:46:59.360 --> 01:47:01.400
by saying that--

01:47:01.400 --> 01:47:03.020
there's a thing
in the World Trade

01:47:03.020 --> 01:47:05.750
Organization called the
national security exemption.

01:47:05.750 --> 01:47:08.030
Like, if your
existence is at stake,

01:47:08.030 --> 01:47:11.570
you know, you can
violate a trade law.

01:47:11.570 --> 01:47:14.560
So the US first claimed the
national security exemption.

01:47:14.560 --> 01:47:15.270
[LAUGHTER]

01:47:16.010 --> 01:47:19.160
Like, if we don't
prevent food and medicine

01:47:19.160 --> 01:47:23.430
from going to Cubans, we'll
disappear as a country.

01:47:23.430 --> 01:47:26.070
But that was too ludicrous.

01:47:26.070 --> 01:47:28.790
So they finally simply
told them, you know,

01:47:28.790 --> 01:47:30.950
the usual answer, get lost.

01:47:30.950 --> 01:47:33.050
I mean, I don't care what
it says in those rules

01:47:33.050 --> 01:47:35.810
anymore than we do when it
says that you can't stop

01:47:35.810 --> 01:47:37.970
the Japanese from sending
supercomputers, which

01:47:37.970 --> 01:47:39.750
are better than ones made here.

01:47:39.750 --> 01:47:41.030
You don't like it?

01:47:41.030 --> 01:47:42.410
Do something about it.

01:47:42.410 --> 01:47:45.080
As Stalin once has
reputed to have

01:47:45.080 --> 01:47:48.470
said when the Vatican criticized
him, how many divisions

01:47:48.470 --> 01:47:49.754
has the Pope, you know?

01:47:49.754 --> 01:47:51.170
Well, I don't know
if he said that

01:47:51.170 --> 01:47:54.207
or not, but that's what
the powerful say, you know?

01:47:54.207 --> 01:47:56.540
You don't like what we're
doing, all right, do something

01:47:56.540 --> 01:47:57.770
about it.

01:47:57.770 --> 01:48:01.420
And there's nothing
anyone can do except us.

01:48:01.420 --> 01:48:02.280
We can do it.

01:48:02.280 --> 01:48:04.820
You know, if there are changes
internally in the United

01:48:04.820 --> 01:48:07.670
States, anything can happen.

01:48:07.670 --> 01:48:10.540
One of the reasons why this
stuff is never reported,

01:48:10.540 --> 01:48:12.730
and it isn't and
never discussed,

01:48:12.730 --> 01:48:15.450
is because people might
find out about it.

01:48:15.450 --> 01:48:17.830
If we had a functioning
democracy here,

01:48:17.830 --> 01:48:20.840
you know, you might find out
what's being done in your name.

01:48:20.840 --> 01:48:23.230
And you might not like it.

01:48:23.230 --> 01:48:25.400
And this goes all
over the world.

01:48:25.400 --> 01:48:27.440
I said I was in Turkey
a couple days ago.

01:48:27.440 --> 01:48:31.060
What we've done there under
the Clinton years is grotesque.

01:48:31.060 --> 01:48:33.570
I mean, the charges against
Milosevic and the Hague,

01:48:33.570 --> 01:48:35.800
you know-- bad
enough, but nothing

01:48:35.800 --> 01:48:37.900
like what happened
in the same years

01:48:37.900 --> 01:48:40.780
that Clinton was paying
for just in Turkey alone.

01:48:40.780 --> 01:48:41.980
That's one place.

01:48:41.980 --> 01:48:43.510
Therefore, it isn't discussed.

01:48:46.880 --> 01:48:51.170
The UN votes on
the Cuban embargo,

01:48:51.170 --> 01:48:53.650
they're scarcely mentioned.

01:48:53.650 --> 01:48:56.920
The vetoes are almost
never mentioned.

01:48:56.920 --> 01:48:58.685
I mean, the most recent one--

01:48:58.685 --> 01:49:01.465
I'll finish-- was last
December, December 15th.

01:49:04.000 --> 01:49:07.900
Bush was making speeches about
how terrible Palestinians are

01:49:07.900 --> 01:49:09.460
enhancing terror.

01:49:09.460 --> 01:49:13.030
Well, there's a way to reduce
terror, which overwhelmingly

01:49:13.030 --> 01:49:16.720
is the powerful people, of
course, the US and Israel.

01:49:16.720 --> 01:49:18.220
There's a way to do it.

01:49:18.220 --> 01:49:22.570
You could implement
the US Mitchell Report

01:49:22.570 --> 01:49:28.750
and send international
monitors who do,

01:49:28.750 --> 01:49:32.440
in fact, reduce violence
when they're there.

01:49:32.440 --> 01:49:34.990
It came up the United
Nations Security Council.

01:49:34.990 --> 01:49:37.480
The Europeans
introduced a resolution

01:49:37.480 --> 01:49:40.540
to that effect calling for
a reduction of violence,

01:49:40.540 --> 01:49:42.040
implementing the
Mitchell Report,

01:49:42.040 --> 01:49:43.780
sending international monitors.

01:49:43.780 --> 01:49:45.370
US vetoed it.

01:49:45.370 --> 01:49:49.510
It went to the General Assembly,
overwhelming vote in favor.

01:49:49.510 --> 01:49:52.900
This time the United
States also picked up

01:49:52.900 --> 01:49:58.540
not only Israel, which is
automatic, but also Nauru

01:49:58.540 --> 01:50:01.510
and one of the other
Pacific Islands.

01:50:01.510 --> 01:50:04.210
I think jointly they
have 20,000 people.

01:50:04.210 --> 01:50:08.440
So it wasn't totally isolated
the way it usually is.

01:50:08.440 --> 01:50:10.480
You think there was
any report about this?

01:50:10.480 --> 01:50:11.980
Take a look, you know?

01:50:11.980 --> 01:50:14.606
If you really search, you could
find a word about it somewhere.

01:50:14.606 --> 01:50:15.540
DORSINVILLE: Professor Chomsky?

01:50:15.540 --> 01:50:17.248
CHOMSKY: I mean, that's
the way it works.

01:50:17.248 --> 01:50:18.000
Yeah.

01:50:18.000 --> 01:50:19.030
Don't me get started.

01:50:19.030 --> 01:50:20.500
[LAUGHTER]

01:50:20.500 --> 01:50:21.970
[APPLAUSE]

01:50:23.832 --> 01:50:24.790
DORSINVILLE: Thank you.

01:50:24.790 --> 01:50:27.640
FARMER: I told
you he would know.

01:50:27.640 --> 01:50:29.490
DORSINVILLE: Okay,
we have a question

01:50:29.490 --> 01:50:32.478
back there, the gentleman
in the orange T-shirt.

01:50:32.478 --> 01:50:34.868
AUDIENCE: Yes, I have a
question for Dr. Farmer.

01:50:34.868 --> 01:50:37.258
[AUDIO OUT] your experience
and your familiarity

01:50:37.258 --> 01:50:39.620
with rural Haiti,
I was wondering

01:50:39.620 --> 01:50:41.875
if you could comment on the
ecological crisis that's

01:50:41.875 --> 01:50:44.904
going on there and
how it's affecting,

01:50:44.904 --> 01:50:47.339
you know, human development
and public health?

01:50:47.339 --> 01:50:48.197
FARMER: Yeah.

01:50:48.197 --> 01:50:49.280
Did you hear the question?

01:50:49.280 --> 01:50:50.510
AUDIENCE: No.

01:50:50.510 --> 01:50:53.130
FARMER: How has the ecological
crisis in rural Haiti

01:50:53.130 --> 01:50:54.650
affected human development?

01:50:54.650 --> 01:50:57.677
And, you know, there's a short
answer and a long answer.

01:50:57.677 --> 01:50:58.760
I'll go for the short one.

01:50:58.760 --> 01:51:01.430
And the impact
has been profound.

01:51:01.430 --> 01:51:04.790
I mentioned already the struggle
for food, wood, and water.

01:51:04.790 --> 01:51:10.255
And the fact that, you know,
people don't have electricity,

01:51:10.255 --> 01:51:12.740
you know, or, say, gas--

01:51:12.740 --> 01:51:14.570
you know, because the
Cubans, for example,

01:51:14.570 --> 01:51:19.160
are trying to help Haiti
develop a way of cooking

01:51:19.160 --> 01:51:23.090
with small little propane type
things that are really small

01:51:23.090 --> 01:51:25.812
and would match the way it's
been done in terms of scale.

01:51:25.812 --> 01:51:27.520
I mean, these are not
places with stoves.

01:51:27.520 --> 01:51:29.180
These are places
where food is cooked

01:51:29.180 --> 01:51:33.640
outside as the Haitians
say, [SPEAKING FRENCH],,

01:51:33.640 --> 01:51:35.280
on three stones.

01:51:35.280 --> 01:51:40.400
So there's really no way to
do that without replacing,

01:51:40.400 --> 01:51:44.769
you know, wood as a
fuel, charcoal as a fuel.

01:51:44.769 --> 01:51:46.560
And, again, I just
don't think that's going

01:51:46.560 --> 01:51:50.330
to happen without some
systematic approach

01:51:50.330 --> 01:51:52.220
to developing an alternative.

01:51:52.220 --> 01:51:54.290
Because in the absence
of an alternative that's

01:51:54.290 --> 01:51:58.970
promoted by a policy,
no one has capital to go

01:51:58.970 --> 01:52:04.430
and by such a thing or
to replace the propane.

01:52:04.430 --> 01:52:07.110
Solar stuff hasn't
worked very well.

01:52:07.110 --> 01:52:10.250
But the ecological crisis
from deforestation alone,

01:52:10.250 --> 01:52:12.830
not talking about
pollution of groundwater,

01:52:12.830 --> 01:52:16.070
has just been
profound and terrible.

01:52:16.070 --> 01:52:20.000
And, again, USAID have
funded tens of millions

01:52:20.000 --> 01:52:22.580
of dollars for
reforestation projects.

01:52:22.580 --> 01:52:24.867
And they, obviously,
have not been successful.

01:52:24.867 --> 01:52:26.450
I do remember, though,
that USAID once

01:52:26.450 --> 01:52:29.840
funded a single drainage project
in Port-au-Prince about five

01:52:29.840 --> 01:52:32.180
different times under the
Duvaliers, the same drainage

01:52:32.180 --> 01:52:33.470
project that never got done.

01:52:33.470 --> 01:52:35.678
Now, thank god they're going
to try to really clamped

01:52:35.678 --> 01:52:37.910
down and make sure that
Aristide's government uses

01:52:37.910 --> 01:52:39.530
funds wisely.

01:52:39.530 --> 01:52:44.339
But I think those giant
reforestation projects-- maybe

01:52:44.339 --> 01:52:46.130
someone here knows more
about it than I do.

01:52:46.130 --> 01:52:49.640
None of them has been really a
success by internal government

01:52:49.640 --> 01:52:51.260
accounting office reviews.

01:52:51.260 --> 01:52:54.740
And, again, that's another tip
from the Noamster, as we say.

01:52:54.740 --> 01:52:55.730
This is on the radio?

01:52:55.730 --> 01:52:56.780
Sorry.

01:52:56.780 --> 01:52:59.330
Another tip from
Professor Chomsky--

01:52:59.330 --> 01:53:00.890
go to official sources.

01:53:00.890 --> 01:53:03.200
And, you know, the
government accounting office

01:53:03.200 --> 01:53:05.930
has published its
own internal report

01:53:05.930 --> 01:53:08.870
on the performance
of our aid wing.

01:53:08.870 --> 01:53:10.162
It's really devastating.

01:53:10.162 --> 01:53:12.620
And, you know, I'd recommend
that you look at that as well.

01:53:12.620 --> 01:53:14.840
Because there's been
projects in the past which

01:53:14.840 --> 01:53:16.190
have been tried and failed.

01:53:16.190 --> 01:53:18.200
My own view as to why
they failed-- well, they

01:53:18.200 --> 01:53:19.580
were being routed
through a government

01:53:19.580 --> 01:53:21.260
really did not have
the health and welfare

01:53:21.260 --> 01:53:23.870
of the Haitian people at heart,
meaning the family dictatorship

01:53:23.870 --> 01:53:25.203
followed by the military juntas.

01:53:25.203 --> 01:53:27.920
Now, again, that may sound
fantastic as a theory

01:53:27.920 --> 01:53:31.100
to some people, but I
believe it makes sense.

01:53:31.100 --> 01:53:32.510
DORSINVILLE: Thank you.

01:53:32.510 --> 01:53:33.510
We have a question here.

01:53:33.510 --> 01:53:34.093
AUDIENCE: Yes.

01:53:34.093 --> 01:53:37.410
This question is for Dr. Farmer.

01:53:37.410 --> 01:53:42.970
Let's talk about AIDS in
Haiti and the correlation

01:53:42.970 --> 01:53:46.460
to the rest of Southern Africa.

01:53:46.460 --> 01:53:47.460
FARMER: Where are you?

01:53:47.460 --> 01:53:49.300
AUDIENCE: I'm here.

01:53:49.300 --> 01:53:50.900
FARMER: Oh, sorry.

01:53:50.900 --> 01:53:56.660
AUDIENCE: And also, 1981,
the NIH started with 4H

01:53:56.660 --> 01:53:58.189
and Haiti was--

01:53:58.189 --> 01:53:58.730
FARMER: Yeah.

01:53:58.730 --> 01:53:59.355
AUDIENCE: Yeah.

01:53:59.355 --> 01:54:04.310
FARMER: I mean, it's really
a very disturbing chapter

01:54:04.310 --> 01:54:05.600
of US-Haitian relations.

01:54:05.600 --> 01:54:06.860
I didn't mention it here.

01:54:06.860 --> 01:54:09.980
But you're right, I think
it's worth mentioning.

01:54:09.980 --> 01:54:14.450
And some people here are old
enough to remember that back

01:54:14.450 --> 01:54:17.900
in the early '80s,
as you were saying,

01:54:17.900 --> 01:54:22.040
the suggestion was widely
circulated in the US press that

01:54:22.040 --> 01:54:23.420
HIV--

01:54:23.420 --> 01:54:25.580
or at that point AIDS,
because we hadn't really

01:54:25.580 --> 01:54:27.350
identified HIV--

01:54:27.350 --> 01:54:30.200
had come to the United
States from Haiti.

01:54:30.200 --> 01:54:32.060
And, of course,
just the opposite

01:54:32.060 --> 01:54:35.285
was true, as is the case I'm
learning with most reporting--

01:54:35.285 --> 01:54:36.770
[LAUGHTER]

01:54:36.770 --> 01:54:39.200
--that HIV actually went to
Haiti from the United States

01:54:39.200 --> 01:54:42.470
and to all the countries of
the circum-Caribbean basin

01:54:42.470 --> 01:54:43.580
the same way, except Cuba.

01:54:43.580 --> 01:54:45.079
Cuba is the only
country that didn't

01:54:45.079 --> 01:54:49.250
have that tight relationship.

01:54:49.250 --> 01:54:51.509
And the countries with
the closest relationships

01:54:51.509 --> 01:54:53.300
to the United States
and the most dependent

01:54:53.300 --> 01:54:58.286
on the kind of aid and expertise
that we've talked about already

01:54:58.286 --> 01:54:59.660
were the countries
most affected,

01:54:59.660 --> 01:55:02.960
with Haiti actually being
the country most dependent

01:55:02.960 --> 01:55:05.000
on US exports.

01:55:05.000 --> 01:55:07.700
So not to make a crude
ecological association,

01:55:07.700 --> 01:55:10.070
but HIV actually came to
Haiti from the United States

01:55:10.070 --> 01:55:13.214
and then spread, because of
poverty, gender inequality,

01:55:13.214 --> 01:55:14.630
and political
violence, which made

01:55:14.630 --> 01:55:18.710
any kind of, again, public
health approach very difficult.

01:55:18.710 --> 01:55:21.200
I mean, I worked during
those years in Haiti.

01:55:21.200 --> 01:55:25.076
And in 1992, for
example, or '93 perhaps,

01:55:25.076 --> 01:55:26.450
a women's group
that I had worked

01:55:26.450 --> 01:55:29.960
with for some time working on
a video about gender inequality

01:55:29.960 --> 01:55:33.140
and HIV, the women's group was
showing the video in a village

01:55:33.140 --> 01:55:34.850
not far from our clinic.

01:55:34.850 --> 01:55:38.570
And they were planning to
lead a discussion afterwards.

01:55:38.570 --> 01:55:42.440
And soldiers came and dislodged
them from the building

01:55:42.440 --> 01:55:44.060
and threatened them
with their lives.

01:55:44.060 --> 01:55:46.100
And, actually, the head
of the women's group

01:55:46.100 --> 01:55:48.440
said to me, you know, I
don't think we should do this

01:55:48.440 --> 01:55:49.790
anymore, AIDS prevention.

01:55:49.790 --> 01:55:51.730
She said, it's
just too dangerous.

01:55:51.730 --> 01:55:55.889
You know, that was 1993.

01:55:55.889 --> 01:55:58.430
And someone, I'm sure, is going
to have a very different take

01:55:58.430 --> 01:55:59.179
on this than I do.

01:55:59.179 --> 01:56:02.060
But reading about this
horrible persecution

01:56:02.060 --> 01:56:04.350
of the Haitian press
alleged to be going on now

01:56:04.350 --> 01:56:06.470
is just amazing to
me when, in fact, we

01:56:06.470 --> 01:56:11.702
have documentation of
a massive eradication

01:56:11.702 --> 01:56:13.910
of the independent press in
Haiti under the Duvaliers

01:56:13.910 --> 01:56:17.300
and under the military regime.

01:56:17.300 --> 01:56:20.390
And there is a great deal--
and I'm mixing things together.

01:56:20.390 --> 01:56:23.450
But the very most difficult
time for doing work

01:56:23.450 --> 01:56:28.430
like that, HIV prevention,
was during the '80s and '90s

01:56:28.430 --> 01:56:32.070
actually, particularly the
late '80s and early '90s.

01:56:32.070 --> 01:56:35.690
So the epidemic in
Haiti is moving forward

01:56:35.690 --> 01:56:39.770
untrammeled by really
significant prevention efforts.

01:56:39.770 --> 01:56:42.080
And that's part of
what is being proposed

01:56:42.080 --> 01:56:45.950
by the Ministry of Health,
a robust HIV program.

01:56:45.950 --> 01:56:48.650
Let me just tell
you this one thing.

01:56:48.650 --> 01:56:51.500
The World Bank and other
funders got together

01:56:51.500 --> 01:56:55.550
and started putting
together this AIDS package

01:56:55.550 --> 01:56:57.140
for the Caribbean,
an aid package.

01:56:57.140 --> 01:56:58.910
And the one country
most affected

01:56:58.910 --> 01:57:01.410
by HIV in the
Caribbean, you know,

01:57:01.410 --> 01:57:04.670
was slated for zero dollars.

01:57:04.670 --> 01:57:06.490
These are professionals.

01:57:06.490 --> 01:57:09.270
These are doctors and public
health experts who are saying,

01:57:09.270 --> 01:57:09.770
oh, well.

01:57:09.770 --> 01:57:11.690
It's okay that we
don't have Haiti,

01:57:11.690 --> 01:57:14.930
the most affected
country in the region,

01:57:14.930 --> 01:57:16.670
involved in our funding program.

01:57:16.670 --> 01:57:19.920
So I blame my own colleagues
for not questioning that.

01:57:19.920 --> 01:57:23.177
I just went to a meeting
in Jamaica two days ago.

01:57:23.177 --> 01:57:25.010
By the way, never go
to Jamaica for one day.

01:57:25.010 --> 01:57:26.142
Stay longer.

01:57:26.142 --> 01:57:27.352
[LAUGHTER]

01:57:27.352 --> 01:57:29.810
I went to a meeting of the Pan
American Health Organization

01:57:29.810 --> 01:57:30.560
for one day.

01:57:33.120 --> 01:57:36.350
And the most affected
country was not represented.

01:57:36.350 --> 01:57:38.780
There's 25 countries represented
there, but not Haiti.

01:57:38.780 --> 01:57:43.520
So, I mean, this is how it plays
out in terms of specific health

01:57:43.520 --> 01:57:44.030
problems.

01:57:44.030 --> 01:57:45.650
I thank you for
asking the question.

01:57:45.650 --> 01:57:46.400
DORSINVILLE: Okay.

01:57:49.210 --> 01:57:50.335
AUDIENCE: Should I go next?

01:57:50.335 --> 01:57:51.085
DORSINVILLE: Yeah.

01:57:51.085 --> 01:57:54.080
AUDIENCE: What is the purported
rationale for the current US

01:57:54.080 --> 01:57:54.590
embargo?

01:57:54.590 --> 01:57:58.880
I mean, the presentations
began with an introduction.

01:57:58.880 --> 01:58:03.950
But so far, I haven't heard
what the purported explanation

01:58:03.950 --> 01:58:06.320
of the current US embargo is.

01:58:06.320 --> 01:58:09.560
What are its publicly
stated goals?

01:58:09.560 --> 01:58:12.320
What is its hidden
agenda if any?

01:58:12.320 --> 01:58:16.100
And, in a larger sense,
what are the policies

01:58:16.100 --> 01:58:18.425
that the United
States and the IMF

01:58:18.425 --> 01:58:26.810
or any other relevant entities
want the government of Haiti

01:58:26.810 --> 01:58:28.820
to be adopting?

01:58:28.820 --> 01:58:31.730
And, conversely,
what are the policies

01:58:31.730 --> 01:58:34.790
that the current
government is attempting

01:58:34.790 --> 01:58:38.870
to adopt instead of
whatever the United

01:58:38.870 --> 01:58:43.910
States and other entities
would prefer to impose?

01:58:43.910 --> 01:58:45.940
FARMER: Well, we'll do
this in a two-part thing,

01:58:45.940 --> 01:58:48.460
because I know Noam
will have things to add.

01:58:48.460 --> 01:58:51.730
But I'll just say that
I believe, in fact,

01:58:51.730 --> 01:58:54.310
that many people in
those institutions

01:58:54.310 --> 01:58:56.167
are opposed to the
embargo, because I've

01:58:56.167 --> 01:58:57.250
spoken with them directly.

01:58:57.250 --> 01:59:03.610
I went to talk to
some of these people

01:59:03.610 --> 01:59:05.620
and, again, more as
a concerned citizen.

01:59:05.620 --> 01:59:09.360
Because our own group does not
depend upon this kind of aid.

01:59:09.360 --> 01:59:12.520
And I say a concerned
American citizen, US citizen.

01:59:12.520 --> 01:59:15.130
Our group doesn't rely
on any of these efforts

01:59:15.130 --> 01:59:16.600
to run our hospital.

01:59:16.600 --> 01:59:18.600
We rely on private
donations from, you know,

01:59:18.600 --> 01:59:21.580
friends and funders and
some progressive foundations

01:59:21.580 --> 01:59:22.490
and churches.

01:59:22.490 --> 01:59:24.430
But this has nothing
to do with us.

01:59:24.430 --> 01:59:26.530
But I did go there,
because there are not

01:59:26.530 --> 01:59:29.440
that many US citizens
who are saying, wait,

01:59:29.440 --> 01:59:30.740
this is unacceptable.

01:59:30.740 --> 01:59:32.560
And I met person
after person who

01:59:32.560 --> 01:59:35.650
said, oh, we are so
embarrassed by this, you know?

01:59:35.650 --> 01:59:37.381
This is a ridiculous policy.

01:59:37.381 --> 01:59:38.380
So then you keep asking.

01:59:38.380 --> 01:59:40.990
Where is this coming from then
if you're embarrassed by it,

01:59:40.990 --> 01:59:41.680
you know?

01:59:41.680 --> 01:59:44.000
You are the vice president,
and then fill in the blank,

01:59:44.000 --> 01:59:44.500
you know?

01:59:44.500 --> 01:59:46.795
Choose your favorite-- I
went to several of them.

01:59:46.795 --> 01:59:49.420
And I already told you what the
head of the Pan American Health

01:59:49.420 --> 01:59:53.710
Organization said, you know,
regarding the fact that you

01:59:53.710 --> 01:59:55.810
can't have an
embargo on a country

01:59:55.810 --> 01:59:58.910
where there's so many
public health problems.

01:59:58.910 --> 02:00:01.030
And so you ask where
is this coming from.

02:00:01.030 --> 02:00:04.240
And Noam will have
gotten to this,

02:00:04.240 --> 02:00:06.130
you know, from
the other way just

02:00:06.130 --> 02:00:08.320
by knowing the information.

02:00:08.320 --> 02:00:11.470
But I finally came to
believe, in any case,

02:00:11.470 --> 02:00:14.290
that this is actually coming
from the State Department

02:00:14.290 --> 02:00:16.840
under the suggestions of
just probably a fairly

02:00:16.840 --> 02:00:17.920
small number of people.

02:00:17.920 --> 02:00:19.671
Jesse Helms is one of
them I'm quite sure.

02:00:19.671 --> 02:00:20.545
AUDIENCE: [INAUDIBLE]

02:00:20.545 --> 02:00:21.650
FARMER: No, I'm serious.

02:00:21.650 --> 02:00:24.580
And I thought, well, you
know, a huge government

02:00:24.580 --> 02:00:28.630
like this, the
hugest government,

02:00:28.630 --> 02:00:31.517
can't have its policy
determined really by one person.

02:00:31.517 --> 02:00:33.850
Of course, that's been a point
of institutional analysis

02:00:33.850 --> 02:00:35.360
is that's not how it works.

02:00:35.360 --> 02:00:38.800
But Haiti is so
beaten down right now

02:00:38.800 --> 02:00:42.005
that you wonder, again,
if someone with-- it's

02:00:42.005 --> 02:00:42.880
almost like a grudge.

02:00:42.880 --> 02:00:45.820
Of course, it makes better sense
to not think of it that way.

02:00:45.820 --> 02:00:47.920
But, right now,
there's a lot of--

02:00:47.920 --> 02:00:50.080
I told you about
talking to someone

02:00:50.080 --> 02:00:53.007
today on the phone who said, you
know, I find this embarrassing.

02:00:53.007 --> 02:00:54.340
And I'd like to see it reversed.

02:00:54.340 --> 02:00:57.897
So that gets me to
just one little point,

02:00:57.897 --> 02:00:59.230
and then I'll pass it over Noam.

02:00:59.230 --> 02:01:01.990
I don't think there's anything
that the Haitian government can

02:01:01.990 --> 02:01:03.880
do to please the people
who are behind this.

02:01:03.880 --> 02:01:06.285
I don't think there's any way.

02:01:06.285 --> 02:01:07.660
Because the things
that have been

02:01:07.660 --> 02:01:10.120
asked for as concessions
have all been made already

02:01:10.120 --> 02:01:11.650
by the Haitian government.

02:01:11.650 --> 02:01:13.930
So there's no way.

02:01:13.930 --> 02:01:15.050
They can't win.

02:01:15.050 --> 02:01:18.016
They could never
do anything to meet

02:01:18.016 --> 02:01:19.390
the demands of
the people who are

02:01:19.390 --> 02:01:22.090
the architects, the intellectual
architects, of the embargo.

02:01:22.090 --> 02:01:27.209
And I'm going to go ahead and
quote Jeffrey Sachs who said,

02:01:27.209 --> 02:01:28.750
a couple of days
ago, "it seems to me

02:01:28.750 --> 02:01:30.375
that there are only
two possibilities--

02:01:30.375 --> 02:01:32.620
one, that the goal
of this policy

02:01:32.620 --> 02:01:34.060
is to overthrow
the democratically

02:01:34.060 --> 02:01:35.226
elected government of Haiti.

02:01:35.226 --> 02:01:37.810
Or, B, that there is
such massive incompetence

02:01:37.810 --> 02:01:41.230
inside the machinery
of state up here

02:01:41.230 --> 02:01:43.600
that they're just bad at
executing another policy.

02:01:43.600 --> 02:01:46.237
So it's either that's their
policy, or they're incompetent.

02:01:46.237 --> 02:01:48.070
Those are the two options
that he could see.

02:01:48.070 --> 02:01:49.370
Noam?

02:01:49.370 --> 02:01:52.370
CHOMSKY: Well, just to
add, the leading individual

02:01:52.370 --> 02:01:56.450
who's spearheading this
happens to be Colin Powell.

02:01:56.450 --> 02:01:59.620
He is regarded as the
administration dove,

02:01:59.620 --> 02:02:02.940
you know, the moderate,
which is probably true.

02:02:02.940 --> 02:02:03.680
What that means?

02:02:03.680 --> 02:02:06.500
He's slightly to the moderate
side of Attila the Hun,

02:02:06.500 --> 02:02:07.040
I suppose.

02:02:07.040 --> 02:02:09.322
[LAUGHTER]

02:02:09.322 --> 02:02:11.780
For example, there was just a
meeting of CARICOM, you know,

02:02:11.780 --> 02:02:12.890
the Caribbean--

02:02:12.890 --> 02:02:16.020
I forget what it stands for,
the Caribbean countries.

02:02:16.020 --> 02:02:19.670
Everyone was opposed to the
embargo, every single one.

02:02:19.670 --> 02:02:22.730
Powell insisted that we're
going to continue it.

02:02:22.730 --> 02:02:25.220
That's our policy.

02:02:25.220 --> 02:02:26.959
And Helms is behind it, too.

02:02:26.959 --> 02:02:27.500
You're right.

02:02:27.500 --> 02:02:31.530
But, you know, they don't have
to yield to Helms on this.

02:02:31.530 --> 02:02:33.680
They're doing it,
because they want to.

02:02:33.680 --> 02:02:35.450
So then you ask why.

02:02:35.450 --> 02:02:37.370
Well, they have a
technical reason.

02:02:37.370 --> 02:02:40.940
Haiti didn't meet our
elevated standards

02:02:40.940 --> 02:02:44.040
of how a democratic
election should be run.

02:02:44.040 --> 02:02:45.620
And I don't doubt
that there were

02:02:45.620 --> 02:02:47.744
all kinds of irregularities
in the election.

02:02:47.744 --> 02:02:49.160
And maybe this was
wrong, and that

02:02:49.160 --> 02:02:50.451
was wrong, and the other thing.

02:02:50.451 --> 02:02:53.180
But to talk about our
standards for elections

02:02:53.180 --> 02:02:56.060
if you consider the
countries to which we

02:02:56.060 --> 02:02:59.410
don't put under embargo,
I mean this doesn't even--

02:02:59.410 --> 02:03:02.750
you know, it's hard to
imagine that people can even

02:03:02.750 --> 02:03:07.970
mouth these words without
such embarrassment.

02:03:07.970 --> 02:03:09.530
It's almost indescribable.

02:03:09.530 --> 02:03:11.390
So that can't be the reason.

02:03:11.390 --> 02:03:13.310
What the real reason
is, I suppose,

02:03:13.310 --> 02:03:14.430
what it's been all along.

02:03:14.430 --> 02:03:16.730
I mean, the reason that
they hated Aristide

02:03:16.730 --> 02:03:19.400
from the beginning,
very beginning,

02:03:19.400 --> 02:03:22.400
you know-- he was a
renegade, and a radical,

02:03:22.400 --> 02:03:25.252
and a firebrand, and
preaching class warfare,

02:03:25.252 --> 02:03:27.710
and this, that, and the other
thing, a psychotic, you know.

02:03:27.710 --> 02:03:29.334
I mean, the charges
against him weren't

02:03:29.334 --> 02:03:31.880
credible-- was because
his government,

02:03:31.880 --> 02:03:36.050
the official terminology was,
it wasn't sufficiently diverse.

02:03:36.050 --> 02:03:40.190
It only included the 90%
of the population who live

02:03:40.190 --> 02:03:42.500
in the slums and the hills.

02:03:42.500 --> 02:03:46.280
And it didn't include what
the United States defines

02:03:46.280 --> 02:03:51.410
as Haitian civil society, namely
the private sector, national

02:03:51.410 --> 02:03:53.190
and international.

02:03:53.190 --> 02:03:56.900
So it didn't give sufficient
representation, meaning

02:03:56.900 --> 02:04:00.710
dominance, to US
investors who were Haitian

02:04:00.710 --> 02:04:04.310
civil society and to,
you know, the Mevs family

02:04:04.310 --> 02:04:07.460
and the Brandt family and
the other small families

02:04:07.460 --> 02:04:10.100
who were the, you
know, current version

02:04:10.100 --> 02:04:13.250
of the guys who were calling
for slavery 200 years ago,

02:04:13.250 --> 02:04:15.320
didn't give them dominance.

02:04:15.320 --> 02:04:17.660
So therefore, it's not
sufficiently broad.

02:04:17.660 --> 02:04:19.260
On the other hand,
if some government,

02:04:19.260 --> 02:04:21.050
you know, like just
about everyone,

02:04:21.050 --> 02:04:24.200
excludes virtually
the entire population,

02:04:24.200 --> 02:04:29.840
but gives privilege to those
who really count, you know,

02:04:29.840 --> 02:04:32.740
the private sector,
national and international,

02:04:32.740 --> 02:04:34.310
then they're fine.

02:04:34.310 --> 02:04:35.570
Then there's no problem.

02:04:35.570 --> 02:04:37.250
I mean, that's been
said officially so

02:04:37.250 --> 02:04:41.130
many times that you can hardly
even call it a hidden agenda.

02:04:41.130 --> 02:04:43.520
You know, it's an open agenda.

02:04:43.520 --> 02:04:47.340
And it's an understandable one.

02:04:47.340 --> 02:04:49.280
Nobody's going to say it.

02:04:49.280 --> 02:04:51.578
They actually say in
almost these words.

02:04:54.980 --> 02:04:57.218
DORSINVILLE: The
gentleman in the back.

02:04:57.218 --> 02:05:09.580
AUDIENCE: [INAUDIBLE] in rural
Haiti to work with people.

02:05:09.580 --> 02:05:13.600
I wonder if you have an
assessment of this initiative

02:05:13.600 --> 02:05:16.330
and if you think this
kind of corporation

02:05:16.330 --> 02:05:20.165
can help when we can get the
$22 million from the bank.

02:05:20.165 --> 02:05:21.790
FARMER: Did everyone
hear the question?

02:05:21.790 --> 02:05:23.410
AUDIENCE: No.

02:05:23.410 --> 02:05:25.270
FARMER: The discussion
of Cuban assistance

02:05:25.270 --> 02:05:28.210
to Haiti in the form of
medical personnel, how

02:05:28.210 --> 02:05:33.250
is this going to work if
aid to resuscitate Haitian

02:05:33.250 --> 02:05:35.380
public health is blocked.

02:05:35.380 --> 02:05:36.710
I think it's a great question.

02:05:36.710 --> 02:05:39.820
First, let me tell you I admire
those physicians enormously.

02:05:39.820 --> 02:05:42.670
And, in fact, some of them
have pitched in our work.

02:05:42.670 --> 02:05:46.180
And they've done
really valiant--

02:05:46.180 --> 02:05:47.350
they're really working hard.

02:05:47.350 --> 02:05:49.720
And there's almost 800
there, I think, now.

02:05:49.720 --> 02:05:52.420
So there are more Cuban
doctors in rural Haiti

02:05:52.420 --> 02:05:53.680
than Haitian doctors.

02:05:53.680 --> 02:05:56.020
There are more Haitians
psychiatrists in Montreal

02:05:56.020 --> 02:05:58.930
than all of Haiti, okay?

02:05:58.930 --> 02:06:00.310
And this is a trend.

02:06:00.310 --> 02:06:06.130
By the way, somebody studied 264
graduates of the medical school

02:06:06.130 --> 02:06:09.267
under a period of a couple--

02:06:09.267 --> 02:06:11.350
I don't remember how many--
classes under Francois

02:06:11.350 --> 02:06:11.850
Duvalier.

02:06:11.850 --> 02:06:14.290
And there were only
four left in Haiti.

02:06:14.290 --> 02:06:17.140
So, again, this is a
longstanding problem,

02:06:17.140 --> 02:06:19.292
lack of medical personnel.

02:06:19.292 --> 02:06:21.250
Cuba, as you might know,
has the largest number

02:06:21.250 --> 02:06:23.570
of doctors per population
in the world of any country

02:06:23.570 --> 02:06:24.670
as far as I know.

02:06:24.670 --> 02:06:27.260
So their sending out
doctors has been great.

02:06:27.260 --> 02:06:30.449
However, I think that the
weakness of the Cuban aid

02:06:30.449 --> 02:06:31.990
strategy has nothing
to do with them.

02:06:31.990 --> 02:06:33.615
It's like, again,
yelling at countries,

02:06:33.615 --> 02:06:34.980
because they're poor.

02:06:34.980 --> 02:06:37.270
You need a third
partner with capital.

02:06:37.270 --> 02:06:39.910
Either the Haitian public
health infrastructure

02:06:39.910 --> 02:06:42.490
needs an infusion
of capital right now

02:06:42.490 --> 02:06:44.650
in order to make that work.

02:06:44.650 --> 02:06:47.530
Because the problem,
frankly, is that if you

02:06:47.530 --> 02:06:51.130
have Cuban doctors fitting
into the Haitian health system.

02:06:51.130 --> 02:06:52.090
That's terrible.

02:06:52.090 --> 02:06:56.020
The Haitian health system is
all fee for service exclusively.

02:06:56.020 --> 02:06:57.910
So what you do--

02:06:57.910 --> 02:06:59.290
you know, you
probably know this,

02:06:59.290 --> 02:07:01.010
because you're from Haiti.

02:07:01.010 --> 02:07:02.560
But if you're in
a clinic in Haiti

02:07:02.560 --> 02:07:04.720
or a hospital, the
public hospitals, when

02:07:04.720 --> 02:07:06.250
the doctor prescribes
something, he

02:07:06.250 --> 02:07:11.740
passes he or she passes the
prescription to the patient

02:07:11.740 --> 02:07:13.130
or the patient's family member.

02:07:13.130 --> 02:07:15.880
And then you go out of the
hospital to the pharmacy

02:07:15.880 --> 02:07:18.309
outside, buy the medicines,
bring them back, give them

02:07:18.309 --> 02:07:18.850
to the nurse.

02:07:18.850 --> 02:07:20.980
I'm talking about gloves
and intravenous fluid,

02:07:20.980 --> 02:07:23.770
too, not just in medicines.

02:07:23.770 --> 02:07:28.711
So having Cubans come in and fit
into a fee for service system

02:07:28.711 --> 02:07:31.210
when they've been trained in a
system of socialized medicine

02:07:31.210 --> 02:07:34.240
where that's not done, you
know, is a major problem.

02:07:34.240 --> 02:07:37.960
And it's a very significant
weakness of the program.

02:07:37.960 --> 02:07:40.660
You know, the obvious partner
for the Cuban aid program

02:07:40.660 --> 02:07:41.330
is us.

02:07:41.330 --> 02:07:43.960
We're the ones, the neighbors
with all the money, you know.

02:07:43.960 --> 02:07:48.370
And in a sane world, of
course, if a group of countries

02:07:48.370 --> 02:07:50.980
were trying to come
together to help improve

02:07:50.980 --> 02:07:52.540
public health in
Haiti, it would be

02:07:52.540 --> 02:07:55.900
a wealthy country and a
country with enormous technical

02:07:55.900 --> 02:07:58.870
capacity and help, which
is Cuba, helping out

02:07:58.870 --> 02:08:00.590
their neighboring country.

02:08:00.590 --> 02:08:03.850
And, right now, there's
still no strategy.

02:08:03.850 --> 02:08:06.730
France, Canada, the other
countries that could do this

02:08:06.730 --> 02:08:09.510
have not come through yet
to be the third partner.

02:08:15.330 --> 02:08:17.290
AUDIENCE: How has
public health initiative

02:08:17.290 --> 02:08:20.464
included education and
prevention treatment in Haiti

02:08:20.464 --> 02:08:22.854
to prevent the spread
of infectious disease

02:08:22.854 --> 02:08:23.810
in particular?

02:08:23.810 --> 02:08:27.569
And how would that [INAUDIBLE]
what you do [INAUDIBLE]??

02:08:27.569 --> 02:08:29.110
FARMER: Did people
hear the question?

02:08:29.110 --> 02:08:32.110
How does prevention, especially
prevention of infectious

02:08:32.110 --> 02:08:33.340
[INAUDIBLE]--

02:08:33.340 --> 02:08:35.272
infectious Jesus did I say?

02:08:35.272 --> 02:08:36.480
[LAUGHTER]

02:08:36.480 --> 02:08:38.810
Infectious diseases,
that's my field.

02:08:38.810 --> 02:08:40.910
I'm supposed to
say that correctly.

02:08:40.910 --> 02:08:43.030
How does the prevention
figure in the plans?

02:08:43.030 --> 02:08:45.520
Well, one of the
big problems, again,

02:08:45.520 --> 02:08:48.684
is that if you say
to people, you know,

02:08:48.684 --> 02:08:51.990
kind of like in the United
States, don't do drugs,

02:08:51.990 --> 02:08:55.360
you know, don't get on welfare,
whatever-- but, you know,

02:08:55.360 --> 02:08:58.810
what happens too often in these
settings is you're exhorting

02:08:58.810 --> 02:09:00.820
people to do things
they cannot do.

02:09:00.820 --> 02:09:03.460
You know, for example,
don't drink dirty water.

02:09:03.460 --> 02:09:04.350
Well, you know--

02:09:04.350 --> 02:09:05.410
DORSINVILLE: There's
not other water.

02:09:05.410 --> 02:09:07.243
FARMER: That's great
in Cambridge, you know,

02:09:07.243 --> 02:09:10.480
where you try not to scoop water
out of a puddle to drink it

02:09:10.480 --> 02:09:13.630
when you're thirsty on
Mass Ave or you buy,

02:09:13.630 --> 02:09:15.940
for no good reason--
no offense Dinesh--

02:09:15.940 --> 02:09:16.930
Poland Springs water.

02:09:16.930 --> 02:09:17.770
Our water is clean.

02:09:17.770 --> 02:09:20.470
It has nothing to do
with individual volition,

02:09:20.470 --> 02:09:23.530
you know, or some kid at
Cambridge Rindge and Latin High

02:09:23.530 --> 02:09:25.960
School.

02:09:25.960 --> 02:09:28.101
As long as they drink
the public water,

02:09:28.101 --> 02:09:29.350
they're not going to get sick.

02:09:29.350 --> 02:09:31.510
So I actually have
limited enthusiasm

02:09:31.510 --> 02:09:35.950
for preventive measures
that rely exclusively

02:09:35.950 --> 02:09:38.987
on exhortation when, in
fact, the big problems are

02:09:38.987 --> 02:09:39.820
structural problems.

02:09:39.820 --> 02:09:43.870
So prevention of infectious
diseases like malaria,

02:09:43.870 --> 02:09:46.550
you know, is very difficult
to do without money,

02:09:46.550 --> 02:09:48.590
without cleaning
up stagnant water.

02:09:48.590 --> 02:09:51.850
And that's not the job
of individual families.

02:09:51.850 --> 02:09:53.210
You know, it's not the job.

02:09:53.210 --> 02:09:55.210
It's the job of a
public health system.

02:09:55.210 --> 02:09:57.010
You know, malaria, typhoid--

02:09:57.010 --> 02:09:59.360
you have to have clean water.

02:09:59.360 --> 02:10:01.810
I didn't have to develop
clean drinking water

02:10:01.810 --> 02:10:03.220
when I was in fifth grade.

02:10:03.220 --> 02:10:05.890
My parents didn't
make me do that.

02:10:05.890 --> 02:10:09.430
I actually never thought about
it until much later in life.

02:10:09.430 --> 02:10:11.710
And that's, again, same thing.

02:10:11.710 --> 02:10:14.396
HIV-- sure, we need
to push education.

02:10:14.396 --> 02:10:15.520
We need to push prevention.

02:10:15.520 --> 02:10:17.394
We need to have culturally
appropriate tools.

02:10:17.394 --> 02:10:19.330
We did all that in our work.

02:10:19.330 --> 02:10:21.940
But their impact
on that epidemic,

02:10:21.940 --> 02:10:24.580
if the major co-factors are
poverty and gender inequality,

02:10:24.580 --> 02:10:25.780
will be limited.

02:10:25.780 --> 02:10:29.320
So we need resources in order
to have a public infrastructure,

02:10:29.320 --> 02:10:31.450
and then prevention
and education

02:10:31.450 --> 02:10:33.040
will really have its impact.

02:10:33.040 --> 02:10:35.890
But, you know, with no
clean water and no access

02:10:35.890 --> 02:10:40.447
to vaccines, you know, exhorting
poor people to, you know,

02:10:40.447 --> 02:10:43.030
wash their hands and drink their
clean water-- not that you're

02:10:43.030 --> 02:10:43.530
saying that.

02:10:43.530 --> 02:10:46.779
I'm just saying
that's what's left

02:10:46.779 --> 02:10:48.820
over when you've really
destroyed a public health

02:10:48.820 --> 02:10:50.244
infrastructure.

02:10:50.244 --> 02:10:51.070
DORSINVILLE: Okay.

02:10:51.070 --> 02:10:52.630
AUDIENCE: Clearly,
there's no money in Haiti.

02:10:52.630 --> 02:10:54.100
But the fact remains
that Aristide

02:10:54.100 --> 02:10:56.950
has been in power for a year,
and it's not very evident

02:10:56.950 --> 02:10:59.180
what he's actually been doing.

02:10:59.180 --> 02:11:01.180
And I was wondering if
you could comment on that

02:11:01.180 --> 02:11:03.250
and, also, what some of
the legitimate criticisms

02:11:03.250 --> 02:11:07.441
are of the so-called opposition.

02:11:07.441 --> 02:11:09.190
FARMER: First of all,
I'm not so sure what

02:11:09.190 --> 02:11:10.720
that means to be in power.

02:11:10.720 --> 02:11:12.190
I mean, in other
places, that means

02:11:12.190 --> 02:11:16.871
having say over something, just
the general concept of power.

02:11:16.871 --> 02:11:19.120
And I've already told you
that the Brigham and Women's

02:11:19.120 --> 02:11:21.400
Hospital is larger than the
hospital for the Republic

02:11:21.400 --> 02:11:23.110
of Haiti for 8 million people.

02:11:23.110 --> 02:11:26.042
So I'm very skeptical
of analyses,

02:11:26.042 --> 02:11:27.250
not that yours is doing this.

02:11:27.250 --> 02:11:29.530
But I'm saying I'm skeptical
analysis that exaggerate

02:11:29.530 --> 02:11:33.100
the power and the agency
of people in these very

02:11:33.100 --> 02:11:34.410
marginalized situations.

02:11:34.410 --> 02:11:37.690
So I actually don't think that
the will of the Haitian people

02:11:37.690 --> 02:11:38.950
has ever been respected.

02:11:38.950 --> 02:11:42.766
They're trying to get a
government that represents

02:11:42.766 --> 02:11:44.140
broad aspirations
of power, but I

02:11:44.140 --> 02:11:45.460
don't think they've succeeded.

02:11:45.460 --> 02:11:48.940
They succeeded for a
few months back in 1991.

02:11:48.940 --> 02:11:50.950
And then you could
actually take a good look.

02:11:50.950 --> 02:11:52.270
You could just look at
the record and say, well,

02:11:52.270 --> 02:11:53.936
what happened during
those seven months?

02:11:53.936 --> 02:11:54.610
How did it work?

02:11:54.610 --> 02:11:57.070
But I'm not sure that's
ever happened since.

02:11:57.070 --> 02:11:59.290
And so I actually
don't agree that we

02:11:59.290 --> 02:12:01.690
can see a government in power.

02:12:01.690 --> 02:12:04.630
A government in power
doesn't have to pay interest

02:12:04.630 --> 02:12:06.310
on loans it's never received.

02:12:06.310 --> 02:12:08.500
A government in
power does not have

02:12:08.500 --> 02:12:13.780
to have its legal rights
approved by someone

02:12:13.780 --> 02:12:15.610
like Jesse Helms, you know?

02:12:15.610 --> 02:12:18.610
So I don't think
that that government

02:12:18.610 --> 02:12:20.470
has been in power yet.

02:12:20.470 --> 02:12:22.660
And the reasons that I
hope it comes into power

02:12:22.660 --> 02:12:25.394
are really related
only to the fact

02:12:25.394 --> 02:12:27.560
that the Haitian people
voted to have them in power.

02:12:27.560 --> 02:12:32.680
And I kind of think that
that's a legitimate aspiration

02:12:32.680 --> 02:12:34.990
for people, to want to
have a democratic regime.

02:12:34.990 --> 02:12:39.420
So that's my take on it as
someone who's working there.

02:12:39.420 --> 02:12:41.680
You know, when I inquire
over specific projects,

02:12:41.680 --> 02:12:44.080
as I did with the health
project, which is my job,

02:12:44.080 --> 02:12:47.087
I found that, again,
they're not in power.

02:12:47.087 --> 02:12:48.670
When I heard they
were paying interest

02:12:48.670 --> 02:12:50.086
on a loan they
haven't received, I

02:12:50.086 --> 02:12:54.990
thought it was a
canard, just a rumor.

02:12:54.990 --> 02:12:59.020
You were mentioning legitimate
criticism of the opposition?

02:12:59.020 --> 02:13:00.460
AUDIENCE: If there is any.

02:13:00.460 --> 02:13:01.550
FARMER: I think
they're all legitimate.

02:13:01.550 --> 02:13:02.350
I think they're all correct.

02:13:02.350 --> 02:13:03.160
That's my view.

02:13:03.160 --> 02:13:07.060
I think the opposition has
no broad-based support,

02:13:07.060 --> 02:13:10.330
does not represent the
aspirations of the majority,

02:13:10.330 --> 02:13:12.910
does not wish to come to power
through democratic elections.

02:13:12.910 --> 02:13:15.096
In fact, they said,
and many of them,

02:13:15.096 --> 02:13:16.720
the leaders of the
opposition movement,

02:13:16.720 --> 02:13:20.080
said we know we can't win
in democratic elections.

02:13:20.080 --> 02:13:21.550
That's what they've
said publicly.

02:13:21.550 --> 02:13:23.091
Again, this is in
The New York Times,

02:13:23.091 --> 02:13:25.720
that great paper of record.

02:13:25.720 --> 02:13:28.860
And so I find that
the criticisms

02:13:28.860 --> 02:13:31.990
I hear from the people with
whom I live in rural Haiti

02:13:31.990 --> 02:13:34.120
are to be very compelling.

02:13:34.120 --> 02:13:37.450
In fact, I haven't heard a
lot of uncompelling criticisms

02:13:37.450 --> 02:13:38.312
of the opposition.

02:13:38.312 --> 02:13:40.270
DORSINVILLE: Okay, we're
going to take two more

02:13:40.270 --> 02:13:45.170
questions-- this gentleman
here and then that lady there.

02:13:45.170 --> 02:13:47.855
AUDIENCE: My question
is for both of you.

02:13:47.855 --> 02:13:50.465
It sounds like both of
you have a great deal of--

02:13:50.465 --> 02:13:52.090
FARMER: For me and
my junior colleague?

02:13:52.090 --> 02:13:53.500
[LAUGHTER]

02:13:57.071 --> 02:13:58.570
AUDIENCE: It sounds
like both of you

02:13:58.570 --> 02:14:01.920
have a great deal of admiration
for the Haitian people,

02:14:01.920 --> 02:14:04.560
for their struggles
over the past 200 years.

02:14:04.560 --> 02:14:08.260
It also sounds like both of you
have learned a lot from them

02:14:08.260 --> 02:14:11.080
and they've motivated you.

02:14:11.080 --> 02:14:15.340
Given that, given their
struggles, and given sort

02:14:15.340 --> 02:14:18.400
of the level of critical
thought in the United States,

02:14:18.400 --> 02:14:21.760
do you really think that
true change in the long run

02:14:21.760 --> 02:14:25.450
is going to come
from internal changes

02:14:25.450 --> 02:14:28.935
within the United
States and [INAUDIBLE]??

02:14:28.935 --> 02:14:31.250
FARMER: Great question.

02:14:31.250 --> 02:14:35.860
CHOMSKY: Well, I think we
can pose it as a dilemma.

02:14:35.860 --> 02:14:39.130
Either it will come internal
to the population, the United

02:14:39.130 --> 02:14:41.380
States, or it won't come.

02:14:41.380 --> 02:14:42.690
OK.

02:14:42.690 --> 02:14:45.750
That's just the nature of power.

02:14:45.750 --> 02:14:48.630
And that's the same
on every other issue.

02:14:48.630 --> 02:14:50.280
You know, the United
States happens

02:14:50.280 --> 02:14:52.530
to have overwhelming power.

02:14:52.530 --> 02:14:54.840
These things are not
brought to the attention

02:14:54.840 --> 02:14:56.490
of the American population.

02:14:56.490 --> 02:14:58.710
How many people in
the United States

02:14:58.710 --> 02:15:02.820
would know anything about one
word of what was said tonight?

02:15:02.820 --> 02:15:05.830
It's all public record, but
nobody would know about it.

02:15:05.830 --> 02:15:07.600
And there's a reason for that.

02:15:07.600 --> 02:15:15.480
The reason is that political,
ideological, economic managers

02:15:15.480 --> 02:15:18.090
are terrified of the population.

02:15:18.090 --> 02:15:20.460
They don't want them to know.

02:15:20.460 --> 02:15:22.470
And they make efforts
to prevent them

02:15:22.470 --> 02:15:25.530
from knowing on all sorts of
issues, not just this one.

02:15:25.530 --> 02:15:27.160
You can run through a long list.

02:15:27.160 --> 02:15:29.220
And I think they're right.

02:15:29.220 --> 02:15:32.160
You know, my suspicion
is that people in power

02:15:32.160 --> 02:15:34.520
are probably correct
in their assessment,

02:15:34.520 --> 02:15:36.774
that if the population
does know what's

02:15:36.774 --> 02:15:38.190
being done in their
names, they're

02:15:38.190 --> 02:15:40.140
not going to let it happen.

02:15:40.140 --> 02:15:42.600
And we've seen
example after example

02:15:42.600 --> 02:15:46.560
of this when it was possible to
get around the centers of power

02:15:46.560 --> 02:15:48.460
through a popular organization.

02:15:48.460 --> 02:15:51.780
So, yes, I think their
judgment is probably accurate.

02:15:51.780 --> 02:15:54.030
It's extremely dangerous
to let the public know

02:15:54.030 --> 02:15:55.890
what's going on.

02:15:55.890 --> 02:15:59.470
And, therefore, you have
to marginalize people

02:15:59.470 --> 02:16:02.490
and, you know, direct
them to something,

02:16:02.490 --> 02:16:05.149
consumerism, or lie
to them, or whatever.

02:16:05.149 --> 02:16:07.440
Because if they find out,
they're not going to like it.

02:16:07.440 --> 02:16:09.650
Most people are
decent human beings.

02:16:09.650 --> 02:16:11.760
They're not going
to want to know.

02:16:11.760 --> 02:16:14.640
If they learn that they're
doing the things that Paul

02:16:14.640 --> 02:16:17.820
was talking about, they're
not going to let it happen.

02:16:17.820 --> 02:16:19.877
And that's our job, you know?

02:16:19.877 --> 02:16:20.960
And it's been done before.

02:16:20.960 --> 02:16:24.019
It can be done here.

02:16:24.019 --> 02:16:24.560
FARMER: Yeah.

02:16:24.560 --> 02:16:26.840
I don't have much to add
to that, because I agree.

02:16:26.840 --> 02:16:29.900
And, you know,
that's why I'm here

02:16:29.900 --> 02:16:36.080
tonight is because I admire
the Haitian people more

02:16:36.080 --> 02:16:37.770
than any other I've ever met.

02:16:37.770 --> 02:16:41.000
And I don't have long
experience in other settings,

02:16:41.000 --> 02:16:46.250
but I do have this really deep,
almost awe, of what they have

02:16:46.250 --> 02:16:48.559
done over the last
210 years and what

02:16:48.559 --> 02:16:49.882
they've resisted and survived.

02:16:49.882 --> 02:16:51.840
And, of course, many of
them have not survived.

02:16:51.840 --> 02:16:56.000
So I've also have a
deep sense of shame

02:16:56.000 --> 02:17:00.090
that so many people
have died along the way.

02:17:00.090 --> 02:17:01.700
And at the same time--

02:17:01.700 --> 02:17:04.356
this gets back--
she's gone already.

02:17:04.356 --> 02:17:05.689
This gets back to your question.

02:17:05.689 --> 02:17:07.280
Oh, she didn't like my answer.

02:17:07.280 --> 02:17:09.500
She split.

02:17:09.500 --> 02:17:12.360
This gets back to my
response to that question.

02:17:16.100 --> 02:17:18.559
I would regard as
romantic notions

02:17:18.559 --> 02:17:21.170
that this is going to be settled
quote unquote, "in Haiti,

02:17:21.170 --> 02:17:22.740
by Haitians."

02:17:22.740 --> 02:17:24.920
And I know you're
saying the same thing.

02:17:24.920 --> 02:17:26.120
That's your point.

02:17:26.120 --> 02:17:29.750
And if it were going to be
settled in Haiti, by Haitians,

02:17:29.750 --> 02:17:32.510
it's already been clear
on so many occasions

02:17:32.510 --> 02:17:38.870
that Haitians in Haiti are not
the primary architects of what

02:17:38.870 --> 02:17:40.610
we've described today.

02:17:40.610 --> 02:17:44.969
And so I go back
to places like--

02:17:44.969 --> 02:17:47.129
I've actually been to
some of these places,

02:17:47.129 --> 02:17:48.650
these powerful bureaucracies.

02:17:48.650 --> 02:17:51.500
You know, I think
that displays, I hope,

02:17:51.500 --> 02:17:56.904
a good deal of faith even
that something decent or less

02:17:56.904 --> 02:17:58.820
punitive could come out
of those institutions.

02:17:58.820 --> 02:18:00.680
But I actually
have a lot of faith

02:18:00.680 --> 02:18:03.860
in the decency of most
people and the belief

02:18:03.860 --> 02:18:07.400
that if you could get this
message out to more people,

02:18:07.400 --> 02:18:09.570
if you could have
it less hidden away

02:18:09.570 --> 02:18:12.379
and have it more the focus
of popular discussion

02:18:12.379 --> 02:18:14.949
by non-experts who are
not running the world,

02:18:14.949 --> 02:18:16.490
here in this country
I mean, it would

02:18:16.490 --> 02:18:19.850
be a wonderful thing for
poor people in general

02:18:19.850 --> 02:18:22.490
and including the poor people
living in poverty in Haiti who

02:18:22.490 --> 02:18:23.969
are the majority.

02:18:23.969 --> 02:18:26.320
And I know that's what
inspires him, too,

02:18:26.320 --> 02:18:30.080
to do work outside of
linguistics which I always

02:18:30.080 --> 02:18:33.110
found impenetrable anyway.

02:18:33.110 --> 02:18:36.080
But, you know, I mean,
that's why we're here.

02:18:38.922 --> 02:18:40.463
AUDIENCE: Hopefully,
you can hear me.

02:18:40.463 --> 02:18:44.830
[AUDIO OUT] [INAUDIBLE]
what I'm about to say,

02:18:44.830 --> 02:18:49.000
[INAUDIBLE] with my background.

02:18:49.000 --> 02:18:51.000
I'm from Malawi, which
is Southern Africa.

02:18:51.000 --> 02:18:52.459
And we just also--

02:18:52.459 --> 02:18:55.010
I mean, if you sit down and
listen to all these lectures,

02:18:55.010 --> 02:18:57.980
you can go to lecture on
Uganda, on Malawi, on Haiti,

02:18:57.980 --> 02:18:59.590
the story is the same.

02:18:59.590 --> 02:19:02.980
And to see that and,
you know, really

02:19:02.980 --> 02:19:05.540
romanticize that each
individual nation is

02:19:05.540 --> 02:19:09.969
going to solve their own
problems or the oppressor

02:19:09.969 --> 02:19:13.150
in power, the
opressee, to that level

02:19:13.150 --> 02:19:15.350
is really something I
find kind of far-fetched.

02:19:15.350 --> 02:19:18.799
Because Mother
Earth is not going

02:19:18.799 --> 02:19:21.559
to produce any more [INAUDIBLE].

02:19:21.559 --> 02:19:24.110
So my welfare in
the United States

02:19:24.110 --> 02:19:26.000
depends on you having less.

02:19:26.000 --> 02:19:27.350
And that's how it is.

02:19:27.350 --> 02:19:30.770
So, I mean, to what
extent are we really being

02:19:30.770 --> 02:19:34.370
realistic in expecting
that when we graduate

02:19:34.370 --> 02:19:37.400
from these institutions, we'll
go to work in the workplace,

02:19:37.400 --> 02:19:41.510
it will literally mean that, you
know, if I buy Pfizer's stock,

02:19:41.510 --> 02:19:42.559
I want it to do well.

02:19:42.559 --> 02:19:44.370
I don't care what
happens out there.

02:19:44.370 --> 02:19:46.280
So those are the
realistic aspects

02:19:46.280 --> 02:19:50.030
of what shapes my life and the
life of other Haitian people.

02:19:50.030 --> 02:19:53.720
So my call-- and I'm
asking this of both of you.

02:19:53.720 --> 02:19:56.630
If you look at it from
a global perspective,

02:19:56.630 --> 02:20:01.060
what is a role of oppressed
nations to get together

02:20:01.060 --> 02:20:03.050
and really--

02:20:03.050 --> 02:20:06.020
you know, because there's
a power in numbers--

02:20:06.020 --> 02:20:08.842
to wave some sort of
progressive [INAUDIBLE] notions

02:20:08.842 --> 02:20:13.022
that correct some
of these things?

02:20:13.022 --> 02:20:14.730
CHOMSKY: I mean, you're
absolutely right.

02:20:14.730 --> 02:20:17.730
There's what's called
South-South cooperation,

02:20:17.730 --> 02:20:21.010
you know, cooperative
interaction

02:20:21.010 --> 02:20:25.300
among the people who are the
most oppressed in the world

02:20:25.300 --> 02:20:26.920
and, by far, the most numerous.

02:20:26.920 --> 02:20:29.500
That can be very valuable.

02:20:29.500 --> 02:20:32.140
But to try to develop
it is not easy.

02:20:32.140 --> 02:20:35.386
For one thing, in every
one of these countries,

02:20:35.386 --> 02:20:36.760
including the ones
you mentioned,

02:20:36.760 --> 02:20:39.700
there are elite
groups who basically

02:20:39.700 --> 02:20:42.520
dominate them whose
tie is to the West,

02:20:42.520 --> 02:20:44.560
not to their local populations.

02:20:44.560 --> 02:20:47.140
They don't want
South-South cooperation

02:20:47.140 --> 02:20:51.349
in the interest of the
general population.

02:20:51.349 --> 02:20:53.140
I forgot-- I didn't
hear where you're from.

02:20:53.140 --> 02:20:53.780
FARMER: Malawi.

02:20:53.780 --> 02:20:54.696
CHOMSKY: Malawi, okay.

02:20:54.696 --> 02:20:56.470
So, you know, that's
the way it is there.

02:20:56.470 --> 02:20:58.330
And that's the way
it is everywhere.

02:20:58.330 --> 02:21:00.070
So South-South
cooperation is going

02:21:00.070 --> 02:21:02.590
to be people to
people cooperation.

02:21:02.590 --> 02:21:06.340
And at that point, it can also
become South-North cooperation.

02:21:06.340 --> 02:21:08.830
Actually, one of the important
things, really important,

02:21:08.830 --> 02:21:11.740
that's developed over
the past couple of years

02:21:11.740 --> 02:21:16.630
for the first time ever
is significant interaction

02:21:16.630 --> 02:21:23.860
among populations,
South and North,

02:21:23.860 --> 02:21:26.322
pursuing the same
goals and interests.

02:21:26.322 --> 02:21:28.030
So, for example, I
was in Brazil a couple

02:21:28.030 --> 02:21:30.730
of weeks ago at the
Porto Alegre meetings.

02:21:30.730 --> 02:21:34.596
There's around 70,000
people there, three times

02:21:34.596 --> 02:21:36.470
or four times what there
were the year before

02:21:36.470 --> 02:21:38.680
at the World Social Forum.

02:21:38.680 --> 02:21:41.020
And they're a fairly
good representation

02:21:41.020 --> 02:21:43.870
of substantial parts,
probably large majorities,

02:21:43.870 --> 02:21:47.020
of the population in many
countries of the world.

02:21:47.020 --> 02:21:51.010
Now, the countries don't have
the right kind of interaction.

02:21:51.010 --> 02:21:56.050
You know, like Brazil, and
India, or South Africa,

02:21:56.050 --> 02:21:58.060
three of the huge
South countries,

02:21:58.060 --> 02:21:59.490
they don't have contact.

02:21:59.490 --> 02:22:01.600
They don't even know
about each other.

02:22:01.600 --> 02:22:06.150
But at meetings like Porto
Alegre, they do come together--

02:22:06.150 --> 02:22:10.330
farmers, you know, workers,
environmental activists,

02:22:10.330 --> 02:22:13.486
women's groups, all sorts of
people from South and North.

02:22:13.486 --> 02:22:14.860
And they have the
same interests.

02:22:14.860 --> 02:22:16.484
You know, some are
rich, some are poor.

02:22:16.484 --> 02:22:19.240
But they basically have the
same interests as human beings

02:22:19.240 --> 02:22:21.670
and are working together
on common programs.

02:22:21.670 --> 02:22:23.870
That's extremely important.

02:22:23.870 --> 02:22:27.670
In fact, if there's going to
be any hope for the future,

02:22:27.670 --> 02:22:28.610
that's where it lies.

02:22:28.610 --> 02:22:30.943
And they're going to have to
change their own countries,

02:22:30.943 --> 02:22:33.169
South and North.

02:22:33.169 --> 02:22:33.710
FARMER: Yeah.

02:22:33.710 --> 02:22:35.376
I don't have much to
add to that, again,

02:22:35.376 --> 02:22:37.480
except that what I was
thinking as you are talking

02:22:37.480 --> 02:22:39.970
is that you mentioned
oppressed nations.

02:22:39.970 --> 02:22:44.710
And, you know, it's really
more oppressed people.

02:22:44.710 --> 02:22:47.650
Because the elites
of so many nations

02:22:47.650 --> 02:22:50.320
have really sided
against the poor,

02:22:50.320 --> 02:22:53.700
in many cases poor
majorities, and with the,

02:22:53.700 --> 02:22:55.930
you know, colonial
powers at one point

02:22:55.930 --> 02:22:57.620
or post-colonial,
neo-colonial powers.

02:22:57.620 --> 02:22:59.530
So, you know, Haiti,
unfortunately,

02:22:59.530 --> 02:23:05.830
is a very classic example
of a Latin American nation

02:23:05.830 --> 02:23:11.260
that within a small
amount of time really,

02:23:11.260 --> 02:23:17.020
its elite aligned itself so
wholly with the foreign powers

02:23:17.020 --> 02:23:22.720
that they were the biggest
sources of, you know,

02:23:22.720 --> 02:23:24.940
negative commentary
about the poor.

02:23:24.940 --> 02:23:27.190
You know, if you read the
literature from 19th century

02:23:27.190 --> 02:23:29.230
Haiti and early
20th century Haiti

02:23:29.230 --> 02:23:32.140
from the elites who, you
know, are writing in French,

02:23:32.140 --> 02:23:34.667
their views of the
Haitian people,

02:23:34.667 --> 02:23:36.250
the people who I
serve as a physician,

02:23:36.250 --> 02:23:43.090
are as bad and racist as some
of those from North America

02:23:43.090 --> 02:23:45.490
where they were
echoed, by the way.

02:23:45.490 --> 02:23:49.775
And so I don't have a lot of
experience working with elites.

02:23:49.775 --> 02:23:50.650
In fact, I have none.

02:23:50.650 --> 02:23:51.983
I would just confess right here.

02:23:51.983 --> 02:23:54.220
In Haiti, I have none.

02:23:54.220 --> 02:23:57.640
And the professionals
who I meet,

02:23:57.640 --> 02:24:01.030
especially physicians,
minister of health,

02:24:01.030 --> 02:24:03.160
are really pretty decent
people in my experience.

02:24:03.160 --> 02:24:05.830
And they're not so
likely to speak that way

02:24:05.830 --> 02:24:09.340
about the people who we
serve as the intellectuals

02:24:09.340 --> 02:24:11.497
that I read when I was
in graduate school,

02:24:11.497 --> 02:24:12.580
the Haitian intellectuals.

02:24:12.580 --> 02:24:18.280
So that process has actually
given me, I hope not,

02:24:18.280 --> 02:24:21.680
undue optimism that
there are people

02:24:21.680 --> 02:24:25.686
in all different layers
of these societies

02:24:25.686 --> 02:24:28.066
who want to see the
right thing done.

02:24:31.400 --> 02:24:33.200
DORSINVILLE: Okay.

02:24:33.200 --> 02:24:36.680
Well, I think our
time has come up.

02:24:36.680 --> 02:24:41.150
And I went to thank
Professor Chomsky and Dr.

02:24:41.150 --> 02:24:43.322
Farmer and particularly--

02:24:43.322 --> 02:24:45.298
[APPLAUSE]

02:25:11.040 --> 02:25:15.360
I also want to thank the Western
Hemisphere Project and all

02:25:15.360 --> 02:25:18.560
the other cosponsoring
MIT organizations

02:25:18.560 --> 02:25:21.600
and the person of
Dinesh to have worked

02:25:21.600 --> 02:25:24.360
very hard to make
this evening possible

02:25:24.360 --> 02:25:26.310
and bring a
historical perspective

02:25:26.310 --> 02:25:29.790
and a compelling approach to
the analysis of the Haitian

02:25:29.790 --> 02:25:30.390
situation.

02:25:30.390 --> 02:25:31.290
Thank you.

02:25:31.290 --> 02:25:34.040
[APPLAUSE]

